export const PROCESSES = [{"id":"troubleshooting-when-you-cant-join-a-zoom-meeting","title":" Troubleshooting When You Can’t Join a Zoom Meeting","category":"join","purpose":"Provide a standardized troubleshooting process for customers who are unable to join a Zoom meeting through a browser, meeting link, or the Zoom Workplace desktop app.","keywords":" Troubleshooting When You Can’t Join a Zoom Meeting join","steps":["Install the Zoom Workplace Desktop App","Uninstall and Reinstall Zoom","Enter the Meeting ID and Passcode Manually","Contact the Host if the Meeting ID Is Invalid"],"referral":"Refer when the issue requires host, account, admin, network, operating-system, or device access beyond basic Zoom guidance.","text":"Process Title: Troubleshooting When You Can’t Join a Zoom Meeting\nPurpose: Provide a standardized troubleshooting process for customers who are unable to join a Zoom meeting through a browser, meeting link, or the Zoom Workplace desktop app.\nApplies To: Zoom Workplace Desktop App and customers attempting to join a Zoom meeting through a meeting link or supported web browser.\nWhen To Use: When a customer cannot join a Zoom meeting, a meeting link does not work, the Zoom desktop app fails to join the meeting, or Zoom reports that the Meeting ID is invalid.\nIntroduction\nBeing unable to join a Zoom meeting can prevent a customer from accessing an important virtual session. This may occur due to an issue with the meeting link, Meeting ID, web browser, or Zoom Workplace desktop app. This process provides step-by-step guidance for identifying and resolving common joining issues so the customer can successfully access the meeting.\nProcess / Step-by-Step Guide\n1. Install the Zoom Workplace Desktop App\nIf the customer is having difficulty joining through a web browser, install the Zoom Workplace desktop app.\nAfter installation, open the Zoom Workplace app and try joining the meeting again.\nNote: Use this step when browser-based joining is unsuccessful or unreliable.\nZoom Download Center\nSample Script\n“Let’s first try joining through the Zoom Workplace desktop app. If you don’t have it installed yet, please download and install Zoom, then open the meeting link again or join through the app.”\n2. Uninstall and Reinstall Zoom\nIf Zoom is already installed but the customer still cannot join, completely uninstall Zoom.\nIf appropriate for the supported environment, use Zoom's CleanZoom utility to remove the existing Zoom installation.\nGo to the official Zoom Download Center.\nDownload and install the latest version of Zoom.\nOpen Zoom and try joining the meeting again.\nNote: Reinstalling Zoom can help when the existing installation is damaged or not functioning correctly. Follow company policy before running downloaded utilities such as CleanZoom.\nUninstall Zoom\nZoom Download Center\nCleanZoom utility\nSample Script\n“Since Zoom is already installed and you’re still unable to join, let’s reinstall the application. We’ll remove the current Zoom installation, download the latest version from Zoom’s official Download Center, and then try joining the meeting again.”\n3. Enter the Meeting ID and Passcode Manually\nSign in to the Zoom Workplace desktop app.\nOn the Home tab, click Join.\nEnter the Meeting ID provided by the host.\nClick Join.\nWhen the Enter meeting passcode window appears, enter the meeting passcode.\nContinue into the meeting if the Meeting ID and passcode are accepted.\nNote: Use this method when the meeting link does not work or joining directly through the link/app is unsuccessful.\nSample Script\n“Let’s try joining manually instead of using the meeting link. Please open Zoom and select Join. Enter the Meeting ID provided by the host, then click Join. When prompted, enter the meeting passcode exactly as provided.”\n4. Contact the Host if the Meeting ID Is Invalid\nConfirm that the Meeting ID was entered correctly.\nIf Zoom continues to report that the Meeting ID is invalid, contact the meeting host.\nAsk the host to confirm or resend the correct Meeting ID and meeting details.\nOnce the correct Meeting ID is received, enter it manually in the Zoom Workplace desktop app and try again.\nNote: An invalid Meeting ID generally requires confirmation from the meeting host. Do not guess or modify the Meeting ID.\nSample Script\n“Zoom is showing that the Meeting ID is invalid. Please contact the meeting host and ask them to confirm or resend the correct Meeting ID. Once you receive it, we can try joining the meeting again.”\nQuick Guide / Remember the Process\nINSTALL → REINSTALL → ENTER → CONFIRM\nINSTALL — the Zoom Workplace desktop app if browser joining is unsuccessful.\nREINSTALL — Zoom using the latest version if the installed app still cannot join the meeting.\nENTER — the Meeting ID and passcode manually instead of using the meeting link.\nCONFIRM — the Meeting ID with the host if Zoom reports that it is invalid.\nReference\nPrimary Reference: Zoom Support — Troubleshooting when you can’t join a Zoom meeting\nSupporting Zoom resources:\nUninstalling Zoom\nZoom Download Center\nCleanZoom utility","images":["assets/rendered/troubleshooting-when-you-cant-join-a-zoom-meeting-page-1.png","assets/rendered/troubleshooting-when-you-cant-join-a-zoom-meeting-page-2.png","assets/rendered/troubleshooting-when-you-cant-join-a-zoom-meeting-page-3.png"],"visualReferences":[]},{"id":"adjusting-the-volume-of-a-zoom-meeting","title":"Adjusting the Volume of a Zoom Meeting","category":"audio","purpose":"Provide a standardized process for helping customers adjust Zoom meeting volume before or during a meeting, including adjusting Zoom volume separately from the computer’s overall volume on Windows.","keywords":"Adjusting the Volume of a Zoom Meeting audio","steps":["Open Zoom Settings","Open Audio Settings","Test the Speaker","Adjust the Volume","Close Settings","Open Audio Options","Return to the Meeting","Open Volume Mixer"],"referral":"Refer when the issue requires host, account, admin, network, operating-system, or device access beyond basic Zoom guidance.","text":"Process Title: Adjusting the Volume of a Zoom Meeting\nPurpose: Provide a standardized process for helping customers adjust Zoom meeting volume before or during a meeting, including adjusting Zoom volume separately from the computer’s overall volume on Windows.\nApplies To: Zoom Workplace desktop app for Windows, macOS, and Linux that meets Zoom’s global minimum version. Mobile users must use the device’s physical or system volume controls.\nWhen To Use: When a customer says Zoom meeting audio is too loud or too quiet, wants to adjust the speaker volume before or during a meeting, or wants to change Zoom’s volume without changing the overall Windows device volume.\nRequirements\nZoom Workplace desktop app for Windows, macOS, or Linux that meets Zoom's global minimum version.\nFor mobile devices, Zoom volume must be adjusted using the mobile device's volume controls.\nZoom global minimum version requirements\nIntroduction\nZoom meeting volume may need to be adjusted when the audio is too loud or too quiet for the customer. On desktop devices, Zoom provides speaker volume controls that can be adjusted before or during a meeting, while Windows users can also adjust Zoom’s volume separately from the computer’s overall volume. Mobile users can control meeting volume using their device’s physical or system volume controls.\nAs a Zoom Support agent, understanding these options is important so you can identify the customer’s volume concern, determine which device they are using, and guide them to the appropriate volume control. This process provides step-by-step guidance for adjusting Zoom meeting volume and confirming that the customer can hear the meeting at a comfortable level before completing the interaction.\nProcess / Step-by-Step Guide\nA. Adjust Volume Outside of a Meeting\n1. Open Zoom Settings\nSign in to the Zoom Workplace desktop app. Click your profile picture in the top-right corner, then select Settings.\nSample Script\n“Let’s adjust your Zoom volume before the meeting. Please sign in to Zoom, click your profile picture in the top-right corner, and select Settings.”\n2. Open Audio Settings\nSelect the Audio tab.\nSample Script\n“In the Settings window, please select Audio. This is where we can check and adjust your Zoom speaker volume.”\n3. Test the Speaker\nUnder Speaker, optionally select Test Speaker to play a test tone.\nSample Script\n“Before changing the volume, you can click Test Speaker so we can hear the current audio level.”\n4. Adjust the Volume\nMove the Speaker volume slider until the sound reaches a comfortable level.\nSample Script\n“Please move the speaker volume slider up or down until the test sound is at a comfortable level for you.”\n5. Close Settings\nClick the X in the top-right corner of the Settings window when finished.\nSample Script\n“Once the volume sounds right, you can close the Settings window. Your Zoom speaker volume is now adjusted.”\nB. Adjust Volume During a Meeting\n1. Open Audio Options\nWhile in the Zoom meeting, click the upward arrow next to Mute/Unmute.\nSample Script\n“While you’re in the meeting, please click the small upward arrow next to Mute or Unmute.”\n2. Open Audio Settings\nSelect Audio Settings.\nSample Script\n“From that menu, please select Audio Settings.”\n3. Test the Speaker\nUnder Speaker, optionally click Test Speaker to play a test tone.\nSample Script\n“You can click Test Speaker first to check the current volume level.”\n4. Adjust the Volume\nMove the Speaker volume slider up or down as needed.\nSample Script\n“Now adjust the speaker volume slider until the meeting audio is at a comfortable level.”\n5. Return to the Meeting\nClose the Settings window to return to the meeting.\nSample Script\n“Once the volume is comfortable, close the Settings window and return to your meeting.”\nC. Windows: Adjust Zoom Volume Without Changing the Device's Overall Volume\nUse Windows Volume Mixer when the customer wants Zoom to be louder or quieter without changing the volume of other applications or the computer's overall volume.\n1. Open Volume Mixer\nRight-click the speaker icon in the lower-right corner of the Windows taskbar, then select Open volume mixer.\nSample Script\n“If you want to change only Zoom’s volume without changing the rest of your computer, please right-click the speaker icon on your Windows taskbar and select Open volume mixer.”\n2. Locate Zoom Meetings\nUnder Apps, locate Zoom Meetings.\nSample Script\n“Under Apps, look for Zoom Meetings. You may see more than one Zoom option.”\n3. Identify the Correct Zoom Option\nIf multiple Zoom entries appear, try adjusting each one to identify which controls the meeting audio.\nSample Script\n“If there are multiple Zoom options, we may need to try each one briefly to identify which controls the meeting audio.”\n4. Adjust Zoom Volume\nMove the Zoom Meetings volume slider up or down as needed. The Zoom meeting volume changes while the computer’s overall volume remains the same.\nSample Script\n“Once we identify the correct Zoom option, adjust its slider to your preferred level. This changes the Zoom meeting volume without changing your computer’s overall volume.”\nImportant Limitations and Reminders\nYou cannot adjust the volume of individual participants in a Zoom meeting.\nIf another participant sounds too loud, adjust the Zoom meeting volume you hear.\nIf another participant sounds too quiet, the issue may be on that participant's microphone. They can try a different microphone or follow Zoom audio troubleshooting.\nOn macOS and Linux, adjusting only Zoom's volume without changing overall device volume requires third-party software; Zoom does not provide this function directly.\nOn mobile devices, use the phone or tablet's volume controls.\nSample Script\n“Zoom doesn’t allow us to change the volume of one participant individually. If the meeting is too loud overall, we can lower the Zoom volume on your side. If only one person sounds too quiet, they may need to check or change their microphone.”\nQuick Guide / Remember the Process\nOPEN → AUDIO → TEST → ADJUST → CONFIRM\nOPEN — Zoom Settings or Audio Settings during the meeting.\nAUDIO — go to the Speaker section.\nTEST — the speaker when needed to hear the current level.\nADJUST — the speaker volume slider to the preferred level.\nCONFIRM — that the meeting audio is comfortable and return to the meeting.\nWindows only — separate Zoom volume: VOLUME MIXER → ZOOM MEETINGS → ADJUST\nReference\nPrimary Reference: Zoom Support — Adjusting the volume of a Zoom meeting\nSupporting Zoom resources:\nZoom audio settings\nZoom global minimum version\nTroubleshooting audio issues\nTroubleshooting speaker or microphone issues","images":["assets/rendered/adjusting-the-volume-of-a-zoom-meeting-page-1.png","assets/rendered/adjusting-the-volume-of-a-zoom-meeting-page-2.png","assets/rendered/adjusting-the-volume-of-a-zoom-meeting-page-3.png","assets/rendered/adjusting-the-volume-of-a-zoom-meeting-page-4.png","assets/rendered/adjusting-the-volume-of-a-zoom-meeting-page-5.png"],"visualReferences":[{"title":"Find the speaker test and volume controls","image":"assets/visual-references/audio-settings.png","documentStep":"Adjust Volume — Open Audio Settings, test the speaker, then adjust speaker volume","instruction":"In Zoom Settings, select Audio. Under Speaker, choose the correct output device, run Test Speaker, and adjust the output volume.","expectedResult":"The customer hears the test tone at a comfortable level before returning to the meeting.","platform":"Desktop — Zoom audio settings","sourceName":"The Royal Conservatory of Music","sourceUrl":"https://www.rcmusic.com/learning/examinations/remote-exams/help-with-remote-practical-exams/remote-examination-zoom-guide","status":"Real Zoom settings screen matched to process document"}]},{"id":"automatically-joining-meetings-with-computer-audio","title":"Automatically Joining Meetings with Computer Audio","category":"audio","purpose":"Provide a standardized process for helping customers automatically connect to Zoom meetings using their computer's speaker and microphone without being prompted to select an audio connection method each time.","keywords":"Automatically Joining Meetings with Computer Audio audio","steps":["Sign In to the Zoom Workplace Desktop App","Open Zoom Settings","Open Meetings & Webinars","Locate the Join Experience Settings","Enable Automatically Connect to Computer Audio","Confirm the Setting"],"referral":"Refer when the issue requires host, account, admin, network, operating-system, or device access beyond basic Zoom guidance.","text":"Process Title: Automatically Joining Meetings with Computer Audio\nPurpose: Provide a standardized process for helping customers automatically connect to Zoom meetings using their computer's speaker and microphone without being prompted to select an audio connection method each time.\nApplies To: Zoom Workplace desktop app for Windows, macOS, or Linux using Zoom's global minimum supported version or higher.\nWhen To Use: When a customer regularly uses the computer's speaker and microphone for Zoom and wants to automatically connect to computer audio when joining meetings or webinars.\nRequirements\nZoom account.\nZoom Workplace desktop app for Windows, macOS, or Linux.\nZoom global minimum supported version or higher.\nZoom global minimum version requirements\nIntroduction\nZoom can automatically connect customers to a meeting using their computer’s speaker and microphone, removing the need to choose an audio connection method each time they join. This setting is helpful for customers who regularly use the same computer audio setup for Zoom meetings and webinars.\nAs a Zoom Support agent, understanding this setting is important so you can identify when a customer wants to automatically connect to computer audio, guide them to the appropriate setting, and explain how enabling it changes their meeting join experience. This process provides step-by-step guidance for enabling automatic computer audio connection and confirming that the setting is configured correctly in the Zoom Workplace desktop app.\nProcess / Step-by-Step Guide\n1. Sign In to the Zoom Workplace Desktop App\nOpen the Zoom Workplace desktop app.\nSign in to the customer's Zoom account.\nSample Script\n“Please open the Zoom Workplace desktop app and sign in to your Zoom account.”\n2. Open Zoom Settings\nClick the profile picture in the top-right corner.\nSelect Settings.\nSample Script\n“Next, click your profile picture in the top-right corner, then select Settings.”\n3. Open Meetings & Webinars\nIn the Settings navigation menu, select Meetings & webinars.\nSample Script\n“In the Settings menu, please select Meetings & webinars.”\n4. Locate the Join Experience Settings\nUnder Join experience, locate the When joining meetings and webinars section.\nSample Script\n“Under Join experience, look for the section labeled ‘When joining meetings and webinars.’”\n5. Enable Automatically Connect to Computer Audio\nTurn on Automatically connect to computer audio.\nThe setting is saved automatically.\nSample Script\n“Please turn on ‘Automatically connect to computer audio.’ Once this is enabled, Zoom will automatically use your computer’s speaker and microphone when you join future meetings.”\n6. Confirm the Setting\nJoin a Zoom meeting to confirm that Zoom connects to computer audio automatically.\nThe customer should no longer receive the usual prompt asking how they want to join the meeting audio.\nSample Script\n“Let’s confirm the setting by joining a meeting. Zoom should now connect directly to your computer audio without asking you to choose an audio method first.”\nIf the Setting Is Not Available\nIf Automatically connect to computer audio does not appear or cannot be changed, the setting may be disabled or controlled by the customer's Zoom administrator.\nIf the customer needs a different audio method, such as telephone audio, review the available Zoom audio types or contact the appropriate Zoom account administrator when the setting is restricted.\nSample Script\n“If you don’t see this option or you’re unable to change it, the setting may be controlled by your Zoom administrator. If you need to join using another audio method, such as telephone audio, we can review the available audio options.”\nWhen to Disable This Setting\nDisable Automatically connect to computer audio if the customer does not always want to use the computer's speaker and microphone.\nThis may be appropriate when the customer sometimes needs to join using telephone audio or another supported audio type.\nAfter disabling the setting, Zoom can prompt the customer to choose an available audio connection method when joining.\nSample Script\n“If you sometimes prefer to join by telephone or use another audio method, you may want to leave this setting off. That way, Zoom can let you choose how you want to connect your audio when you join.”\nQuick Guide / Remember the Process\nSIGN IN → SETTINGS → MEETINGS → JOIN EXPERIENCE → ENABLE → TEST\nSIGN IN - to the Zoom Workplace desktop app.\nSETTINGS - open Zoom Settings from the profile picture.\nMEETINGS - select Meetings & webinars.\nJOIN EXPERIENCE - locate When joining meetings and webinars.\nENABLE - Automatically connect to computer audio.\nTEST - by joining a meeting and confirming that computer audio connects automatically.\nReference\nPrimary Reference: Zoom Support - Automatically joining meetings with computer audio\nSupporting Zoom resources:\nAudio types for joining Zoom meetings\nZoom global minimum version requirements","images":["assets/rendered/automatically-joining-meetings-with-computer-audio-page-1.png","assets/rendered/automatically-joining-meetings-with-computer-audio-page-2.png","assets/rendered/automatically-joining-meetings-with-computer-audio-page-3.png","assets/rendered/automatically-joining-meetings-with-computer-audio-page-4.png"],"visualReferences":[{"title":"Automatically connect to computer audio","image":"assets/visual-references/join-experience-settings.png","documentStep":"Join Experience — Enable Automatically connect to computer audio","instruction":"In Meetings & webinars, turn on Automatically connect to computer audio.","expectedResult":"Zoom uses the computer speaker and microphone automatically on future joins.","platform":"Desktop — current Zoom Workplace settings","sourceName":"Zapier","sourceUrl":"https://zapier.com/blog/zoom-tips/","status":"Recent real Zoom Workplace screen matched to process document"}]},{"id":"chatting-in-a-zoom-meeting","title":"Chatting in a Zoom Meeting","category":"controls","purpose":"Provide a standardized guide for using Zoom in-meeting chat, including public, private, and group chats; replies and reactions; formatting; screen-sharing chat; and host chat controls.","keywords":"Chatting in a Zoom Meeting controls","steps":["In the meeting controls toolbar, click Chat.","If viewing a private thread, click Main chat to return to the main meeting chat.","Press Enter or click Send.","Click Chat.","Click the New chat icon in the top-left of the chat panel.","Search for and select the participant.","Click Chat, then New chat.","Select each participant to include."],"referral":"Refer when the issue requires host, account, admin, network, operating-system, or device access beyond basic Zoom guidance.","text":"Process Title: Chatting in a Zoom Meeting\nPurpose: Provide a standardized guide for using Zoom in-meeting chat, including public, private, and group chats; replies and reactions; formatting; screen-sharing chat; and host chat controls.\nApplies To: Zoom Workplace desktop app for Windows, macOS, and Linux; Zoom mobile app for Android and iOS; and the Zoom Web App.\nWhen To Use: When a participant or host needs help sending or managing in-meeting chat messages, private/group chats, replies, reactions, formatting, or participant chat permissions.\nRequirements and Important Notes\nMeeting chat must be enabled.\nWindows | macOS | Linux: Zoom global minimum supported version or higher.\nAndroid | iOS: Zoom global minimum supported version or higher.\nZoom Web App is supported.\nFor in-meeting group chats, desktop and mobile participants must use Zoom Workplace version 6.2.10 or higher.\nAvailable chat functions can be limited by the host or account administrator.\nHosts cannot see private participant-to-participant chats or group chats they are not part of. Private participant messages are not included when the host saves the meeting chat transcript.\nIf Chat Etiquette is configured by the account administrator, a participant may be warned or blocked from sending a message that triggers the policy.\nProcess / Step-by-Step Guide\nA. Windows | macOS | Linux\nA1. Chat With Everyone\nIn the meeting controls toolbar, click Chat.\nIf viewing a private thread, click Main chat to return to the main meeting chat.\nEnter the message in the text compose box.\nOptional: use Format, Emoji, GIF, File, or Screenshot if available.\nPress Enter or click Send.\nSample Script\n“Please click Chat in the meeting controls. Make sure you’re in Main chat, type your message, add any available formatting or attachment if needed, then press Enter or click Send.”\nA2. Send a Private Chat\nClick Chat.\nClick the New chat icon in the top-left of the chat panel.\nSearch for and select the participant.\nEnter the message and optionally add formatting, emojis, GIFs, files, or screenshots.\nPress Enter or click Send.\nThe private conversation appears as a separate thread. Use Main chat to return to the meeting-wide chat.\nSample Script\n“To message one participant privately, click New chat, select the participant’s name, type your message, and send it. The private conversation will appear separately from Main chat.”\nA3. Create a Private Group Chat\nClick Chat, then New chat.\nSelect each participant to include.\nAll participants being added must use Zoom Workplace 6.2.10 or higher.\nEnter the message, customize it if needed, and send.\nThe group conversation appears separately from Main chat.\nSample Script\n“To start a group chat, click New chat and select each person you want to include. Everyone added must be on Zoom Workplace version 6.2.10 or higher.”\nA4. Reply to or React to a Message\nTo reply: click Chat, then click the Reply icon below the message, enter the reply, and send it.\nTo react: click the Add reaction icon below the message, select an emoji, or open More for the full emoji list.\nTo remove your reaction, click the emoji you previously added.\nSample Script\n“To reply directly to a message, click the Reply icon beneath it. To add a reaction, click the reaction icon and choose an emoji.”\nA5. Chat While Screen Sharing\nWhile sharing your screen, click Chat in the meeting controls toolbar.\nZoom opens a floating chat window that is not visible to other participants.\nA red indicator beside Chat shows unread messages.\nSample Script\n“While you’re sharing, click Chat to open the floating chat window. Other participants cannot see that chat window in your shared content.”\nA6. Format Chat Messages\nClick the Format icon in the chat window.\nAvailable rich-text options include Bold, Italic, Underline, Strikethrough, text/highlight Color, text Size, Link, Paragraph styles, Bulleted list, and Numbered list.\nUnder More, additional options include Increase/Decrease indent, Quote, and Clear style.\nSample Script\n“Click the Format icon to access rich-text options such as bold, italics, lists, links, colors, and more. Some options may be restricted by your administrator.”\nA7. Host: Manage Chat Settings\nJoin the meeting as host.\nClick Chat, then click More in the chat panel.\nUse Save chat when available to save chat messages to a TXT file.\nUnder Participant can chat with, choose No one, Host and co-hosts, Everyone, or Everyone and anyone directly.\nSample Script\n“As the host, open Chat and click More. From there, you can save the chat when available and choose who participants are allowed to message.”\nB. Android | iOS\nB1. Chat With Everyone\nTap Chat in the meeting controls toolbar.\nEnter the message in the Message everyone compose box.\nOptional: select text to display formatting controls.\nOptional: tap + to add a photo or file, or tap Emoji.\nTap Send.\nSample Script\n“Tap Chat, type your message in ‘Message everyone,’ add formatting, a file, photo, or emoji if needed, then tap Send.”\nB2. Send a Private Chat\nTap Chat.\nIn Meeting chat, tap the down arrow, then New chat.\nSearch for and select the participant, then tap OK.\nEnter the message; optionally format text, attach a photo/file, or add an emoji.\nTap Send.\nUse Main Chat to return to the meeting-wide conversation.\nSample Script\n“For a private message, tap the down arrow in Meeting chat, choose New chat, select the participant, tap OK, then type and send your message.”\nB3. Create a Private Group Chat\nTap Chat > down arrow > New chat.\nSelect each participant, then tap OK.\nParticipants added to the group must use Zoom Workplace 6.2.10 or higher.\nEnter and send the message; optional formatting, files/photos, and emojis can be used when available.\nUse Main Chat to return to the main meeting chat.\nSample Script\n“To create a group chat, open New chat and select multiple participants. Everyone added must be using Zoom Workplace version 6.2.10 or higher.”\nB4. Reply to or React to a Message\nTo reply: tap Chat, then tap the Reply icon below the message, enter the reply, customize if needed, and tap Send.\nTo react: tap the Reaction icon below the message and select an emoji; tap More for additional emojis.\nTap your previously added reaction to remove it.\nSample Script\n“Tap the Reply icon beneath a message to respond in its thread, or tap the reaction icon to add an emoji.”\nB5. Format Chat Messages\nEnter a message in the compose box.\nSelect the text you want to customize.\nUse the formatting toolbar for Bold, Italic, Underline, Strikethrough, Link, Bulleted/Numbered lists, and Increase/Decrease indentation.\nSample Script\n“To format text on mobile, type your message and select the text you want to change. The formatting toolbar will appear with the available options.”\nB6. Host: Manage Chat Settings\nTap More in the meeting controls toolbar, then Host tools.\nUnder Allow all participants to, tap Chat with.\nChoose No one, Host and co-hosts, Everyone, or Everyone and anyone directly.\nSample Script\n“As the host, tap More, then Host tools. Under ‘Allow all participants to,’ select ‘Chat with’ and choose who participants can message.”\nC. Zoom Web App\nC1. Chat With Everyone or Send a Private Message\nJoin the meeting from the Zoom Web App.\nClick Chat in the meeting controls toolbar.\nUse the to: drop-down menu to select Everyone in Meeting or an available private recipient.\nEnter the message.\nOptional: use Format, File, or Emoji if available.\nPress Enter or click Send.\nPrivate messages are identified as Direct Message.\nSample Script\n“Click Chat, choose the recipient from the ‘to:’ menu, type your message, and press Enter or click Send. A private message will be labeled as a Direct Message.”\nC2. Reply to or React to a Message\nTo reply: click Chat, hover over the message, click Reply, enter the response, and send.\nTo react: hover over the message, click Add reaction, then choose an emoji or click More for the full list.\nClick your previously added emoji to remove the reaction.\nSample Script\n“Hover over the message you want to respond to. Select Reply for a threaded response, or select the reaction icon to add an emoji.”\nC3. Chat While Screen Sharing\nWhile screen sharing, click Chat in the meeting controls toolbar.\nA floating chat window opens and is not visible to other meeting participants.\nA red indicator beside Chat shows unread messages.\nSample Script\n“While sharing your screen, click Chat to open the floating chat window. The chat window itself is not visible to other meeting participants.”\nC4. Format Chat Messages\nClick the Format icon.\nAvailable options include Bold, Italic, Underline, Strikethrough, Color, Size, Link, Paragraph styles, Bulleted/Numbered lists, and additional options under More such as indentation, Quote, and Clear style.\nSample Script\n“Click the Format icon to customize your message using the available rich-text options.”\nC5. Host: Manage Chat Settings\nJoin the meeting as host from the Zoom Web App.\nClick Chat.\nBelow the chat window, click the ellipses/More icon.\nUnder Participants Can Chat with, choose No one, Host and co-hosts, Everyone, or Everyone and anyone directly.\nSample Script\n“As the host, open Chat and click the ellipses below the chat window. Under ‘Participants Can Chat with,’ choose the level of chat access you want to allow.”\nUnderstanding the Host Chat Options\nIf Chat or an Option Is Missing\nConfirm that meeting chat is enabled.\nCheck whether the host has limited who participants can chat with.\nCheck whether the account administrator has disabled the feature or a specific chat option.\nFor group chats, confirm participants use Zoom Workplace version 6.2.10 or higher.\nRemember that formatting, GIFs, file sharing, screenshots, private chat, and other options may be unavailable depending on account or meeting settings.\nSample Script\n“If you don’t see the chat option or a specific feature, we’ll first confirm that chat is enabled and check whether the host or account administrator has restricted that function.”\nQuick Guide / Remember the Process\nDESKTOP: CHAT → MAIN / NEW CHAT → SELECT RECIPIENT → TYPE → CUSTOMIZE → SEND\nMOBILE: CHAT → MAIN / NEW CHAT → SELECT → OK → TYPE → SEND\nWEB APP: CHAT → TO: → SELECT RECIPIENT → TYPE → SEND\nHOST: CHAT / MORE → CHAT PERMISSIONS → CHOOSE ACCESS\nReference\nZoom Support — Chatting in a Zoom meeting\nZoom Support — Enabling or disabling meeting chat\nZoom Support — Chat Etiquette Tool\nZoom Support — Saving in-meeting chat\nZoom Support — Sending files in chat\nZoom Support — Zoom global minimum version","images":["assets/rendered/chatting-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-1.png","assets/rendered/chatting-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-2.png","assets/rendered/chatting-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-3.png","assets/rendered/chatting-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-4.png","assets/rendered/chatting-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-5.png","assets/rendered/chatting-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-6.png","assets/rendered/chatting-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-7.png"],"visualReferences":[{"title":"Locate in-meeting Chat","image":"assets/visual-references/workplace-controls.png","documentStep":"Meeting Chat — Select Chat from the bottom toolbar","instruction":"Click Chat, confirm the intended recipient, then type the message. Availability may depend on host settings.","expectedResult":"The meeting chat panel opens and the customer can verify who will receive the message.","platform":"Desktop — Zoom Workplace 6.x meeting toolbar","sourceName":"Stanford University IT","sourceUrl":"https://uit.stanford.edu/service/zoom/release","status":"Current Zoom Workplace interface matched to process document"}]},{"id":"enabling-and-managing-multiple-audio-input-channels-in-zoom","title":"Enabling and Managing Multiple Audio Input Channels in Zoom","category":"audio","purpose":"Provide a standardized process for helping customers select and manage specific audio input channels from a connected multi-channel audio interface in the Zoom desktop app.","keywords":"Enabling and Managing Multiple Audio Input Channels in Zoom audio","steps":["Sign In to the Zoom Desktop App","Open Zoom Settings","Open Audio Settings","Select the Connected Audio Interface","Confirm Specific Audio Input Channels Are Available","Open the Audio Input Channel Selection","Select the Channels to Use","Save the Channel Selection"],"referral":"Refer when the issue requires host, account, admin, network, operating-system, or device access beyond basic Zoom guidance.","text":"Process Title: Enabling and Managing Multiple Audio Input Channels in Zoom\nPurpose: Provide a standardized process for helping customers select and manage specific audio input channels from a connected multi-channel audio interface in the Zoom desktop app.\nApplies To: Zoom desktop app users with an input device or audio interface that Zoom detects as having three or more audio input channels.\nWhen To Use: When a customer uses a multi-channel microphone or audio interface and needs to choose which input channels Zoom should use during a live meeting or webinar.\nImportant Requirement\nThe connected input device must be detected by Zoom with three or more audio input channels.\nIf the Use specific audio input channels option does not appear, check the Zoom app version and confirm that the device has at least three audio channels connected.\nIntroduction\nZoom can work with multi-channel audio interfaces that provide multiple audio input channels, allowing customers to choose which channels are used during a meeting or webinar. This is useful when a connected microphone or audio interface has three or more input channels and only specific channels are needed. This process provides step-by-step guidance for selecting, managing, and saving the appropriate audio input channels in the Zoom desktop app.\nProcess / Step-by-Step Guide\n1. Sign In to the Zoom Desktop App\nOpen the Zoom desktop app.\nSign in to the customer's Zoom account.\nSample Script\n“Please open the Zoom desktop app and sign in to your account.”\n2. Open Zoom Settings\nClick the profile picture.\nSelect Settings.\nSample Script\n“Next, click your profile picture and select Settings.”\n3. Open Audio Settings\nIn the Settings menu, click Audio.\nSample Script\n“In the Settings menu, please select Audio.”\n4. Select the Connected Audio Interface\nLocate the Microphone section.\nOpen the microphone drop-down menu to view the available input devices.\nSelect the connected audio interface or multi-channel input device.\nSample Script\n“Under Microphone, open the drop-down menu and select the audio interface or input device you want Zoom to use.”\n5. Confirm Specific Audio Input Channels Are Available\nIf Zoom detects three or more channels on the selected microphone, Use specific audio input channels is automatically enabled.\nAn additional Audio input channels setting appears and shows how many channels are currently enabled.\nSample Script\n“If Zoom detects at least three input channels, the ‘Use specific audio input channels’ option should appear automatically. You should also see an Audio input channels setting showing how many channels are enabled.”\n6. Open the Audio Input Channel Selection\nNext to Audio input channels, click # Channel(s).\nA window opens showing all audio input channels detected by Zoom.\nSample Script\n“Next to Audio input channels, click the channel count. Zoom will display all of the input channels it detects from your device.”\n7. Select the Channels to Use\nSelect the checkbox next to one or more audio input channels that should be used by Zoom.\nConfirm that only the desired channels are selected.\nSample Script\n“Please select the checkbox beside each audio channel you want Zoom to use. You can choose one or multiple channels.”\n8. Save the Channel Selection\nClick Save to confirm the changes.\nThe selected channels will be used for audio during a live Zoom meeting or webinar.\nSample Script\n“Once the correct channels are selected, click Save. The changes will not take effect until you save them.”\n9. Test the Selected Channels\nJoin or return to a live meeting or webinar.\nConfirm that audio from the selected input channels can be heard as expected.\nSample Script\n“Now let’s test the setup in the meeting or webinar and confirm that Zoom is receiving audio from the channels you selected.”\nIf the Multiple-Channel Option Does Not Appear\nConfirm that the correct audio interface is selected under Microphone.\nConfirm that Zoom detects at least three audio input channels from the device.\nCheck the Zoom desktop app version.\nReconnect the audio interface if needed, then reopen the Audio settings and check again.\nSample Script\n“If you don’t see ‘Use specific audio input channels,’ let’s first confirm that the correct interface is selected and that Zoom detects at least three channels. We’ll also check the Zoom app version and reconnect the device if needed.”\nImportant Reminder\nChannel changes are not applied immediately when a checkbox is selected. The customer must click Save before the selected channels take effect in a live meeting or webinar.\nSample Script\n“Selecting a channel does not apply the change right away. Please make sure you click Save before testing the audio in your meeting or webinar.”\nQuick Guide / Remember the Process\nSETTINGS → AUDIO → SELECT DEVICE → CHANNELS → CHECK → SAVE → TEST\nSETTINGS — open Zoom Settings.\nAUDIO — select the Audio menu.\nSELECT DEVICE — choose the connected multi-channel audio interface under Microphone.\nCHANNELS — open Audio input channels.\nCHECK — select one or more channels to use.\nSAVE — confirm the selections so the changes take effect.\nTEST — the selected channels in a live meeting or webinar.\nReference\nPrimary Reference: Zoom Support — How to enable and manage multiple input channels\nKey source note: The Use specific audio input channels option appears when the selected input device is detected with three or more channels, and channel selections must be saved before they take effect.","images":["assets/rendered/enabling-and-managing-multiple-audio-input-channels-in-zoom-page-1.png","assets/rendered/enabling-and-managing-multiple-audio-input-channels-in-zoom-page-2.png","assets/rendered/enabling-and-managing-multiple-audio-input-channels-in-zoom-page-3.png","assets/rendered/enabling-and-managing-multiple-audio-input-channels-in-zoom-page-4.png"],"visualReferences":[]},{"id":"joining-a-zoom-meeting","title":"Joining a Zoom Meeting","category":"join","purpose":"To provide clear and standardized instructions for joining a Zoom meeting using the Zoom Workplace desktop app, mobile app, web browser, meeting link, or Meeting ID.","keywords":"Joining a Zoom Meeting join","steps":["· Check that your microphone, speakers/headset, and camera are working if required.","· Make sure Zoom has permission to access your microphone and camera.","“Please open the email or calendar invitation containing your Zoom meeting details.”","“Please click the Zoom meeting link in your invitation. This should open Zoom or prompt you to choose how you want to join.”","“Before we join, please check that your microphone, speaker, and camera are using the devices you want. You can also choose whether your camera is on or off before entering.”","“Please click Join. If you see a Waiting Room message, that means you’re connected successfully and are waiting for the host to admit you.”","“Please open the Zoom Workplace app and sign in to your account.”","“Please go to Home, Meetings, or Calendar and look for the meeting you want to join.”"],"referral":"Refer when the issue requires host, account, admin, network, operating-system, or device access beyond basic Zoom guidance.","text":"Process Title: Joining a Zoom Meeting\nPurpose: To provide clear and standardized instructions for joining a Zoom meeting using the Zoom Workplace desktop app, mobile app, web browser, meeting link, or Meeting ID.\nApplies To: Zoom Workplace Desktop App, Zoom Workplace Mobile App, and supported web browsers.\nWhen To Use: Use this process when joining a scheduled or active Zoom meeting, whether through an invitation link, the Zoom Workplace app, a web browser, or a Meeting ID. It can also be used when assisting someone who is unsure how to access a Zoom meeting.\nIntroduction\nJoining a Zoom meeting is a common first step when attending a virtual session. Zoom provides several ways to join, including through a meeting invitation link, the Zoom Workplace desktop or mobile app, a supported web browser, or by entering a Meeting ID. This process provides step-by-step guidance to help users access a Zoom meeting using the method available to them. \nProcess / Step-by-Step Guide\nBefore Joining a Meeting\n·       Ensure you have a stable internet connection.\n·     Install and update the Zoom Workplace app if you plan to use the desktop or mobile application.\n·       Have the meeting link or Meeting ID provided by the host.\n·       Check that your microphone, speakers/headset, and camera are working if required.\n·       Make sure Zoom has permission to access your microphone and camera.\nNote: For the best meeting experience, use the Zoom Workplace desktop or mobile app when possible.\nMethod 1: Join Using a Meeting Link\nThis is the simplest and most common way to join a Zoom meeting.\nSample Script\n“Please open the email or calendar invitation containing your Zoom meeting details.”\n“Please click the Zoom meeting link in your invitation. This should open Zoom or prompt you to choose how you want to join.”\n“If you have the Zoom Workplace app installed, please select the option to open Zoom. If you prefer to use your browser and that option is available, you can select Join from your browser.”\n“Before we join, please check that your microphone, speaker, and camera are using the devices you want. You can also choose whether your camera is on or off before entering.”\n“Please click Join. If you see a Waiting Room message, that means you’re connected successfully and are waiting for the host to admit you.”\nMethod 2: Join Through the Zoom Workplace App\nDesktop App\nSample Script\n“Please open the Zoom Workplace app and sign in to your account.”\n“Please go to Home, Meetings, or Calendar and look for the meeting you want to join.”\n“Select the scheduled meeting so we can open its details.”\n“Please click Join. If you’re the meeting host, select Start instead.”\nMobile App\nQuick Flow: Open Zoom Workplace → Sign In → Meetings/Calendar → Select Meeting → Join\nHost Reminder: If you are the meeting host, select Start instead of Join.\nMethod 3: Join From a Web Browser\nUse this option when the user does not want to download or install the Zoom application.\nImportant: The meeting host must allow browser joining for this option to be available. The web experience may also have fewer features than the full Zoom Workplace application.\nSample Script\n“Please open your meeting invitation and click the Zoom meeting link.”\n“If your browser asks you to open or download Zoom, please cancel that prompt so we can stay on the webpage.”\n“At the bottom of the page, please select Join from your browser.”\n“If your browser asks for microphone or camera permission, please select Allow so Zoom can use those devices.”\n“Please enter the name you would like other meeting participants to see.”\n“Now click Join. If you’re placed in the Waiting Room, please wait for the host to admit you.” \nMethod 4: Join Using a Meeting ID\nQuick Flow: Open Zoom's Join page → Enter the Meeting ID → Click Join → Open Zoom Workplace when prompted.\nUse this method when you have the Meeting ID but do not have a clickable meeting link.\nSample Script\n“Please open Zoom and select Join, or open the Zoom Join Meeting page in your browser.”\n“Please enter the Meeting ID exactly as it appears in your invitation.”\n“Click Join. If the meeting asks for a passcode, please enter the passcode provided in your invitation.” \n“Please check your audio and video preferences, then select Join to enter the meeting.”\nAlternative Ways to Join\nZoom Chat: If another Zoom user invites you to an active meeting, an incoming meeting notification appears. Select Join.\nAuto-call: When enabled and the user has opted in, Zoom can automatically call the participant when a scheduled meeting begins. Select Join from the incoming notification.\nTelephone: If phone dial-in is available, call the number in the invitation and enter the Meeting ID and passcode when prompted.\nSIP/H.323 or Zoom Room: These options are primarily used with supported conference-room and enterprise meeting systems.\nQuick Flow\nLOCATE  →  OPEN  →  CHECK  →  JOIN\nLOCATE — Find the meeting invitation or scheduled meeting.\nOPEN — Open the meeting through the link, Zoom app, browser, or Meeting ID.\nCHECK — Verify your microphone, speaker, and camera.\nJOIN — Enter the meeting or wait for the host to admit you.\nKey Reminders\n·       Verify audio and video settings before joining whenever possible.\n·       If joining through a browser, allow Zoom to access the microphone and camera.\n·       Being placed in a Waiting Room does not necessarily indicate a connection problem; wait for the host to admit you.\n·       Use the Zoom Workplace app as the primary joining method when possible.\nReference\nZoom Support — Joining a Zoom Meeting\n","images":["assets/rendered/joining-a-zoom-meeting-page-1.png","assets/rendered/joining-a-zoom-meeting-page-2.png","assets/rendered/joining-a-zoom-meeting-page-3.png","assets/rendered/joining-a-zoom-meeting-page-4.png","assets/rendered/joining-a-zoom-meeting-page-5.png","assets/rendered/joining-a-zoom-meeting-page-6.png"],"visualReferences":[{"title":"Join from the Zoom Workplace desktop app","image":"assets/visual-references/join-meeting.png","documentStep":"Joining a Zoom Meeting — Join without signing in","instruction":"Open Zoom Workplace and select “Join a meeting.” Then enter the Meeting ID or use the meeting link from the invitation.","expectedResult":"The customer reaches the meeting preview, waiting screen, or active meeting.","platform":"Windows desktop — Zoom Workplace 6.3.5 shown","sourceName":"PALCS HelpDesk","sourceUrl":"https://support.palcs.org/hc/en-us/articles/38306094116627-Zoom-sign-in-options-removal-blank-screen","status":"Matched to process document"}]},{"id":"locate-describe-guide-confirm","title":"Locate → Describe → Guide → Confirm","category":"support","purpose":"To provide agents with a consistent communication method for guiding arbitrators through Zoom setup, navigation, and basic troubleshooting. The process helps agents give clear, simple, step-by-step directions without assuming that the arbitrator is familiar with Zoom.","keywords":"Locate → Describe → Guide → Confirm support","steps":["Help the arbitrator identify the correct area of the Zoom interface before asking them to click or select anything.","If the control is not visible, do not repeatedly tell them to click it. Reassess the platform, window, meeting role, or available options.","“Please click Unmute once. After you click it, let me know what you see.”","Check whether the meeting toolbar is hidden or whether the option is under More/Reactions/Participants.","Allow the arbitrator time to locate and select the control."],"referral":"Refer when the issue requires host, account, admin, network, operating-system, or device access beyond basic Zoom guidance.","text":"Process Title: Using Locate → Describe → Guide → Confirm for Zoom Support\nPurpose: To provide agents with a consistent communication method for guiding arbitrators through Zoom setup, navigation, and basic troubleshooting. The process helps agents give clear, simple, step-by-step directions without assuming that the arbitrator is familiar with Zoom.\nApplies To: Agents assisting arbitrators with basic Zoom functions on the Zoom desktop app, Zoom Web App, and Zoom mobile app, including joining meetings, audio and video setup, meeting controls, Participants, Chat, reactions, and screen sharing.\nWhen To Use: Use this process whenever an agent needs to verbally guide an arbitrator to locate or use a Zoom control, complete a setup step, or perform approved basic troubleshooting. It is especially useful when the agent cannot see or control the arbitrator’s device and must rely on clear verbal instructions.\nIntroduction\nRemote Zoom support can be challenging because the agent may not be able to see exactly what the arbitrator sees. Controls may also appear in different locations depending on the device, operating system, Zoom version, meeting role, window size, and meeting settings. For this reason, agents should avoid giving several instructions at once or assuming that every Zoom interface looks identical.\nLocate → Describe → Guide → Confirm is a simple communication framework that keeps the interaction organized. The agent first helps the arbitrator find the correct area of Zoom, describes what the control should look like, guides the arbitrator through one action at a time, and then confirms the result before moving forward.\nCore principle: Do not move to the next step until the arbitrator confirms what they see or what happened.\nProcess / Step-by-Step Guide\nStep 1 — LOCATE\nHelp the arbitrator identify the correct area of the Zoom interface before asking them to click or select anything.\nIdentify the arbitrator’s device or platform when it affects the instructions.\nUse clear directional language such as bottom-left, bottom toolbar, top-right, or More.\nRefer to the visible label and icon when possible.\nAsk the arbitrator to confirm that they can see the control before continuing.\nIf the control is not visible, do not repeatedly tell them to click it. Reassess the platform, window, meeting role, or available options.\nSample Script\n“At the bottom of your Zoom meeting window, please look for the microphone icon labeled Mute or Unmute. Let me know when you see it.”\nStep 2 — DESCRIBE\nExplain what the control looks like and what it does so the arbitrator can distinguish it from nearby controls.\nUse the control’s Zoom label whenever possible.\nDescribe the icon in simple language.\nBriefly explain the function before asking the arbitrator to use it.\nAvoid technical terms unless they are necessary and explained.\nDo not overload the arbitrator with unrelated information.\nSample Script\n“The Mute control has a microphone icon. It controls whether the other participants can hear your microphone.”\nStep 3 — GUIDE\nGive the arbitrator the action needed to complete the task. Provide one instruction at a time and wait for confirmation.\nGive a single clear action.\nPause and allow the arbitrator time to complete it.\nAvoid combining several clicks or settings into one instruction.\nIf a menu opens, guide the arbitrator through the next visible choice.\nIf the arbitrator reports something different from what you expected, stop and reassess instead of continuing from memory.\nSample Script\n“Please click Unmute once. After you click it, let me know what you see.”\nStep 4 — CONFIRM\nVerify that the action produced the intended result before proceeding or closing the interaction.\nAsk the arbitrator what changed on the screen.\nWhen appropriate, test the function rather than relying only on the visual indicator.\nIf successful, acknowledge the result and continue to the next task.\nIf unsuccessful, return to Locate and reassess the issue.\nIf approved basic troubleshooting is exhausted or the issue is outside the agent’s support scope, follow the referral process.\nSample Script\n“Great. Let’s confirm that it worked. Please speak normally for a moment. Can the other participants hear you now?”\nComplete Example: Microphone\nAdditional Zoom Examples\nIf the Arbitrator Cannot Find the Control\nDo not assume the arbitrator is overlooking the control.\nConfirm whether they are using Windows, macOS, Linux, the Web App, Android, or iOS.\nAsk what controls or labels they can currently see.\nCheck whether the meeting toolbar is hidden or whether the option is under More/Reactions/Participants.\nConsider whether the feature is unavailable because of the arbitrator’s role or meeting settings.\nIf the control requires host permission or another action outside the agent’s scope, explain the support boundary and direct the arbitrator to the appropriate contact.\nSample Script\n“That’s okay. Zoom can look different depending on the device and meeting settings. Can you tell me whether you’re using a computer, web browser, phone, or tablet, and what options you currently see?”\nCommunication Standards\nUse simple, nontechnical language.\nGive one instruction at a time.\nAvoid saying “just click” or implying that a control should be obvious.\nUse labels and locations instead of vague directions such as “over there” or “that button.”\nAllow the arbitrator time to locate and select the control.\nRepeat or rephrase an instruction when necessary rather than speaking faster.\nDo not assume success; always confirm the result.\nMaintain a calm, professional, and neutral tone.\nDo not make unauthorized account, meeting, or proceeding decisions.\nCommon Mistakes to Avoid\nQuick Guide / Remember the Process\nLOCATE  →  DESCRIBE  →  GUIDE  →  CONFIRM\nAgent Self-Check\n☐ Did I identify the correct Zoom area or platform?\n☐ Did I clearly describe the control?\n☐ Did I provide only one instruction at a time?\n☐ Did I allow the arbitrator time to complete the step?\n☐ Did I ask what they saw when the interface differed?\n☐ Did I confirm that the action actually worked?\n☐ Did I remain within the approved basic support scope?\nReference\nUse this communication framework together with the applicable client-approved Zoom process documentation and current official Zoom Support instructions for the specific feature or troubleshooting procedure. If the issue reaches a roadblock outside basic support, follow the Zoom Basic Support Boundaries, Decision Path & Referral Process.","images":["assets/rendered/locate-describe-guide-confirm-page-1.png","assets/rendered/locate-describe-guide-confirm-page-2.png","assets/rendered/locate-describe-guide-confirm-page-3.png","assets/rendered/locate-describe-guide-confirm-page-4.png","assets/rendered/locate-describe-guide-confirm-page-5.png","assets/rendered/locate-describe-guide-confirm-page-6.png","assets/rendered/locate-describe-guide-confirm-page-7.png"],"visualReferences":[]},{"id":"muting-your-microphone-when-joining-a-zoom-meeting","title":"Muting Your Microphone When Joining a Zoom Meeting","category":"audio","purpose":"Provide a standardized process for helping customers automatically mute their microphone when joining Zoom meetings and optionally prevent audio or video from connecting before entering a specific meeting.","keywords":"Muting Your Microphone When Joining a Zoom Meeting audio","steps":["Sign In to Zoom","Open Settings","Open Meetings & Webinars","Enable Keep My Microphone Muted","Unmute When Ready to Speak","Sign In and Select Join","Select Don't Connect to Audio","Optional: Turn Off Video"],"referral":"Refer when the issue requires host, account, admin, network, operating-system, or device access beyond basic Zoom guidance.","text":"Process Title: Muting Your Microphone When Joining a Zoom Meeting\nPurpose: Provide a standardized process for helping customers automatically mute their microphone when joining Zoom meetings and optionally prevent audio or video from connecting before entering a specific meeting.\nApplies To: Zoom Workplace desktop app for Windows, macOS, and Linux version 5.0.0 or higher, and Zoom Workplace mobile app for Android and iOS version 5.0.0 or higher.\nWhen To Use: When a customer wants to enter Zoom meetings with the microphone muted by default, reduce background-noise disruptions, or choose not to connect to audio before joining a specific meeting.\nRequirements\nZoom Workplace desktop app: Windows, macOS, or Linux version 5.0.0 or higher.\nZoom Workplace mobile app: Android or iOS version 5.0.0 or higher.\nIntroduction\nMuting the microphone before joining a Zoom meeting can help prevent unwanted background noise and interruptions when entering a session. Zoom allows customers to keep their microphone muted by default when joining meetings or choose not to connect to audio or video for a specific meeting.\nAs a Zoom Support agent, understanding these options is important so you can identify what the customer wants to accomplish, explain the difference between muting the microphone and not connecting to audio, and guide them to the appropriate setting. This process provides step-by-step guidance to help customers manage their audio and video preferences and confirm that the selected settings work as expected.\nProcess / Step-by-Step Guide\nA. Automatically Mute the Microphone When Joining Meetings\n1. Sign In to Zoom\nOpen the Zoom Workplace desktop app and sign in.\nSample Script\n“Please open the Zoom Workplace app and sign in to your account.”\n2. Open Settings\nClick your profile picture, then select Settings.\nSample Script\n“Next, click your profile picture and select Settings.”\n3. Open Meetings & Webinars\nIn Settings, select Meetings & webinars.\nSample Script\n“In the Settings menu, please select Meetings & webinars.”\n4. Enable Keep My Microphone Muted\nUnder When joining meetings and webinars, turn on Keep my microphone muted.\nSample Script\n“Under ‘When joining meetings and webinars,’ please turn on ‘Keep my microphone muted.’ Once enabled, Zoom will automatically mute your microphone whenever you join a meeting.”\n5. Unmute When Ready to Speak\nAfter joining a meeting, manually select Unmute when you are ready to speak.\nSample Script\n“When you enter future meetings, your microphone will start muted. When you’re ready to speak, simply click or tap Unmute.”\nWhy this setting is useful: It can help prevent background noise and meeting disruptions, especially when joining from a loud environment or entering a meeting late.\nB. Disable Audio Before Joining a Specific Meeting\n1. Sign In and Select Join\nSign in to the Zoom Workplace desktop app and begin the process of joining the meeting.\nSample Script\n“If you only want to prevent audio from connecting for this meeting, please sign in to Zoom and select Join.”\n2. Select Don't Connect to Audio\nBefore entering the meeting, select the Don't connect to audio check box.\nSample Script\n“Before you enter the meeting, please select the ‘Don’t connect to audio’ check box. This will prevent Zoom from connecting your meeting audio when you join.”\n3. Optional: Turn Off Video\nIf desired, select the Turn off my video check box.\nSample Script\n“If you’d also like to enter with your camera off, select ‘Turn off my video.’ This step is optional.”\n4. Join the Meeting\nClick Join to enter the meeting with the selected audio and video preferences.\nSample Script\n“Now click Join. You’ll enter the meeting without connected audio, and with your video off as well if you selected that option.”\nImportant Distinction\nKeep my microphone muted: Zoom connects to meeting audio, but the customer's microphone starts muted. The customer can manually unmute when ready to speak.\nDon't connect to audio: Zoom does not connect the customer to meeting audio when entering that specific meeting. The customer must connect to audio later if they want to hear and speak through Zoom.\nSample Script\n“If you want to hear the meeting but simply enter without others hearing you, use ‘Keep my microphone muted.’ If you select ‘Don’t connect to audio,’ Zoom will not connect your meeting audio until you choose to connect it later.”\nQuick Guide / Remember the Process\nSETTINGS → MEETINGS → MUTE → JOIN → UNMUTE\nSETTINGS — open Zoom Settings.\nMEETINGS — select Meetings & webinars.\nMUTE — enable Keep my microphone muted.\nJOIN — future meetings with the microphone automatically muted.\nUNMUTE — manually when ready to speak.\nFor one meeting only: JOIN → DON'T CONNECT TO AUDIO → OPTIONAL VIDEO OFF → ENTER MEETING\nReference\nPrimary Reference: Zoom Support — Muting your microphone when joining a meeting\nSupporting Zoom resources:\nZoom audio settings\nJoining a Zoom meeting","images":["assets/rendered/muting-your-microphone-when-joining-a-zoom-meeting-page-1.png","assets/rendered/muting-your-microphone-when-joining-a-zoom-meeting-page-2.png","assets/rendered/muting-your-microphone-when-joining-a-zoom-meeting-page-3.png","assets/rendered/muting-your-microphone-when-joining-a-zoom-meeting-page-4.png"],"visualReferences":[{"title":"Keep the microphone muted when joining","image":"assets/visual-references/join-experience-settings.png","documentStep":"Mute on Join — Settings → Meetings & webinars → Join experience","instruction":"Turn on Keep my microphone muted under When joining meetings and webinars.","expectedResult":"Future meetings open with the customer’s microphone muted until they choose to unmute.","platform":"Desktop — current Zoom Workplace settings","sourceName":"Zapier","sourceUrl":"https://zapier.com/blog/zoom-tips/","status":"Recent real Zoom Workplace screen matched to process document"}]},{"id":"setting-your-video-to-stay-on-or-off-when-joining-meetings-and-webinars","title":"Setting Your Video to Stay On or Off When Joining Meetings and Webinars","category":"video","purpose":"Provide a standardized process for helping customers control whether their camera stays on or off by default when joining Zoom meetings and webinars, or change the video option for a specific meeting before joining.","keywords":"Setting Your Video to Stay On or Off When Joining Meetings and Webinars video","steps":["Click your profile picture, then select Settings.","Select Meetings & webinars.","On the Home tab, click Join.","Deselect the Turn off my video check box.","Click Join. Your camera will be on when you enter the meeting or webinar.","“If you only want your camera on for this meeting, click Join and enter the meeting details. Before joining, make sure ‘Turn off my video’ is not selected, then click Join.”","Tap your profile picture.","Tap Settings, then tap Meetings."],"referral":"Refer when the issue requires host, account, admin, network, operating-system, or device access beyond basic Zoom guidance.","text":"Process Title: Setting Your Video to Stay On or Off When Joining Meetings and Webinars\nPurpose: Provide a standardized process for helping customers control whether their camera stays on or off by default when joining Zoom meetings and webinars, or change the video option for a specific meeting before joining.\nApplies To: Zoom Workplace desktop app for Windows, macOS, and Linux, and Zoom Workplace mobile app for Android and iOS, using Zoom's current global minimum supported version or higher.\nWhen To Use: When a customer wants their camera to automatically stay on or off when joining meetings or webinars, or wants to change the video setting before joining a specific session.\nRequirements\nZoom Workplace desktop app for Windows, macOS, or Linux: Zoom global minimum version or higher.\nZoom Workplace mobile app for Android or iOS: Zoom global minimum version or higher.\nZoom global minimum version requirements\nIntroduction\nZoom allows customers to control whether their camera is on or off when joining a meeting or webinar. Customers can set a default video preference for future sessions or change the video option before joining a specific session.\nAs a Zoom Support agent, understanding these settings is important so you can identify the customer’s preferred video behavior, determine whether they want to change the default setting or only a specific meeting, and guide them to the appropriate video option. This process provides step-by-step guidance for managing these settings on the Zoom Workplace desktop and mobile apps and confirming that the customer’s video preference is set correctly.\nProcess / Step-by-Step Guide\nA. Desktop - Set the Camera to Stay On by Default\nSign in to the Zoom Workplace desktop app.\nClick your profile picture, then select Settings.\nSelect Meetings & webinars.\nUnder Join experience, locate Keep my camera off.\nTurn the Keep my camera off toggle OFF.\nYour camera will stay on when joining meetings or webinars unless you change the option before joining.\nSample Script\n“To have your camera stay on when you join meetings, please open Zoom Settings and select Meetings & webinars. Under Join experience, find ‘Keep my camera off’ and turn that setting off. Your camera will then be set to stay on when you join.”\nB. Desktop - Turn Video On for a Specific Meeting or Webinar\nSign in to the Zoom Workplace desktop app.\nOn the Home tab, click Join.\nEnter the Meeting ID or personal link name and enter your name.\nDeselect the Turn off my video check box.\nClick Join. Your camera will be on when you enter the meeting or webinar.\nSample Script\n“If you only want your camera on for this meeting, click Join and enter the meeting details. Before joining, make sure ‘Turn off my video’ is not selected, then click Join.”\nC. Mobile - Set the Camera to Stay On by Default\nSign in to the Zoom Workplace mobile app.\nTap your profile picture.\nTap Settings, then tap Meetings.\nUnder When joining meetings, locate Turn off my video.\nTurn the Turn off my video toggle OFF.\nYour camera will stay on when joining meetings or webinars unless you change the option before joining.\nSample Script\n“On your mobile device, open Zoom Settings and select Meetings. Under ‘When joining meetings,’ turn off ‘Turn off my video.’ This sets your camera to stay on when you join.”\nD. Mobile - Turn Video On for a Specific Meeting or Webinar\nSign in to the Zoom Workplace mobile app.\nTap Home, then tap Join.\nEnter the Meeting ID or personal link name and enter your name.\nUnder Join options, turn the Turn off my video toggle OFF.\nTap Join. Your camera will be on when you enter the meeting or webinar.\nSample Script\n“For this meeting only, tap Join and enter the meeting details. Under Join options, make sure ‘Turn off my video’ is turned off, then tap Join.”\nE. Desktop - Set the Camera to Stay Off by Default\nSign in to the Zoom Workplace desktop app.\nClick your profile picture, then select Settings.\nSelect Meetings & webinars.\nUnder Join experience, locate Keep my camera off.\nTurn the Keep my camera off toggle ON.\nYour camera will stay off when joining meetings or webinars until you manually start your video.\nSample Script\n“To have your camera stay off whenever you join, open Zoom Settings and select Meetings & webinars. Under Join experience, turn on ‘Keep my camera off.’ You can still manually start your video after entering the meeting.”\nF. Desktop - Turn Video Off for a Specific Meeting or Webinar\nSign in to the Zoom Workplace desktop app.\nOn the Home tab, click Join.\nEnter the Meeting ID or personal link name and enter your name.\nSelect the Turn off my video check box.\nClick Join. Your camera will be off when you enter the meeting or webinar.\nSample Script\n“If you only want your camera off for this meeting, click Join and enter the meeting details. Before joining, select ‘Turn off my video,’ then click Join.”\nG. Mobile - Set the Camera to Stay Off by Default\nSign in to the Zoom Workplace mobile app.\nTap your profile picture.\nTap Settings, then tap Meetings.\nUnder When joining meetings, locate Turn off my video.\nTurn the Turn off my video toggle ON.\nYour camera will stay off when joining meetings or webinars until you manually start your video.\nSample Script\n“On your mobile device, open Zoom Settings and select Meetings. Under ‘When joining meetings,’ turn on ‘Turn off my video.’ Your camera will then stay off when you join.”\nH. Mobile - Turn Video Off for a Specific Meeting or Webinar\nSign in to the Zoom Workplace mobile app.\nTap Home, then tap Join.\nEnter the Meeting ID or personal link name and enter your name.\nUnder Join options, turn the Turn off my video toggle ON.\nTap Join. Your camera will be off when you enter the meeting or webinar.\nSample Script\n“For this meeting only, tap Join and enter the meeting details. Under Join options, turn on ‘Turn off my video,’ then tap Join.”\nImportant Reminders\nDefault settings apply to future meetings and webinars until the customer changes the setting again.\nJoin options allow the customer to change the camera behavior for the meeting they are about to enter.\nIf the camera is set to stay off, the customer can manually enable video after joining.\nKeeping the camera off when joining can help reduce disruptions when entering a meeting late or before the session has started.\nSample Script\n“The default setting controls how your camera normally behaves when you join. You can still change the video option before a specific meeting, and you can turn your camera on or off after you enter.”\nQuick Guide / Remember the Process\nSETTINGS → MEETINGS → CAMERA → CHOOSE → JOIN\nSETTINGS - open Zoom Settings.\nMEETINGS - open Meetings & webinars on desktop or Meetings on mobile.\nCAMERA - locate Keep my camera off or Turn off my video.\nCHOOSE - turn the setting on to keep the camera off, or off to keep the camera on.\nJOIN - the meeting and manually change video if needed.\nFor one meeting only: JOIN → VIDEO OPTION → ON/OFF → ENTER\nReference\nPrimary Reference: Zoom Support - Setting your video to stay on or off when joining meetings and webinars\nSupporting Zoom resources:\nShowing and hiding your video in a meeting\nZoom audio and video settings\nZoom global minimum version requirements","images":["assets/rendered/setting-your-video-to-stay-on-or-off-when-joining-meetings-and-webinars-page-1.png","assets/rendered/setting-your-video-to-stay-on-or-off-when-joining-meetings-and-webinars-page-2.png","assets/rendered/setting-your-video-to-stay-on-or-off-when-joining-meetings-and-webinars-page-3.png","assets/rendered/setting-your-video-to-stay-on-or-off-when-joining-meetings-and-webinars-page-4.png","assets/rendered/setting-your-video-to-stay-on-or-off-when-joining-meetings-and-webinars-page-5.png"],"visualReferences":[{"title":"Choose the camera behavior before joining","image":"assets/visual-references/join-experience-settings.png","documentStep":"Joining Video Preference — Settings → Meetings & webinars → Join experience","instruction":"Use Keep my camera off to enter with video disabled. Use Show video preview first when the customer wants to check their appearance before joining.","expectedResult":"The next meeting follows the selected camera preference.","platform":"Desktop — current Zoom Workplace settings","sourceName":"Zapier","sourceUrl":"https://zapier.com/blog/zoom-tips/","status":"Recent real Zoom Workplace screen matched to process document"}]},{"id":"sharing-your-screen-desktop-or-content-in-zoom","title":"Sharing Your Screen, Desktop, or Content in Zoom","category":"sharing","purpose":"Provide a standardized process for sharing a screen, desktop, application, document, media, camera, audio, or other supported content during a Zoom meeting or webinar.","keywords":"Sharing Your Screen, Desktop, or Content in Zoom sharing","steps":["A1. Open Screen Share","In the meeting or webinar controls toolbar, click Share.","“Please click Share in the meeting controls. Zoom will open the window where you can choose what you want participants to see.”","A2. Choose What to Share","Select Screens, Documents, or Advanced. Some options may be unavailable if disabled by the account administrator.","Screens: share the entire screen or specific application windows. To select multiple apps, hold Ctrl on Windows or Shift on macOS.","A3. Choose Optional Presenter and Share Options","Optional: choose a Presenter layout when available for the selected content type."],"referral":"Refer when the issue requires host, account, admin, network, operating-system, or device access beyond basic Zoom guidance.","text":"Process Title: Sharing Your Screen, Desktop, or Content in Zoom\nPurpose: Provide a standardized process for sharing a screen, desktop, application, document, media, camera, audio, or other supported content during a Zoom meeting or webinar.\nApplies To: Zoom Workplace on Windows, macOS, Linux, Zoom Web App, Android, and iOS.\nWhen To Use: When a participant, host, co-host, or panelist needs help sharing supported content during a Zoom meeting or webinar, changing share options, sharing audio, or stopping a share.\nKey Information and Requirements\nScreen sharing must be enabled for the meeting.\nHosts cannot monitor a participant's screen activity unless that participant chooses to share the screen.\nBasic accounts use Only Host for screen sharing by default.\nIn webinars, only the host, co-hosts, and panelists can share.\nWindows, macOS, and Linux desktop apps must meet Zoom's global minimum supported version; Android/iOS mobile apps must also meet the global minimum version. Zoom Web App is supported.\nAndroid screen sharing requires Android 10.0 or higher.\nLinux sessions using Wayland can share only the entire desktop or Whiteboard. To share a specific application, use an Xorg session.\nFor larger meetings and webinars, Zoom recommends starting the event before beginning the screen share.\nExamples of Content You Can Share\nEntire desktop or mobile screen\nOne or more specific applications\nA portion of the screen\nContent from a second camera\nDocs and supported document repositories\nZoom Whiteboard\nLocally stored video\nComputer/device audio\niPhone/iPad screen\nProcess / Step-by-Step Guide\nA. Windows | macOS\nA1. Open Screen Share\nIn the meeting or webinar controls toolbar, click Share.\nThe screen-share selection window opens.\nSample Script\n“Please click Share in the meeting controls. Zoom will open the window where you can choose what you want participants to see.”\nA2. Choose What to Share\nSelect Screens, Documents, or Advanced. Some options may be unavailable if disabled by the account administrator.\nScreens: share the entire screen or specific application windows. To select multiple apps, hold Ctrl on Windows or Shift on macOS.\nDocuments: share Whiteboard, Zoom Docs, or supported third-party document repositories. Follow the sign-in/access prompts when using a third-party service.\nAdvanced: share a video file, Zoom Clip, second camera, computer audio only, iPhone/iPad via AirPlay or cable, or a selected portion of the screen.\nSample Script\n“Choose the tab that matches what you want to share: Screens for your desktop or apps, Documents for collaborative files, or Advanced for options such as video, a second camera, computer audio, or part of your screen.”\nA3. Choose Optional Presenter and Share Options\nOptional: choose a Presenter layout when available for the selected content type.\nOptional: enable Share sound if participants need to hear computer audio. Audio can be Mono or Stereo (high fidelity).\nOptional: enable Optimize for video sharing only when sharing a full-screen video clip. Using it for other content may make the share blurry.\nOptional for host/co-host: enable Share to breakout rooms to share from the main session into open breakout rooms.\nOptional: select Show Zoom windows if you need the Zoom app itself visible in the share.\nSample Script\n“Before sharing, we can turn on sound, choose a presenter layout, or optimize for a full-screen video if needed. Only use video optimization when you are actually sharing a video clip.”\nA4. Start Sharing\nClick Share.\nAfter the share is established, Zoom displays a banner indicating that participants can see the selected content.\nOptional: hover over You are screen sharing and use the drop-down arrow to preview what participants are receiving.\nSample Script\n“Click Share. Once Zoom confirms the share, participants can see the content you selected.”\nA5. Use the Floating Sharing Controls\nWhile sharing, Zoom moves meeting controls to a floating toolbar.\nAvailable controls can include Stop share, Audio, Video, Participants, Chat, Share, Pause/Resume, Layout, Annotate, Show/Hide meeting, and More.\nMore can include recording, transcript, breakout rooms, polls/quizzes, focus mode, annotation permissions, annotator names, hiding floating controls, Share computer sounds, and Optimize for video sharing. Availability depends on role and account settings.\nSample Script\n“While you’re sharing, use the floating toolbar for meeting controls. To temporarily freeze the share, choose Pause; to finish, choose Stop share.”\nA6. Stop Sharing\nClick Stop share in the floating toolbar.\nSample Script\n“When you’re finished, click Stop share. Participants will no longer see your shared content.”\nWindows | macOS - Additional Notes\nZoom may automatically switch to full screen while viewing shared content. Use Exit Full Screen or press Esc to exit.\nAutomatic full-screen behavior can be changed in desktop app settings under Window size when screen sharing.\nIf Second camera is selected, use Switch Camera to change the shared camera.\nAdvanced screen-sharing settings for hosts can control who may share, how many may share, and who can interrupt an existing share.\nDual-monitor mode can display shared content on one monitor and participants on the other.\nOn macOS 10.15 Catalina, Zoom requires Screen Recording permission in macOS privacy settings to share the screen.\nA7. Show Zoom Windows During Screen Share\nIn the Zoom web portal, open Settings > In Meeting (Basic) and enable Show Zoom windows during screen share.\nSign out and back in to the desktop app, or exit and reopen it.\nIn the desktop app, open profile picture > Settings > Share screen.\nUnder When I share, enable Show my Zoom Workplace Application and meeting windows.\nSample Script\n“If you need participants to see the Zoom window itself, we’ll first enable that option in the web portal, then turn on ‘Show my Zoom Workplace Application and meeting windows’ in the desktop Share screen settings.”\nB. Linux\nB1. Open Screen Share\nIn the meeting controls toolbar, click Share.\nSample Script\n“Please click Share in the meeting controls to open the Linux screen-sharing options.”\nB2. Select the Content\nBasic: share the entire desktop, a specific application window, or Whiteboard.\nAdvanced > Portion of Screen: share only the area inside the adjustable green border.\nAdvanced > Music or Computer Sound Only: share computer audio without sharing the screen; select Mono or Stereo (high fidelity) when available.\nWayland limitation: Wayland sessions can share only the entire desktop or Whiteboard. Use Xorg if a specific application must be shared.\nSample Script\n“Choose Basic for your desktop, application, or Whiteboard. Advanced includes a portion of the screen or computer audio only. If you’re using Wayland, a specific application cannot be shared, so use the full desktop or switch to Xorg.”\nB3. Configure Optional Share Settings\nOptional: Share Computer Sound.\nOptional: Optimize Screen Sharing for Video Clip only for full-screen video; otherwise it may blur the shared screen.\nOptional for host/co-host: Share to breakout rooms.\nSample Script\n“If participants need to hear your computer, enable Share Computer Sound. Only turn on video optimization when you’re sharing a full-screen video clip.”\nB4. Start Sharing\nClick Share.\nZoom displays a banner when participants can see the shared content.\nOptional: preview the shared content from You are screen sharing.\nSample Script\n“Click Share. When Zoom confirms the share, participants can see the content you selected.”\nB5. Use Linux Sharing Controls\nThe movable sharing controls can include Mute/Unmute, Start/Stop Video, Security, Participants, New Share, Pause Share, Annotate, Polling, and More.\nMore can include Chat, local/cloud recording, Meeting Info, annotation permissions, annotator names, video panel controls, Audio Settings, Video Settings, Share Computer Sound, Optimize Share for Full-screen Video Clip, and End Meeting.\nSample Script\n“During the share, the movable toolbar gives you access to meeting and sharing controls. Use New Share to change content, Pause Share to freeze the current view, or Stop the share when finished.”\nB6. Stop Sharing\nUse the sharing controls to stop the current screen share.\nSample Script\n“When you’re done presenting, stop the share so participants return to the normal meeting view.”\nLinux - Dual Monitors\nIf dual monitors are enabled, shared content can be displayed on one monitor while participants appear on the second.\nC. Zoom Web App\nC1. Open Screen Sharing\nIn the meeting controls toolbar, click Share Screen.\nSample Script\n“Please click Share Screen at the bottom of the Zoom Web App.”\nC2. Choose What to Share\nSelect a browser tab, application window, or entire desktop.\nThe selection interface varies by browser; follow the browser's on-screen prompts.\nSample Script\n“Choose whether you want to share a browser tab, an application window, or your entire desktop, then follow your browser’s prompts.”\nC3. Optional: Share Tab Audio\nAt the bottom of the share window, enable Also share tab audio if participants need to hear audio from the browser tab.\nThis option is currently available only in Chrome.\nSample Script\n“If you’re using Chrome and need participants to hear audio from the tab, turn on ‘Also share tab audio.’”\nC4. Start Sharing\nClick Share.\nZoom displays a banner when participants can see the shared content.\nOptional: preview the share using the control under You are screen sharing.\nSample Script\n“Click Share. Once Zoom confirms the share, participants can see the browser tab, window, or desktop you selected.”\nC5. Stop Sharing\nUse the meeting sharing controls to stop the share.\nSample Script\n“When you’re finished, click Stop Share to end the presentation.”\nZoom Web App - Troubleshooting Notes\nIf live changes to a shared application document do not appear for others, stop and restart the share or share the entire screen.\nOn Windows 10, the browser may show only a limited number of open applications. Close unnecessary apps and try again if the needed app is not listed.\nD. Android\nD1. Open Share\nIn the meeting controls toolbar, tap Share.\nSample Script\n“Please tap Share in the meeting controls.”\nD2. Choose the Content Type\nPhotos: select a photo from the file manager or photo app.\nDocuments: share a locally stored PDF or image.\nBox, Dropbox, Google Drive, Microsoft OneDrive, or OneDrive for Business: grant Zoom access and select a supported PDF or image.\nWebsite URL: enter a URL to open and share a website.\nBookmark: share a bookmark stored in Zoom. Bookmarks are not synced and are deleted when signing out.\nScreen: share the phone's entire screen.\nCamera: share the device camera or attached webcam.\nShare Whiteboard: open a shareable Whiteboard.\nSample Script\n“Choose the type of content you want to share. On Android, you can share items such as your screen, photos, documents, a website, cloud files, a camera, or Whiteboard.”\nD3. Follow the Prompts and Begin Sharing\nFollow the Android/Zoom prompts for the selected content.\nGrant any required permissions when prompted.\nSample Script\n“Please follow the on-screen prompts and allow the required permissions so Zoom can begin sharing the selected content.”\nD4. Optional: Share Device Audio\nBegin sharing the screen first.\nTap Share Audio to include supported device audio.\nTap Share Audio again to disable it, or tap Stop Share to end the share.\nSome apps block their audio from being captured for security or privacy reasons.\nSample Script\n“If you also need to share sound from the device, tap Share Audio after screen sharing begins. Some apps may prevent their audio from being shared.”\nD5. Stop Sharing\nTap Stop Share in the meeting controls toolbar.\nSample Script\n“When you’re finished, tap Stop Share.”\nE. iOS\nE1. Open Share\nIn the meeting controls toolbar, tap Share.\nSample Script\n“Please tap Share in the meeting controls.”\nE2. Choose the Content Type\nScreen (requires iOS 11 or higher).\nPhotos.\nShare Camera.\niCloud Drive.\nBox, Dropbox, Google Drive, Microsoft OneDrive, or OneDrive for Business. Grant Zoom access; only PDFs and images are supported for these services.\nWebsite URLs.\nBookmark. Bookmarks are not synced and are deleted when signing out.\nWhiteboard on iPad only.\nSample Script\n“Choose the content you want to share. On iOS, available choices can include your screen, photos, camera, iCloud Drive, supported cloud files, websites, bookmarks, and Whiteboard on iPad.”\nE3. Begin Sharing\nFollow the on-screen prompts to start the selected share.\nSample Script\n“Please follow the iOS prompts to start sharing the content you selected.”\nE4. Stop Sharing\nTap Stop Share in the meeting controls toolbar.\nSample Script\n“When you’re finished, tap Stop Share to end the screen share.”\niOS - Important Notes\nYou cannot annotate while sharing the entire iOS screen directly into the meeting. Use the separate iOS application-sharing method if full-screen annotation is required.\nWhile sharing your screen, you can hide your self-view by swiping the video preview toward the edge of the screen.\nIf the Share Option Is Missing or Unavailable\nConfirm that screen sharing is enabled for the meeting.\nAsk the host whether participant sharing is allowed. Basic accounts default to Only Host.\nIn a webinar, confirm that the user is a host, co-host, or panelist.\nConfirm that the app meets the supported minimum version and the device meets platform requirements.\nOn Linux Wayland, use full desktop/Whiteboard or switch to Xorg for application-only sharing.\nOn macOS, confirm required Screen Recording permission if the screen cannot be captured.\nSome share types or options may be disabled by the account administrator.\nSample Script\n“If Share is missing or unavailable, we’ll confirm that screen sharing is enabled, check your meeting role and host permissions, and then verify the platform and device requirements.”\nQuick Guide / Remember the Process\nWINDOWS | macOS: SHARE → SCREENS / DOCUMENTS / ADVANCED → OPTIONS → SHARE → STOP SHARE\nLINUX: SHARE → BASIC / ADVANCED → OPTIONS → SHARE → STOP\nWEB APP: SHARE SCREEN → TAB / WINDOW / DESKTOP → OPTIONAL AUDIO → SHARE → STOP\nANDROID: SHARE → CONTENT TYPE → PERMISSIONS → OPTIONAL SHARE AUDIO → STOP SHARE\niOS: SHARE → CONTENT TYPE → FOLLOW PROMPTS → STOP SHARE\nReference\nZoom Support — Sharing your screen, desktop, or other content during a Zoom meeting\nZoom Support — Screen sharing settings and participant sharing permissions\nZoom Support — Zoom global minimum version\nZoom Support — Sharing computer/device audio\nZoom Support — Sharing a locally stored video\nZoom Support — Zoom Whiteboard\nZoom Support — Sharing iPhone/iPad screens\nZoom Support — Advanced screen sharing settings\nZoom Support — Presenter layouts\nZoom Support — Annotation tools","images":["assets/rendered/sharing-your-screen-desktop-or-content-in-zoom-page-1.png","assets/rendered/sharing-your-screen-desktop-or-content-in-zoom-page-2.png","assets/rendered/sharing-your-screen-desktop-or-content-in-zoom-page-3.png","assets/rendered/sharing-your-screen-desktop-or-content-in-zoom-page-4.png","assets/rendered/sharing-your-screen-desktop-or-content-in-zoom-page-5.png","assets/rendered/sharing-your-screen-desktop-or-content-in-zoom-page-6.png","assets/rendered/sharing-your-screen-desktop-or-content-in-zoom-page-7.png","assets/rendered/sharing-your-screen-desktop-or-content-in-zoom-page-8.png","assets/rendered/sharing-your-screen-desktop-or-content-in-zoom-page-9.png"],"visualReferences":[{"title":"Choose what to share","image":"assets/visual-references/screen-share.png","documentStep":"Sharing Your Screen — Select a screen, window, or supported content","instruction":"Select the screen or application window. Enable Share sound when the shared content includes audio, then click Share.","expectedResult":"The selected screen or application is visible to meeting participants.","platform":"Windows desktop — Zoom Share Screen dialog shown","sourceName":"Community Bible Study Answers","sourceUrl":"https://answers.communitybiblestudy.org/knowledge-base/group-leaders-how-to-show-the-weekly-teaching-video-in-zoom/","status":"Matched to process document"}]},{"id":"showing-and-hiding-your-video-in-a-zoom-meeting","title":"Showing and Hiding Your Video in a Zoom Meeting","category":"video","purpose":"Provide a standardized process for helping customers turn their Zoom video on or off, hide or restore Self View, and identify the appropriate next steps when video is not displaying correctly.","keywords":"Showing and Hiding Your Video in a Zoom Meeting video","steps":["In the meeting controls toolbar, click Start Video.","“To turn your camera on, please click Start Video in the meeting controls. Once the slash disappears from the camera icon, your video is on and other participants can see you.”","In the meeting controls toolbar, click Stop Video.","“To turn your camera off, please click Stop Video. When you see the slash through the camera icon, your video is off and other participants can no longer see your camera feed.”","Click the ellipsis (…) or More icon on your video tile.","Select Hide Self View.","In the top-right corner of the Zoom video window, click View.","Select Show Self View."],"referral":"Refer when the issue requires host, account, admin, network, operating-system, or device access beyond basic Zoom guidance.","text":"Process Title: Showing and Hiding Your Video in a Zoom Meeting\nPurpose: Provide a standardized process for helping customers turn their Zoom video on or off, hide or restore Self View, and identify the appropriate next steps when video is not displaying correctly.\nApplies To: Zoom meeting participants using the Zoom Workplace app with access to in-meeting video controls.\nWhen To Use: When a customer wants to start or stop their camera, hide their own video tile without turning off the camera, restore Self View, or needs guidance when Zoom video is not displaying properly.\nIntroduction\nZoom provides different video controls that allow customers to manage how they appear during a meeting. Customers can start or stop their camera, hide their own video tile using Self View without turning off their camera, or restore Self View when needed.\nAs a Zoom Support agent, understanding the difference between these controls is important so you can identify what the customer wants to accomplish, determine whether they want to turn off their camera or simply hide their Self View, and guide them to the appropriate control. This process provides step-by-step guidance for managing these video options and identifying the appropriate next steps when the customer’s video is not displaying as expected.\nProcess / Step-by-Step Guide\nA. Turn Your Video On\nStart or join a Zoom meeting.\nIn the meeting controls toolbar, click Start Video.\nConfirm that the slash through the camera icon disappears. The customer's video is now visible to other meeting participants.\nSample Script\n“To turn your camera on, please click Start Video in the meeting controls. Once the slash disappears from the camera icon, your video is on and other participants can see you.”\nB. Turn Your Video Off\nIn the meeting controls toolbar, click Stop Video.\nConfirm that the camera icon displays a slash, indicating that the video is off.\nOther participants will no longer see the customer's camera feed.\nSample Script\n“To turn your camera off, please click Stop Video. When you see the slash through the camera icon, your video is off and other participants can no longer see your camera feed.”\nC. Hide Self View Without Turning Off the Camera\nStart or join a Zoom meeting with the camera turned on.\nHover over your own video tile.\nClick the ellipsis (…) or More icon on your video tile.\nSelect Hide Self View.\nConfirm that your own video tile disappears from your screen. Other participants can still see your video.\nImportant: Hide Self View does not turn off the camera. It only removes the customer's own video tile from their personal screen.\nSample Script\n“If you want to keep your camera on but stop seeing yourself on your own screen, hover over your video, click the three-dot More menu, and select Hide Self View. Other participants will still be able to see you.”\nD. Restore Self View\nIn the top-right corner of the Zoom video window, click View.\nSelect Show Self View.\nConfirm that the customer's own video tile appears again on their screen.\nSample Script\n“To see your own video again, click View in the top-right corner of the meeting window, then select Show Self View. Your video tile should appear on your screen again.”\nE. Troubleshoot Video Problems\nIf the video is turned on but does not display correctly, confirm that the correct camera and video settings are being used.\nTest the video before joining a meeting when possible.\nIf Zoom is crashing when video is enabled, follow Zoom's video-crash troubleshooting guidance.\nIf the video works but the quality is poor, review Zoom's video-quality settings and options such as HD video and low-light adjustment.\nUse the separate camera troubleshooting process when the camera is not detected or Zoom cannot access it.\nSample Script\n“If your video is turned on but still isn’t displaying correctly, we’ll check your camera and video settings next. We can also test your video to confirm that Zoom detects the camera. If Zoom crashes when video is enabled or the picture quality is poor, we’ll follow the appropriate video troubleshooting steps.”\nImportant Distinction\nStart Video / Stop Video controls whether other participants can see the customer's camera feed.\nHide Self View / Show Self View controls only whether the customer sees their own video tile on their screen.\nHiding Self View does not hide the customer's video from other meeting participants.\nSample Script\n“Turning your video off prevents other participants from seeing you. Hiding Self View is different—it only hides your own video from your screen while everyone else can still see your camera.”\nQuick Guide / Remember the Process\nSTART → STOP → HIDE → SHOW → TROUBLESHOOT\nSTART — select Start Video to make the camera feed visible to participants.\nSTOP — select Stop Video to turn the camera feed off.\nHIDE — select Hide Self View to remove only your own video tile from your screen.\nSHOW — select Show Self View to restore your own video tile.\nTROUBLESHOOT — camera or video settings if the video does not display correctly.\nReference\nPrimary Reference: Zoom Support — Showing and hiding your video in a meeting\nSupporting Zoom resources:\nAdjusting the Zoom video layout\nTroubleshooting Zoom video crashes\nImproving Zoom video quality\nTesting your video","images":["assets/rendered/showing-and-hiding-your-video-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-1.png","assets/rendered/showing-and-hiding-your-video-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-2.png","assets/rendered/showing-and-hiding-your-video-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-3.png"],"visualReferences":[{"title":"Locate the Video control","image":"assets/visual-references/workplace-controls.png","documentStep":"Show or Hide Video — Use Video at the bottom-left of the meeting toolbar","instruction":"Select Video to start or stop the camera. Use the small arrow beside it only when a different camera or video setting is needed.","expectedResult":"The button changes between Start Video and Stop Video, confirming the camera state.","platform":"Desktop — Zoom Workplace 6.x meeting toolbar","sourceName":"Stanford University IT","sourceUrl":"https://uit.stanford.edu/service/zoom/release","status":"Current Zoom Workplace interface matched to process document"}]},{"id":"testing-your-audio-settings-for-zoom-meetings","title":"Testing Your Audio Settings for Zoom Meetings","category":"audio","purpose":"Provide a standardized process for testing and adjusting Zoom speakers and microphones before or while joining a meeting, with separate procedures for desktop and mobile devices.","keywords":"Testing Your Audio Settings for Zoom Meetings audio","steps":["Open the Speaker and Microphone Test","Test the Speaker","Test the Microphone","Join Meeting Audio","Open Zoom Settings","Open the Audio Tab","Optional: Automatically Adjust Microphone Volume","Open the Zoom Test Meeting"],"referral":"Refer when the issue requires host, account, admin, network, operating-system, or device access beyond basic Zoom guidance.","text":"Process Title: Testing Your Audio Settings for Zoom Meetings\nPurpose: Provide a standardized process for testing and adjusting Zoom speakers and microphones before or while joining a meeting, with separate procedures for desktop and mobile devices.\nApplies To: Zoom desktop app for Windows, macOS, and Linux; Zoom mobile app for Android and iOS.\nWhen To Use: When a customer wants to verify that their speaker and microphone work before a meeting, needs to select a different audio device, or is having difficulty hearing or being heard.\nAudio Connection Options\nZoom meeting audio can be joined through computer/device audio or by telephone. A Zoom test meeting can also be used to test audio and become familiar with meeting controls.\nRequirements\nMicrophone: built-in microphone, USB microphone, or an inline microphone on headphones.\nSpeaker or headphones.\nZoom desktop app for Windows, macOS, or Linux: Zoom global minimum supported version or higher.\nZoom mobile app for Android or iOS: Zoom global minimum supported version or higher.\nIntroduction \nAs a Zoom support agent, you may assist customers who want to confirm that their audio is working properly before or during a meeting. This process will guide you through testing the customer’s speaker and microphone, selecting the appropriate audio device, and confirming their audio connection. Follow the applicable steps based on the customer’s device and confirm that they can hear and be heard before completing the interaction.\nProcess / Step-by-Step Guide\nA. Windows | macOS | Linux\nA1. Test Audio When Joining a Meeting\n1. Open the Speaker and Microphone Test\nWhile joining a meeting, click Test speaker and microphone.\nZoom opens a pop-up window and begins the speaker test.\nSample Script\n“Before we connect you to the meeting audio, please click ‘Test speaker and microphone.’ Zoom will first test your speaker.”\n2. Test the Speaker\nListen for the ringtone.\nIf you do not hear it, use the speaker drop-down menu or click No, Try Another Speaker.\nContinue switching speakers until the ringtone is audible, then click Yes.\nSample Script\n“Do you hear the ringtone? If not, select another speaker or click ‘No, Try Another Speaker’ until you hear it. Once you do, click Yes.”\n3. Test the Microphone\nSpeak into the microphone and listen for Zoom's audio replay.\nIf you do not hear the replay, use the microphone drop-down menu or click No, Try Another Microphone.\nWhen the replay is audible, click Yes.\nSample Script\n“Please speak into your microphone and listen for the replay. If you don’t hear your voice, choose another microphone or click ‘No, Try Another Microphone.’ When you hear the replay, click Yes.”\n4. End the Test\nClick End test to close the speaker and microphone test.\nSample Script\n“Both devices are working. Please click ‘End test’ to close the test window.”\n5. Join Meeting Audio\nIn the meeting controls toolbar, click Join audio.\nJoin using the selected speaker and microphone.\nSample Script\n“Now click ‘Join audio’ in the meeting controls so Zoom can use the speaker and microphone we just tested.”\nDuring a meeting: Click the arrow next to Mute/Unmute to choose a different speaker or microphone.\nA2. Test Audio Settings Before a Meeting\n1. Open Zoom Settings\nSign in to the Zoom desktop app.\nClick the profile picture in the top-right corner, then click Settings.\nSample Script\n“Please sign in to Zoom, click your profile picture, and open Settings.”\n2. Open the Audio Tab\nClick Audio.\nSample Script\n“In Settings, please select the Audio tab.”\n3. Test the Speaker\nUnder Speaker, click Test speaker to play a test tone.\nIf the tone is not audible, select another speaker from the drop-down menu or adjust Volume.\nSample Script\n“Under Speaker, click ‘Test speaker.’ If you can’t hear the tone, choose another speaker or adjust the volume until you can hear it clearly.”\n4. Test the Microphone\nUnder Microphone, click Test microphone.\nSpeak normally. The Input volume bar should move when Zoom detects audio.\nZoom records and plays the audio back.\nIf needed, choose another microphone or adjust the input level.\nSample Script\n“Now click ‘Test microphone’ and speak normally. You should see the Input volume bar move, and Zoom will play your recording back. If needed, we can select another microphone or adjust the input level.”\n5. Optional: Automatically Adjust Microphone Volume\nTurn on Automatically adjust microphone volume if the customer wants Zoom to manage microphone input volume automatically.\nSample Script\n“If you’d like Zoom to manage your microphone level automatically, turn on ‘Automatically adjust microphone volume.’”\nA3. Troubleshoot Microphone Access\nmacOS 10.14 Mojave or earlier: If Zoom cannot access the microphone, check the operating system permissions and confirm that Zoom has microphone access.\nWindows 10: Search for Microphone privacy settings. Enable Allow apps to access your microphone and enable microphone access for Zoom.\nSample Script\n“If Zoom still can’t access your microphone, we’ll check your computer’s microphone privacy permissions and make sure Zoom is allowed to use it.”\nB. Android | iOS\nB1. Join a Test Meeting to Test Audio Settings\n1. Open the Zoom Test Meeting\nUsing a mobile browser, go to http://zoom.us/test .\nSample Script\n“Please open your mobile browser and go to the Zoom test meeting page at zoom.us/test.”\n2. Launch the Zoom Mobile App\nLaunch the Zoom mobile app to join the test meeting.\nIf this is the first Zoom meeting on the device, allow Zoom access to the microphone when prompted.\nSample Script\n“Please launch the Zoom app to enter the test meeting. If Zoom asks for microphone permission, please allow it so the audio test can run.”\n3. Connect to Device Audio\nTap Wifi or Cellular Data.\nZoom automatically tests the device's speaker and microphone.\nSample Script\n“To connect your audio, tap ‘Wifi or Cellular Data.’ Zoom will automatically test your device’s speaker and microphone.”\n4. Exit the Test Meeting\nTap Done when the test is complete.\nSample Script\n“Once the audio test is complete, tap Done to exit the test meeting.”\nB2. Join With Device Audio in a Meeting\n1. Sign In and Join the Meeting\nSign in to the Zoom mobile app.\nStart or join a meeting.\nIf this is the first Zoom meeting on the device, allow microphone access when prompted.\nSample Script\n“Please sign in to the Zoom mobile app and start or join the meeting. If Zoom asks for microphone access, please allow it.”\n2. Choose an Audio Connection Method\nTap Wifi or Cellular Data to connect audio through the internet.\nTap Dial in to view telephone dial-in options.\nTap Call My Phone to have Zoom call the phone and connect it to the meeting. This option is unavailable if the host does not have an audio plan.\nSample Script\n“Please choose how you want to join the meeting audio. ‘Wifi or Cellular Data’ uses your internet connection, ‘Dial in’ provides telephone numbers, and ‘Call My Phone’ is available only when the meeting supports that option.”\n3. Mute or Unmute as Needed\nOptional: Tap the microphone icon in the bottom-left of the meeting controls toolbar to mute or unmute.\nSample Script\n“Once you’re connected, you can tap the microphone icon in the bottom-left corner to mute or unmute yourself.”\n4. Change the Audio Connection Method\nOptional: Tap More in the meeting controls toolbar.\nTap Disconnect Audio.\nReconnect using a different available audio method.\nSample Script\n“If you need to use a different audio method, tap More, then ‘Disconnect Audio.’ After disconnecting, you can choose another available way to join the meeting audio.”\nIf the Audio Test Is Unsuccessful\nConfirm that the correct speaker or headphones are connected and selected.\nConfirm that the correct microphone is connected and selected.\nCheck the device's microphone permissions for Zoom.\nOn desktop, use the arrow next to Mute/Unmute during the meeting to select a different speaker or microphone.\nIf the issue continues, follow the appropriate Zoom speaker or microphone troubleshooting process for desktop or mobile.\nSample Script\n“If the test still isn’t successful, we’ll confirm the selected speaker and microphone, check Zoom’s microphone permissions, and then continue with the appropriate audio troubleshooting steps.”\nQuick Guide / Remember the Process\nDESKTOP: TEST → LISTEN → SPEAK → SELECT → JOIN\nMOBILE: TEST MEETING → PERMISSION → WIFI/CELLULAR → VERIFY → DONE\nReference\nZoom Support — Testing your audio settings for Zoom meetings\nZoom Support — Joining a Zoom meeting\nZoom Support — Joining meeting audio by telephone\nZoom Support — Joining a Zoom test meeting\nZoom Support — Audio settings\nZoom Support — Troubleshooting speaker or microphone issues on the Zoom desktop app\nZoom Support — Troubleshooting speaker or microphone issues on your mobile device","images":["assets/rendered/testing-your-audio-settings-for-zoom-meetings-page-1.png","assets/rendered/testing-your-audio-settings-for-zoom-meetings-page-2.png","assets/rendered/testing-your-audio-settings-for-zoom-meetings-page-3.png","assets/rendered/testing-your-audio-settings-for-zoom-meetings-page-4.png","assets/rendered/testing-your-audio-settings-for-zoom-meetings-page-5.png","assets/rendered/testing-your-audio-settings-for-zoom-meetings-page-6.png"],"visualReferences":[{"title":"Test the selected speaker and microphone","image":"assets/visual-references/audio-settings.png","documentStep":"Testing Your Audio Settings — Desktop audio settings","instruction":"Open Settings, choose Audio, select the correct speaker and microphone, then use Test speaker and Test microphone.","expectedResult":"The customer hears the test tone and the microphone input meter responds.","platform":"Windows desktop — current Zoom Workplace settings shown","sourceName":"The Royal Conservatory of Music","sourceUrl":"https://www.rcmusic.com/learning/examinations/remote-exams/help-with-remote-practical-exams/remote-examination-zoom-guide","status":"Matched to process document"}]},{"id":"testing-your-video-in-zoom","title":"Testing Your Video in Zoom","category":"video","purpose":"Provide a standardized process for testing a camera before or during a Zoom meeting and confirming that Zoom is using the correct camera.","keywords":"Testing Your Video in Zoom video","steps":["Sign In to Zoom","Open Settings","Open Video & Effects","Confirm or Change the Camera","Adjust Video Settings if Needed","Start or Join a Meeting","Open the Video Menu","Open Video Settings"],"referral":"Refer when the issue requires host, account, admin, network, operating-system, or device access beyond basic Zoom guidance.","text":"Process Title: Testing Your Video in Zoom\nPurpose: Provide a standardized process for testing a camera before or during a Zoom meeting and confirming that Zoom is using the correct camera.\nApplies To: Zoom desktop app for Windows, macOS, and Linux; Zoom mobile app for Android and iOS.\nWhen To Use: When a customer wants to verify that their camera works before a meeting, cannot see their camera preview, needs to select another camera, or wants to review video effects and settings.\nRequirements\nZoom desktop app for Windows, macOS, or Linux: Zoom global minimum supported version or higher.\nZoom mobile app for Android or iOS: Zoom global minimum supported version or higher.\nA working built-in or external camera recognized by the device.\nA Zoom test meeting can also be used to become familiar with meeting controls and test both audio and video.\nIntroduction\nAs a Zoom support agent, you may assist customers who need to confirm that their camera is working properly before or during a meeting. Understanding this process is important because video issues can prevent customers from participating as expected and may be caused by something as simple as an incorrect camera selection or video setting. This process will guide you through testing the customer’s camera, checking the video preview, selecting the correct camera, and reviewing applicable video settings and effects. By understanding these steps, you can identify the customer’s concern, provide the appropriate guidance, and confirm that their video is working correctly before completing the interaction.\nProcess / Step-by-Step Guide\nA. Windows | macOS | Linux\nA1. Test Your Video Before a Meeting\n1. Sign In to Zoom\nOpen the Zoom desktop app and sign in.\nSample Script\n“Please open the Zoom desktop app and sign in to your account.”\n2. Open Settings\nClick your profile picture.\nClick Settings.\nSample Script\n“Next, click your profile picture in the top-right corner and select Settings.”\n3. Open Video & Effects\nClick the Video & effects tab.\nZoom displays a live preview from the currently selected camera.\nSample Script\n“In Settings, select ‘Video & effects.’ You should now see a live preview from the camera Zoom is currently using.”\n4. Confirm or Change the Camera\nReview the camera preview.\nIf another camera is available, select the desired camera.\nConfirm that the new camera displays correctly in the preview.\nSample Script\n“Please check the video preview. If this isn’t the camera you want to use, select another available camera and confirm that the preview appears correctly.”\n5. Adjust Video Settings if Needed\nUse the options below the preview to adjust camera and video settings.\nAvailable enhancements can include lighting adjustments, virtual backgrounds, filters, and other video effects.\nSample Script\n“If needed, you can also adjust your video appearance here, including lighting, backgrounds, filters, and other available effects.”\nA2. Test Your Video While in a Meeting\n1. Start or Join a Meeting\nStart a Zoom meeting or join an existing meeting.\nSample Script\n“Please start or join the Zoom meeting so we can check the camera from the meeting controls.”\n2. Open the Video Menu\nIn the meeting controls toolbar, locate the Video icon.\nClick the up arrow next to Video.\nSample Script\n“At the bottom of the meeting window, locate Video and click the small up arrow next to it.”\n3. Open Video Settings\nClick Video settings.\nZoom opens the video settings and displays the camera preview.\nSample Script\n“Please select ‘Video settings.’ This will open your camera settings and show the current video preview.”\n4. Select Another Camera if Needed\nIf the camera video does not appear, locate Camera.\nOpen the Camera drop-down menu.\nSelect another available camera and check the preview again.\nSample Script\n“If you don’t see your camera video, open the Camera drop-down menu and select another available camera. Let’s check whether the preview appears.”\n5. Adjust Video & Effects if Needed\nUse the available settings below the preview to adjust the camera and video.\nOptions can include lighting, virtual backgrounds, filters, and other enhancements.\nSample Script\n“Once the camera is working, you can make any needed adjustments to your lighting, background, filters, or other video settings.”\nB. Android | iOS\nB1. Test Your Video Using a Meeting\n1. Sign In to the Zoom Mobile App\nOpen the Zoom mobile app and sign in.\nSample Script\n“Please open the Zoom mobile app and sign in to your account.”\n2. Open the Meetings Tab\nTap the Meetings tab.\nSample Script\n“Next, tap the Meetings tab.”\n3. Start or Join a Meeting\nStart an instant meeting or join a meeting to test the camera.\nSample Script\n“Please start or join a meeting so we can test your camera.”\n4. Turn Video On\nTap the Video On toggle to enable the camera.\nIf this is the first Zoom meeting on the device, allow Zoom permission to access the camera and microphone when prompted.\nSample Script\n“Please turn on ‘Video On.’ If Zoom asks for camera or microphone permission, select Allow so the app can use those devices.”\n5. Confirm the Camera Preview\nConfirm that your video appears correctly in the meeting.\nIf video does not appear, verify that Zoom has camera permission on the device.\nSample Script\n“You should now see your video in the meeting. If it doesn’t appear, we’ll check that Zoom has permission to use your camera.”\n6. Open More\nIn the meeting controls toolbar, tap More.\nSample Script\n“To review additional video options, tap More in the meeting controls.”\n7. Open Background & Effects\nTap Background & effects.\nUse the available options to set a virtual background, create or select an avatar, or apply a video filter.\nSample Script\n“Tap ‘Background & effects.’ From here, you can choose an available virtual background, avatar, or video filter.”\nIf the Video Test Is Unsuccessful\nConfirm that the camera is physically connected and available to the device.\nOn desktop, open the Camera drop-down menu and try another detected camera.\nConfirm that Zoom has permission to access the camera.\nClose other applications that may be using the camera.\nIf the camera still does not display, continue with the separate Zoom camera troubleshooting process.\nSample Script\n“If the camera still isn’t displaying, we’ll confirm that the correct camera is selected, check Zoom’s camera permission, and make sure another application isn’t already using the camera.”\nImportant Platform Difference\nQuick Guide / Remember the Process\nDESKTOP: SETTINGS → VIDEO & EFFECTS → PREVIEW → SELECT CAMERA → ADJUST\nMOBILE: MEETINGS → JOIN/START → VIDEO ON → VERIFY → MORE → BACKGROUND & EFFECTS\nReference\nZoom Support — Testing your video\nZoom Support — Joining a Zoom test meeting\nZoom Support — Zoom global minimum version\nZoom Support — Video settings\nZoom Support — Enhancing your video\nZoom Support — Virtual backgrounds\nZoom Support — Avatars\nZoom Support — Video filters","images":["assets/rendered/testing-your-video-in-zoom-page-1.png","assets/rendered/testing-your-video-in-zoom-page-2.png","assets/rendered/testing-your-video-in-zoom-page-3.png","assets/rendered/testing-your-video-in-zoom-page-4.png","assets/rendered/testing-your-video-in-zoom-page-5.png","assets/rendered/testing-your-video-in-zoom-page-6.png"],"visualReferences":[{"title":"Select and verify the correct camera","image":"assets/visual-references/video-settings.png","documentStep":"Testing Your Video — Test before a meeting","instruction":"Open Settings, choose Video, and select the correct camera from the Camera menu. Confirm that the preview appears.","expectedResult":"The selected camera displays a clear live preview.","platform":"macOS desktop — Zoom settings shown","sourceName":"Prodigy EMS","sourceUrl":"https://marketing.prodigyems.com/blog-posts/how-to-maximize-your-zoom-recordings","status":"Matched to process document"}]},{"id":"transferring-meetings-and-webinars-between-devices","title":"Transferring Meetings and Webinars Between Devices","category":"devices","purpose":"Provide a standardized process for transferring an active Zoom meeting or webinar from one signed-in device to another without manually leaving and rejoining the session.","keywords":"Transferring Meetings and Webinars Between Devices devices","steps":["Sign In to the Zoom Web Portal","Open Account Settings","Enable or Disable Device Transfer","Start from the Current Desktop or Mobile Device","Open the Transfer Option","Select or Pair With the Zoom Room","Complete the Transfer","Start the Meeting on Desktop or Mobile"],"referral":"Refer when the issue requires host, account, admin, network, operating-system, or device access beyond basic Zoom guidance.","text":"Process Title: Transferring Meetings and Webinars Between Devices\nPurpose: Provide a standardized process for transferring an active Zoom meeting or webinar from one signed-in device to another without manually leaving and rejoining the session.\nApplies To: Zoom Workplace desktop app for Windows and macOS; Zoom mobile app for Android and iOS; Zoom Web App; and Zoom Rooms.\nWhen To Use: When a user needs to continue an active meeting or webinar on another device, such as when changing locations, conserving battery, moving from mobile to desktop, or transferring the session to a Zoom Room.\nIntroduction\nZoom's meeting and webinar transfer feature allows a participant to move an active session from one device to another while remaining signed in to the same Zoom account. \nThis is useful when a user needs to move from a computer to a phone, return to a desktop for additional controls, or continue the meeting in a Zoom Room. \nAfter the transfer is completed successfully, the device that was previously connected to the meeting is automatically disconnected. \nBecause some meeting information does not carry over, users should understand the transfer limitations before switching devices.\nRequirements\nMeeting and webinar transfer must be available/enabled for the account.\nWindows and macOS desktop apps must meet Zoom's global minimum supported version or higher.\nAndroid and iOS mobile apps must meet Zoom's global minimum supported version or higher.\nZoom Rooms app must meet Zoom's global minimum supported version or higher.\nZoom Web App is supported.\nThe user must be signed in to the same Zoom account on both devices.\nAccount owner or administrator privileges are required to enable or disable the account-level transfer setting.\nImportant Transfer Limitations\nCertain settings and meeting data do not transfer to the new device.\nExamples include sent chat messages, raised-hand status, an active local recording, closed captions, submitted Q&A, and the view of the full transcription.\nThis behavior is similar to leaving or disconnecting from a session and then rejoining it.\nSample Script\n“Before we switch devices, please note that some information will not move with you, such as your sent chat messages, raised-hand status, active local recording, captions, submitted Q&A, and the full transcript view.”\nEnable Meeting and Webinar Transfer - Account Administrators\n1. Sign In to the Zoom Web Portal\nSign in as an administrator with permission to edit account settings.\nSample Script\n“Please sign in to the Zoom web portal using an administrator account that can edit the account settings.”\n2. Open Account Settings\nIn the navigation menu, select Account Management, then Account Settings.\nSelect the Meeting tab.\nSample Script\n“Open Account Management, select Account Settings, and then open the Meeting tab.”\n3. Enable or Disable Device Transfer\nUnder In Meeting (Basic), locate Allow users to transfer meetings between devices.\nTurn the setting on or off as needed.\nSample Script\n“Under ‘In Meeting (Basic),’ locate ‘Allow users to transfer meetings between devices’ and enable the setting.”\nBasic User Note\nBasic users do not have access to the Account Settings page. According to the provided Zoom guidance, the transfer feature is enabled by default and accessible to Basic users.\nProcess / Step-by-Step Guide\nA. Windows | macOS\nA1. Sign In on the Device You Want to Switch To\nOpen the Zoom desktop app.\nSign in to the same Zoom account being used on the device currently connected to the meeting or webinar.\nSample Script\n“On the computer you want to use, please open Zoom and sign in to the same account that is currently connected to the meeting.”\nA2. Open the Home Tab\nClick Home to view upcoming meetings and webinars.\nLocate the session marked In progress.\nFor the active session, Switch replaces the usual Start or Join button.\nSample Script\n“Please open the Home tab and find the meeting marked ‘In progress.’ Instead of Start or Join, you should see a Switch button.”\nA3. Transfer the Session\nClick Switch.\nWait for the desktop app to join the active meeting or webinar.\nAfter the transfer succeeds, the previously connected device automatically disconnects.\nSample Script\n“Click Switch. Once this computer successfully joins the meeting, Zoom will automatically disconnect the other device.”\nB. Android | iOS\nB1. Sign In on the Mobile Device\nOpen the Zoom mobile app.\nSign in to the same Zoom account being used on the currently connected device.\nSample Script\n“Please open the Zoom mobile app and sign in to the same account that is currently connected to the meeting.”\nB2. Open Meetings\nTap the Meetings tab.\nReview the list of upcoming meetings and webinars.\nLocate the session marked In progress. Switch replaces Start or Join for that active session.\nSample Script\n“Tap Meetings and locate the session marked ‘In progress.’ You should see Switch instead of the normal Start or Join option.”\nB3. Transfer the Session\nTap Switch.\nWait for the mobile app to join the meeting or webinar.\nAfter the transfer succeeds, the device that was previously connected automatically disconnects.\nSample Script\n“Tap Switch. Once the meeting opens successfully on your phone or tablet, Zoom will automatically disconnect the previous device.”\nC. Zoom Web App\nC1. Sign In\nSign in to the Zoom web portal using the same Zoom account as the device currently connected to the session.\nSample Script\n“Please sign in to the Zoom web portal using the same account that is currently connected to the meeting.”\nC2. Open the Web App Home Tab\nIn the navigation menu, click Web app, then Home.\nReview upcoming meetings and webinars.\nLocate the session marked In progress. Switch replaces Start or Join.\nSample Script\n“Open Web app, then Home, and find the meeting marked ‘In progress.’ The Switch button should appear for that session.”\nC3. Transfer the Session\nClick Switch.\nWait for the Zoom Web App to join the active meeting or webinar.\nAfter the Web App connects successfully, the other device automatically disconnects.\nSample Script\n“Click Switch. Once the Zoom Web App joins successfully, Zoom will disconnect the device that was previously in the meeting.”\nD. Zoom Rooms\nD1. Transfer Using the In-Meeting Interface\n1. Start from the Current Desktop or Mobile Device\nSign in to the Zoom desktop or mobile app.\nStart or join the meeting.\nSample Script\n“On your current device, please make sure you’re signed in to Zoom and already in the meeting you want to transfer.”\n2. Open the Transfer Option\nClick or tap More.\nSelect Transfer meeting to room.\nSample Script\n“Open More in the meeting controls, then select ‘Transfer meeting to room.’”\n3. Select or Pair With the Zoom Room\nSelect the room you want to pair with, or enter the Sharing Key shown on the Zoom Room display.\nSample Script\n“Please select the Zoom Room you want to use. If it doesn’t appear, enter the Sharing Key displayed in the room.”\n4. Complete the Transfer\nClick or tap Continue.\nFollow any remaining on-screen prompts to complete the transfer.\nSample Script\n“Click Continue to transfer the active meeting to the selected Zoom Room.”\nD2. Transfer by Pairing the Devices\n1. Start the Meeting on Desktop or Mobile\nSign in to the Zoom desktop or mobile app.\nStart or join the meeting.\nSample Script\n“Please make sure you’re signed in and already connected to the meeting on your desktop or mobile device.”\n2. Pair With a Zoom Room\nOutside the meeting window, click or tap the Pair with a Zoom Room or Zoom for Home device icon.\nSelect the room and choose Pair, or enter the Sharing Key from the room display.\nSample Script\n“Outside the meeting window, select the Pair with a Zoom Room option. Choose the room or enter its Sharing Key, then pair the devices.”\n3. Select the Active Meeting\nClick or tap the Pair icon again.\nSelect Meeting in progress.\nSample Script\n“Once the devices are paired, select the Pair icon again and choose ‘Meeting in progress.’”\n4. Choose the Transfer Mode\nSelect Switch to room only to move the meeting to the Zoom Room.\nOr select Switch to room, connect as a companion to transfer to the room while keeping the current device connected as a companion.\nSample Script\n“Choose ‘Switch to room only’ if you want the room to take over the meeting, or ‘Switch to room, connect as a companion’ if you also want to keep this device connected as a companion.”\nIf the Switch Option Does Not Appear\nConfirm the meeting or webinar is currently active on the other device.\nConfirm both devices are signed in to the same Zoom account.\nConfirm the Zoom app meets the required global minimum supported version.\nConfirm that Allow users to transfer meetings between devices is enabled when the account is managed by an administrator.\nOn the destination device, confirm that the correct Home or Meetings view is being used and that the active session is listed as In progress.\nFor Zoom Rooms, confirm that the correct room is selected or that the Sharing Key was entered correctly.\nSample Script\n“If Switch isn’t appearing, we’ll confirm that both devices are signed in to the same Zoom account, that the meeting is still in progress, and that the transfer setting and app versions meet the requirements.”\nWhat Happens After a Successful Transfer\nThe destination device joins the active meeting or webinar.\nThe previously connected device automatically disconnects, except when a Zoom Room transfer option explicitly keeps the original device connected as a companion.\nMeeting information that does not transfer may need to be re-established on the new device.\nQuick Guide / Remember the Process\nWINDOWS | macOS: SIGN IN → HOME → IN PROGRESS → SWITCH → CONFIRM\nANDROID | iOS: SIGN IN → MEETINGS → IN PROGRESS → SWITCH → CONFIRM\nWEB APP: SIGN IN → WEB APP → HOME → IN PROGRESS → SWITCH\nZOOM ROOM: MORE → TRANSFER TO ROOM → SELECT ROOM / SHARING KEY → CONTINUE\nPAIRING METHOD: PAIR DEVICE → MEETING IN PROGRESS → CHOOSE TRANSFER MODE\nReference\nZoom Support — Transferring meetings and webinars between devices\nZoom Support — Zoom global minimum version\nZoom Support — Pairing a Zoom Room from the desktop app\nZoom Support — Connecting to a Zoom Room from the mobile app","images":["assets/rendered/transferring-meetings-and-webinars-between-devices-page-1.png","assets/rendered/transferring-meetings-and-webinars-between-devices-page-2.png","assets/rendered/transferring-meetings-and-webinars-between-devices-page-3.png","assets/rendered/transferring-meetings-and-webinars-between-devices-page-4.png","assets/rendered/transferring-meetings-and-webinars-between-devices-page-5.png","assets/rendered/transferring-meetings-and-webinars-between-devices-page-6.png","assets/rendered/transferring-meetings-and-webinars-between-devices-page-7.png"],"visualReferences":[{"title":"Find the active meeting and switch devices","image":"assets/visual-references/switch-device.png","documentStep":"Transfer Between Devices — Find the active meeting, then select Switch","instruction":"On the second signed-in device, locate the active meeting card and select Switch. Confirm the transfer prompt when shown.","expectedResult":"The meeting continues on the second device and disconnects from the original device according to Zoom’s transfer behavior.","platform":"Zoom Workplace app — device transfer view","sourceName":"UTokyo utelecon","sourceUrl":"https://utelecon.adm.u-tokyo.ac.jp/en/zoom/misc/app/","status":"Real Zoom interface matched to process document"}]},{"id":"troubleshooting-speaker-or-microphone-issues-in-the-zoom-desktop-app","title":"Troubleshooting Speaker or Microphone Issues in the Zoom Desktop App","category":"audio","purpose":"Provide a standardized process for resolving speaker and microphone issues when a customer cannot hear meeting audio or other participants cannot hear the customer.","keywords":"Troubleshooting Speaker or Microphone Issues in the Zoom Desktop App audio","steps":["Check the Audio Device","Check the Mute Status in Zoom","Check Zoom Audio Settings","Windows: Check Advanced Audio Settings","Check Microphone Privacy Permissions","Test the Speaker and Microphone Again","Quick Guide / Remember the Process"],"referral":"Refer when the issue requires host, account, admin, network, operating-system, or device access beyond basic Zoom guidance.","text":"Process Title: Troubleshooting Speaker or Microphone Issues in the Zoom Desktop App\nPurpose: Provide a standardized process for resolving speaker and microphone issues when a customer cannot hear meeting audio or other participants cannot hear the customer.\nApplies To: Zoom Workplace Desktop App on Windows and macOS. Privacy-setting steps vary by operating system.\nWhen To Use: When participants cannot hear the customer, the customer cannot hear sound during a Zoom call, Zoom does not detect the speaker or microphone, or the selected audio device is not working properly.\nIntroduction\nSpeaker or microphone issues can interrupt communication during a Zoom meeting by preventing the customer from hearing others or being heard. These issues may be related to the selected audio device, Zoom audio settings, device connections, or operating system permissions. This process provides step-by-step guidance for identifying and resolving common speaker and microphone issues in the Zoom Workplace desktop app on Windows and macOS.\nProcess / Step-by-Step Guide\n1. Check the Audio Device\nMake sure the microphone cable is fully inserted into the correct port.\nIf Zoom does not detect the microphone, unplug the device and plug it back in.\nFor Bluetooth devices, make sure the device is turned on and properly connected.\nIf using a USB audio device, confirm that it is a Zoom-supported USB device.\nMake sure the microphone is not muted in Zoom.\nIf the microphone or headset has a physical mute button or switch, make sure it is turned off.\nIncrease the computer or laptop volume and confirm that the device itself is not muted.\nNote: USB devices that are not listed as Zoom-supported may still work. However, Zoom recommends supported USB devices for the best experience and to reduce the likelihood of audio issues.\nZoom-supported USB devices\nSample Script\n“Let’s start by checking your audio device. Please make sure your microphone or headset is securely connected, turned on, and not muted. If it’s a Bluetooth device, confirm that it’s connected to the correct computer. If Zoom isn’t detecting it, please disconnect the device and reconnect it.”\n2. Check the Mute Status in Zoom\nLook at the Audio/Mute icon in the meeting controls.\nIf the microphone is muted, click the icon to unmute.\nIf the customer remains muted because the host muted them, ask the host to unmute them. The customer may send a chat message to the host.\nCheck for a physical mute button or switch on the microphone or headset.\nUnmuting in a Zoom meeting\nUsing in-meeting chat\nSample Script\n“Please check the microphone icon in your Zoom meeting controls. If you see that you’re muted, click it to unmute. If you can’t unmute yourself, the host may have restricted it, so please send the host a chat message asking to be unmuted. Also check your headset or microphone for a physical mute button.”\n3. Check Zoom Audio Settings\nSign in to the Zoom Workplace desktop app.\nClick the profile picture, then select Settings.\nSelect the Audio tab.\nUnder Speaker, test the speaker.\nSelect the correct speaker from the drop-down menu.\nAdjust the Output Volume slider as needed.\nUnder Microphone, test the microphone.\nSelect the correct microphone from the drop-down menu.\nAdjust the Input Volume slider as needed.\nNote: During a meeting, the microphone or speaker can also be changed by clicking the upward arrow next to Mute and selecting the preferred audio device.\nTesting Zoom audio settings\nSample Script\n“Next, let’s check Zoom’s audio settings. Please open Settings and select Audio. Under Speaker, choose the speaker you want to use and run the speaker test. Then, under Microphone, select your microphone and test it. We can also adjust the input and output volume if needed.”\n4. Windows: Check Advanced Audio Settings\nIf the issue continues on Windows, open Zoom's advanced audio settings.\nLocate Signal processing by Windows audio device drivers.\nTry disabling this option, then test the audio again.\nNote: This is a Windows-specific troubleshooting option and may not be necessary for every audio issue.\nZoom advanced audio settings\nSample Script\n“Since the issue is continuing on Windows, let’s check one additional Zoom audio setting. We’ll open the advanced audio settings and try disabling ‘Signal processing by Windows audio device drivers,’ then test your audio again.”\n5. Check Microphone Privacy Permissions\nIf the audio device does not appear in Zoom, confirm that Zoom has permission to access the microphone.\nFor Windows: Open Start → Settings → Privacy.\nUnder App permissions, select Microphone.\nEnable Allow apps to access your microphone.\nScroll down and enable Allow desktop apps to access the microphone.\nFor macOS, review the system privacy settings and grant Zoom permission to access the microphone.\nNote: Privacy menus and wording may vary depending on the operating system version.\nGranting Zoom permissions on macOS\nSample Script\n“If your microphone isn’t appearing in Zoom, your computer may be blocking access to it. Let’s check your privacy settings and make sure microphone access is enabled and Zoom is allowed to use it.”\n6. Test the Speaker and Microphone Again\nReturn to Zoom → Settings → Audio.\nTest the speaker and confirm that the test sound can be heard.\nTest the microphone and confirm that Zoom detects the customer's voice.\nReturn to the meeting and confirm that the customer can hear others and other participants can hear the customer.\nNote: Complete a final test after changing device, audio, or privacy settings to confirm the issue is resolved.\nSample Script\n“Now that we’ve updated the settings, let’s test your speaker and microphone again. Please confirm that you can hear the speaker test and that Zoom detects your voice when you test the microphone. Then we’ll return to the meeting and confirm that the audio is working.”\n6. Quick Guide / Remember the Process\nCHECK → UNMUTE → SELECT → TEST → ALLOW → RETEST\nCHECK — the physical microphone, speaker, headset, cable, Bluetooth connection, and device volume.\nUNMUTE — the microphone in Zoom and check any physical mute button on the audio device.\nSELECT — the correct speaker and microphone in Zoom's Audio Settings.\nTEST — the selected speaker and microphone and adjust input/output volume.\nALLOW — Zoom to access the microphone through the computer's privacy settings.\nRETEST — the audio after making changes and confirm both sides can hear each other.\n7. Reference\nPrimary Reference: Zoom Support — Troubleshooting speaker or microphone issues in the desktop app\nSupporting Zoom resources:\nZoom-supported USB devices\nUnmuting in a Zoom meeting\nTesting audio settings for Zoom meetings\nAdvanced audio settings\nGranting Zoom permissions on macOS\nZoom Workplace mobile app guidance (Android/iOS)","images":["assets/rendered/troubleshooting-speaker-or-microphone-issues-in-the-zoom-desktop-app-page-1.png","assets/rendered/troubleshooting-speaker-or-microphone-issues-in-the-zoom-desktop-app-page-2.png","assets/rendered/troubleshooting-speaker-or-microphone-issues-in-the-zoom-desktop-app-page-3.png","assets/rendered/troubleshooting-speaker-or-microphone-issues-in-the-zoom-desktop-app-page-4.png"],"visualReferences":[{"title":"Test the desktop speaker and microphone","image":"assets/visual-references/audio-settings.png","documentStep":"Desktop Audio Troubleshooting — Select devices and run Test Speaker / Test Mic","instruction":"Use the device dropdowns to choose the intended speaker and microphone, then test one device at a time.","expectedResult":"Zoom plays a test tone and records or detects the customer’s voice.","platform":"Desktop — Zoom audio settings","sourceName":"The Royal Conservatory of Music","sourceUrl":"https://www.rcmusic.com/learning/examinations/remote-exams/help-with-remote-practical-exams/remote-examination-zoom-guide","status":"Real Zoom settings screen matched to process document"}]},{"id":"troubleshooting-speaker-or-microphone-issues-on-a-mobile-device","title":"Troubleshooting Speaker or Microphone Issues on a Mobile Device","category":"audio","purpose":"Provide a standardized process for resolving speaker and microphone issues when using the Zoom Workplace mobile app.","keywords":"Troubleshooting Speaker or Microphone Issues on a Mobile Device audio","steps":["Check the Zoom Speaker","Check the Mobile Device Volume","Try Earphones or a Headset","Restart the Mobile Device","Reinstall Zoom","Check the Mute Status","Connect to Meeting Audio","Try Earphones with a Microphone"],"referral":"Refer when the issue requires host, account, admin, network, operating-system, or device access beyond basic Zoom guidance.","text":"Process Title: Troubleshooting Speaker or Microphone Issues on a Mobile Device\nPurpose: Provide a standardized process for resolving speaker and microphone issues when using the Zoom Workplace mobile app.\nApplies To: Zoom Workplace mobile app on iOS and Android devices.\nWhen To Use: When a customer cannot hear other participants, other participants cannot hear the customer, the microphone is muted or unavailable, or mobile audio is not connected after joining a Zoom meeting.\nRequirements\nZoom Workplace mobile app installed on an iOS or Android device.\nApple App Store — Zoom Workplace\nGoogle Play — Zoom Workplace\nIntroduction\nSpeaker or microphone issues on a mobile device can disrupt communication during a Zoom meeting by preventing the customer from hearing other participants or being heard clearly. These issues may be related to the microphone being muted, meeting audio not being connected, device permissions, or mobile audio settings. This process provides step-by-step guidance for identifying and resolving common speaker and microphone issues when using the Zoom Workplace mobile app on iOS and Android devices.\nProcess / Step-by-Step Guide\nIdentify whether the customer is experiencing a speaker issue, a microphone issue, or both. Follow the applicable process below.\nA. Troubleshooting Speaker Issues\n1. Check the Zoom Speaker\nLook at the speaker icon in the top-left corner of the meeting screen.\nIf the speaker is turned off, tap the icon to turn it on.\nSample Script\n“Let’s first check your Zoom speaker. Please look at the speaker icon in the top-left corner of your meeting screen. If it’s turned off, tap it to turn the speaker on.”\n2. Check the Mobile Device Volume\nUse the device's volume buttons or notification/control panel to increase the volume.\nConfirm that the mobile device is not set to mute or vibrate only.\nSample Script\n“Next, please increase the volume using your phone’s volume buttons. Also make sure the phone itself isn’t set to mute or vibrate only.”\n3. Try Earphones or a Headset\nConnect compatible earphones or a headset.\nCheck whether meeting audio can be heard through the connected device.\nSample Script\n“Let’s try using earphones or a headset to see whether you can hear the meeting audio through another audio device.”\n4. Restart the Mobile Device\nClose Zoom if needed.\nRestart the mobile device.\nOpen Zoom again, rejoin the meeting, and test the speaker.\nSample Script\n“Since the issue is continuing, please restart your phone. Once it turns back on, reopen Zoom, rejoin the meeting, and check whether you can hear the other participants.”\n5. Reinstall Zoom\nUninstall the Zoom Workplace mobile app.\nReinstall Zoom from the Apple App Store or Google Play.\nOpen Zoom, rejoin the meeting, and test the speaker again.\nSample Script\n“If restarting didn’t resolve the issue, let’s reinstall Zoom. Please remove the Zoom app, reinstall it from your device’s official app store, then rejoin the meeting and test the audio again.”\nB. Troubleshooting Microphone Issues\n1. Check the Mute Status\nCheck the Audio/Mute icon in the meeting controls.\nIf muted, tap the icon to unmute.\nIf the customer cannot unmute because the host muted them, send the host a chat message requesting to be unmuted.\nSample Script\n“Let’s check whether your microphone is muted. Please tap the Audio icon if it shows that you’re muted. If Zoom won’t allow you to unmute, the host may have muted you, so please send the host a chat message asking to be unmuted.”\n2. Connect to Meeting Audio\nLook for the Join Audio icon in the meeting controls.\nTap Join Audio.\nSelect Call Over Internet.\nIf prompted, allow Zoom to access the microphone.\nSample Script\n“Now let’s make sure your phone is connected to the meeting audio. Please tap Join Audio, then select Call Over Internet. If Zoom asks for microphone permission, select Allow.”\n3. Try Earphones with a Microphone\nConnect compatible earphones or a headset with a built-in microphone.\nTest whether other participants can hear the customer.\nSample Script\n“Let’s try a headset or earphones with a built-in microphone. Once connected, please speak again so we can check whether the meeting can hear you.”\n4. Check Microphone Permissions\niOS: Open Settings → Privacy/Privacy & Security → Microphone, then make sure Zoom has microphone access.\nAndroid: Open Settings → Apps & notifications/Apps → App permissions or Permission Manager → Microphone, then allow Zoom microphone access.\nAndroid menu names can vary by device manufacturer and operating system version.\nSample Script\n“If Zoom still can’t use your microphone, let’s check your phone’s permissions. Please open your device settings, find the microphone permissions, and make sure Zoom is allowed to access the microphone.”\n5. Close Other Apps Using the Microphone\nOpen the device's recent or currently running apps view.\nClose other apps that may be using the microphone.\nReturn to Zoom and test the microphone again.\nSample Script\n“Another app may be using your microphone. Please open your recent apps and close any other apps that could be using audio or the microphone. Then return to Zoom and test your microphone again.”\n6. Restart the Mobile Device\nRestart the mobile device.\nOpen Zoom again.\nRejoin the meeting and test the microphone.\nSample Script\n“Let’s restart your phone to refresh the audio connection. After the restart, reopen Zoom, rejoin the meeting, and test your microphone again.”\n7. Reinstall Zoom\nUninstall the Zoom Workplace mobile app.\nReinstall Zoom from the Apple App Store or Google Play.\nOpen Zoom and allow microphone access if prompted.\nRejoin the meeting and test the microphone.\nSample Script\n“If the microphone issue is still happening, please uninstall Zoom and reinstall it from your device’s official app store. When you reopen Zoom, allow microphone access if prompted, then rejoin the meeting and test again.”\nNote: If the customer is using the Zoom desktop app instead of a mobile device, use the separate Windows/macOS speaker and microphone troubleshooting process.\nQuick Guide / Remember the Process\nSpeaker Issues\nTURN ON → VOLUME → HEADSET → RESTART → REINSTALL\nTURN ON — the Zoom speaker.\nVOLUME — increase the mobile device volume and confirm the phone is not muted.\nHEADSET — try compatible earphones or a headset.\nRESTART — the mobile device and test again.\nREINSTALL — Zoom if the speaker issue continues.\nMicrophone Issues\nUNMUTE → JOIN AUDIO → ALLOW → CLOSE → RESTART → REINSTALL\nUNMUTE — the microphone in the Zoom meeting controls.\nJOIN AUDIO — and select Call Over Internet.\nALLOW — Zoom to access the device microphone.\nCLOSE — other applications that may be using the microphone.\nRESTART — the mobile device and test again.\nREINSTALL — Zoom if the microphone issue continues.\nReference\nPrimary Reference: Zoom Support — Troubleshooting speaker or microphone issues on your mobile device\nSupporting resources:\nZoom Workplace on Apple App Store\nZoom Workplace on Google Play\nUsing in-meeting chat\nDesktop speaker/microphone troubleshooting","images":["assets/rendered/troubleshooting-speaker-or-microphone-issues-on-a-mobile-device-page-1.png","assets/rendered/troubleshooting-speaker-or-microphone-issues-on-a-mobile-device-page-2.png","assets/rendered/troubleshooting-speaker-or-microphone-issues-on-a-mobile-device-page-3.png","assets/rendered/troubleshooting-speaker-or-microphone-issues-on-a-mobile-device-page-4.png","assets/rendered/troubleshooting-speaker-or-microphone-issues-on-a-mobile-device-page-5.png"],"visualReferences":[]},{"id":"uninstalling-and-reinstalling-the-zoom-application","title":"Uninstalling and Reinstalling the Zoom Application","category":"devices","purpose":"To guide agents in assisting arbitrators with removing and reinstalling the Zoom application when a fresh installation is an appropriate approved troubleshooting step.","keywords":"Uninstalling and Reinstalling the Zoom Application devices","steps":["— Identify the Device","Open the Windows search bar and search for Control Panel.","Open the Control Panel app.","If Control Panel is in Category view, select Uninstall a Program. If View by is set to Large icons or Small icons, select Programs and Features.","In the program list, select zoom.us or Zoom Workplace (version 6.0.0 and higher), then select Uninstall.","If prompted to confirm, select Yes.","“We’ll use the standard Windows uninstall first. Please open Control Panel and let me know when you see the list of installed programs.”","Open the Zoom Workplace desktop app."],"referral":"Refer when the issue requires host, account, admin, network, operating-system, or device access beyond basic Zoom guidance.","text":"Process Title: Uninstalling and Reinstalling the Zoom Application\nPurpose: To guide agents in assisting arbitrators with removing and reinstalling the Zoom application when a fresh installation is an appropriate approved troubleshooting step.\n Applies To: Windows, macOS, Linux, Android, iOS, and Zoom Rooms.\n When To Use:  Use this process when Zoom needs to be removed completely from a device, when the current installation is damaged or not functioning as expected, or when an approved troubleshooting process specifically calls for a fresh installation.\nIntroduction\nUninstalling and reinstalling Zoom can resolve problems caused by a damaged installation, outdated application files, or local application settings. The steps differ by operating system, so agents must first identify the arbitrator’s device before providing instructions.\nA standard uninstall should normally be used first. Clean-removal methods on Windows, macOS, and Linux can remove additional local configuration data or cryptographic keys and therefore require extra caution. Agents should not guide an arbitrator through advanced removal steps when doing so exceeds the company’s basic-support scope.\nImportant: Before uninstalling, make sure the arbitrator understands that the Zoom application will be removed and will need to be downloaded and installed again. Do not remove organization-managed applications or settings if the arbitrator does not have permission to do so.\nProcess / Step-by-Step Guide\nStep 1 — Identify the Device\nConfirm whether the arbitrator is using Windows, macOS, Linux, Android, iOS, or a Zoom Room.\nUse only the instructions for that platform.\nIf the device is organization-managed and the arbitrator cannot install/uninstall applications, direct them to their IT/help desk.\nSample Script\n“Before we begin, please confirm which device you are using: Windows, Mac, Linux, Android, iPhone or iPad, or a Zoom Room.”\nA. Windows\nStandard Method — Control Panel\nOpen the Windows search bar and search for Control Panel.\nOpen the Control Panel app.\nIf Control Panel is in Category view, select Uninstall a Program. If View by is set to Large icons or Small icons, select Programs and Features.\nIn the program list, select zoom.us or Zoom Workplace (version 6.0.0 and higher), then select Uninstall.\nIf prompted to confirm, select Yes.\nAfter uninstalling, download the latest Zoom application from the official Zoom Download Center and reinstall it.\nSample Script\n“We’ll use the standard Windows uninstall first. Please open Control Panel and let me know when you see the list of installed programs.”\nOptional Advanced Method — CleanZoom\nImportant: CleanZoom removes the Zoom desktop app plus local configuration/settings and cryptographic keys. This may cause permanent loss of access to certain end-to-end encrypted Zoom Mail emails or encrypted Zoom Phone voicemails unless an appropriate backup key or account escrow is available. It also removes the Zoom plugin for Outlook. For basic arbitrator support, do not use CleanZoom unless specifically authorized by the client’s support procedure.\nDownload and run CleanZoom only when the advanced clean-removal procedure is authorized.\nAfter it completes, download Zoom from the official Zoom Download Center and reinstall it.\nSample Script\n“A complete clean uninstall removes additional Zoom data and may affect encrypted Zoom content. Because this goes beyond a standard uninstall, please use it only if your organization or authorized technical support has instructed you to do so.”\nB. macOS\nStandard Method — Zoom Workplace Menu\nOpen the Zoom Workplace desktop app.\nAt the top of the screen, select Zoom Workplace.\nSelect Uninstall Zoom Workplace.\nSelect OK to confirm removal of the Zoom Workplace desktop app and its components.\nAfter uninstalling, download the latest Zoom Workplace app from the official Zoom Download Center and reinstall it.\nSample Script\n“Please open Zoom Workplace. At the top of your Mac screen, select Zoom Workplace, then choose Uninstall Zoom Workplace.”\nOptional Advanced Method — Remove Keychain Entries\nImportant: Removing Zoom Safe Keychain entries can remove cryptographic keys and may cause permanent loss of access to certain E2EE Zoom Mail emails or encrypted Zoom Phone voicemails. For basic arbitrator support, this should be referred to the arbitrator’s authorized IT/technical support unless your client specifically approves the procedure.\nUninstall Zoom Workplace using the standard macOS method.\nPress Command + Space to open Spotlight Search.\nSearch for Keychain Access.app and open it.\nIf prompted, select Keychain Access.\nIn the search box, enter Zoom Safe.\nRemove the entries whose names begin with Zoom Safe.\nDownload and reinstall Zoom from the official Zoom Download Center.\nC. Linux\nIdentify the Linux distribution before providing a command. Administrative privileges may be required.\nDebian, Ubuntu, or Linux Mint\nOpen Terminal.\nEnter the command below and press Enter:\nsudo apt remove zoom\nAfter removal, download the Zoom Workplace desktop app from the official Zoom Download Center and reinstall it.\nopenSUSE\nOpen Terminal.\nEnter the command below and press Enter:\nsudo zypper remove zoom\nAfter removal, download Zoom Workplace from the official Zoom Download Center and reinstall it.\nOracle Linux, CentOS, Red Hat, or Fedora\nOpen Terminal.\nEnter the command below and press Enter:\nsudo yum remove zoom\nAfter removal, download Zoom Workplace from the official Zoom Download Center and reinstall it.\nOptional Advanced Method — Remove Local Zoom Configuration\nImportant: The following clean-removal command deletes locally stored Zoom configuration data. Clean removal can also affect cryptographic keys and encrypted Zoom content. Do not use advanced removal commands unless authorized and the arbitrator understands the impact.\nrm -r $HOME/.config/zoom.conf $HOME/.zoom\nSample Script\n“Because Linux uninstall commands depend on the distribution and may require administrator privileges, I’ll first confirm which Linux version you’re using.”\nD. Android\nMethod 1 — Google Play Store\nOpen the Google Play Store.\nSearch for Zoom Workplace.\nOpen Zoom Workplace.\nTap Uninstall.\nWhen prompted, tap OK.\nAfter removal, reinstall Zoom Workplace from the Google Play Store.\nMethod 2 — Android Settings\nUse this method if uninstalling through Google Play is unsuccessful or Zoom was installed using an APK. Menu names can vary by Android version and device manufacturer.\nOpen Settings.\nTap Apps.\nFind and select Zoom.\nTap Uninstall.\nWhen prompted, tap OK.\nReinstall Zoom Workplace from the Google Play Store.\nSample Script\n“On Android, we can remove Zoom through the Google Play Store or through your device’s Apps settings. The exact menu names may look slightly different depending on your phone.”\nE. iOS\nOpen Settings.\nTap General.\nTap iPhone Storage or iPad Storage.\nTap Zoom.\nTap Delete App.\nWhen warned that deleting Zoom also deletes its documents and data, tap Delete App to confirm.\nAfter removal, reinstall Zoom from the iOS App Store.\nImportant: Zoom notes that some cryptographic keys remain in the iOS Keychain and currently cannot be removed completely.\nSample Script\n“Please open Settings, then General, and select iPhone Storage or iPad Storage. We’ll locate Zoom there and remove the app before reinstalling it from the App Store.”\nF. Zoom Rooms\nRemove and Reinstall the Zoom Rooms Applications\nUninstall the Zoom Rooms app from the room device using the uninstall method for that device’s operating system.\nUninstall the Zoom Rooms Controller app from the controller device using the uninstall method for that controller’s operating system.\nAfter removal, download Zoom Rooms from the official Zoom Download Center and reinstall the required Zoom Rooms applications.\nSample Script\n“Zoom Rooms can involve both a room device and a controller. The Zoom Rooms application and controller application may need to be handled separately.”\nDeleting a Zoom Room from the Web Portal — Admin-Only\nImportant: Deleting a Zoom Room from the Zoom web portal requires administrator access or a role with access to Zoom Rooms. This is outside basic arbitrator support. Agents should not instruct an arbitrator to perform these steps unless the user is an authorized administrator and the client’s process specifically permits it.\nAn authorized administrator signs in to the Zoom web portal.\nOpen Admin Center.\nSelect Product configuration, then Rooms, then Zoom Rooms.\nSelect the Zoom Room to be deleted.\nSelect Delete.\nSample Script\n“Deleting a Zoom Room from the web portal requires administrative access. Since our support is limited to basic Zoom assistance, please contact your organization’s authorized Zoom administrator for this action.”\nAfter Reinstalling — Confirm the Result\nOpen Zoom Workplace or the Zoom Rooms application.\nConfirm that the application launches successfully.\nIf appropriate, confirm the arbitrator can access the meeting/join screen.\nTest the specific feature that originally led to the reinstall, such as joining, audio, or video.\nDo not assume reinstalling resolved the issue; confirm the original problem is no longer occurring.\nSample Script\n“Zoom has been reinstalled. Let’s open the application and check the original issue again to confirm whether the fresh installation resolved it.”\nWhen to Stop and Refer for Additional Assistance\nThe arbitrator does not have permission to uninstall or install applications.\nThe device is managed by an organization and installation is restricted.\nAdministrative credentials are required and the arbitrator does not have them.\nThe issue requires CleanZoom, Keychain removal, Linux configuration deletion, or another advanced clean-removal step outside approved basic support.\nThe issue involves Zoom Rooms web-portal administration.\nThe installation repeatedly fails after the standard reinstall process.\nThe original issue continues after a successful reinstall and all approved basic troubleshooting is complete.\nSample Script\n“We’ve reached a step that requires permissions or technical access beyond the basic Zoom support we provide. Please contact your organization’s IT help desk or authorized Zoom administrator for additional assistance.”\nQuick Guide / Remember the Process\nReference\nSource: Zoom Support guidance provided for “Uninstalling and reinstalling the Zoom application.” Use the current official Zoom Download Center and the applicable official Zoom installation instructions when reinstalling. Advanced clean-removal methods should be used only when appropriate and authorized.","images":["assets/rendered/uninstalling-and-reinstalling-the-zoom-application-page-1.png","assets/rendered/uninstalling-and-reinstalling-the-zoom-application-page-2.png","assets/rendered/uninstalling-and-reinstalling-the-zoom-application-page-3.png","assets/rendered/uninstalling-and-reinstalling-the-zoom-application-page-4.png","assets/rendered/uninstalling-and-reinstalling-the-zoom-application-page-5.png","assets/rendered/uninstalling-and-reinstalling-the-zoom-application-page-6.png","assets/rendered/uninstalling-and-reinstalling-the-zoom-application-page-7.png"],"visualReferences":[]},{"id":"using-bluetooth-headphones-with-zoom-on-android-ios","title":"Using Bluetooth Headphones with Zoom on Android   iOS","category":"audio","purpose":"Provide a standardized process for connecting, selecting, and testing Bluetooth headphones in Zoom meetings across desktop and mobile devices, including supported native Bluetooth call-control functionality on Windows.","keywords":"Using Bluetooth Headphones with Zoom on Android   iOS audio","steps":["Connect the Headphones and Join the Meeting","Join with Computer Audio","Open the Audio Device Menu","Select the Bluetooth Headphones as the Microphone","Select the Bluetooth Headphones as the Speaker","Connect to Meeting Audio","Confirm the Bluetooth Headphones"],"referral":"Refer when the issue requires host, account, admin, network, operating-system, or device access beyond basic Zoom guidance.","text":"Process Title: Using Bluetooth Headphones with Zoom\nPurpose: Provide a standardized process for connecting, selecting, and testing Bluetooth headphones in Zoom meetings across desktop and mobile devices, including supported native Bluetooth call-control functionality on Windows.\nApplies To: Zoom desktop app on Windows/macOS; Zoom mobile app on Android/iOS; and supported Zoom-certified native Bluetooth headsets on eligible Windows 11 PCs.\nWhen To Use: When a customer wants to use Bluetooth headphones for Zoom audio, cannot confirm the correct Bluetooth microphone/speaker, or needs help setting up a Zoom-certified native Bluetooth headset and its call controls.\nRequirements / Before You Begin\nBluetooth headphones paired and connected to the device being used for Zoom.\nFor Android/iOS, first-time Zoom users may be prompted to grant required device permissions.\nFor native Bluetooth call controls on Windows, the headset and PC must meet the additional requirements listed later in this document.\nSample Script\n“Before we start, please make sure your Bluetooth headphones are paired and connected to the device you’re using for Zoom. If Zoom asks for permissions on mobile, please allow the permissions needed for meeting audio.”\nIntroduction\nBluetooth headphones can provide a convenient wireless audio option for Zoom meetings by allowing customers to use a compatible headset for both listening and speaking. Depending on the device and headset, customers may also have access to supported call-control features. This process provides step-by-step guidance for connecting, selecting, and testing Bluetooth headphones in Zoom, as well as identifying the appropriate setup for supported Zoom-certified native Bluetooth headsets on eligible Windows devices.\nProcess / Step-by-Step Guide\nA. Windows / macOS - Connect Bluetooth Headphones for a Zoom Meeting\n1. Connect the Headphones and Join the Meeting\nConnect the Bluetooth headphones to the computer.\nJoin or start the Zoom meeting.\nSample Script\n“Please make sure your Bluetooth headphones are connected to your computer, then join or start the Zoom meeting.”\n2. Join with Computer Audio\nIf Zoom prompts you to choose an audio method, click Join with Computer Audio.\nSample Script\n“If Zoom asks how you want to join the audio, please select ‘Join with Computer Audio.’”\n3. Open the Audio Device Menu\nIn the meeting controls toolbar, locate Mute/Unmute.\nClick the arrow next to Mute/Unmute.\nSample Script\n“Next to Mute or Unmute, click the small arrow to open the audio device options.”\n4. Select the Bluetooth Headphones as the Microphone\nUnder Select a Microphone, choose the Bluetooth headphones.\nSample Script\n“Under ‘Select a Microphone,’ please choose your Bluetooth headphones.”\n5. Select the Bluetooth Headphones as the Speaker\nUnder Select a Speaker, choose the same Bluetooth headphones.\nConfirm that meeting audio is heard through the headphones.\nSample Script\n“Under ‘Select a Speaker,’ choose your Bluetooth headphones as well. Then confirm that you can hear the meeting through them.”\nImportant: If the headphones are not listed under Microphone or Speaker, they may not currently be connected to the computer.\nSample Script\n“If your headphones aren’t listed, let’s check the computer’s Bluetooth connection first and make sure the headset is still connected.”\nB. Android / iOS - Connect Bluetooth Headphones for a Zoom Meeting\n1. Connect the Headphones and Join the Meeting\nConnect the Bluetooth headphones to the mobile device.\nJoin or start a Zoom meeting.\nIf this is the first Zoom meeting on the device, grant the requested Zoom permissions.\nSample Script\n“Please make sure your headphones are connected to your phone or tablet, then join or start the meeting. If Zoom asks for permissions, please allow them so the meeting audio can work correctly.”\n2. Connect to Meeting Audio\nTap Wifi or Cellular Data.\nSample Script\n“When Zoom asks how you want to connect to the meeting audio, tap ‘Wifi or Cellular Data.’”\n3. Confirm the Bluetooth Headphones\nOptional: In the top corner of the meeting, tap the Bluetooth audio option/logo if it appears.\nConfirm that the Bluetooth headphones are selected.\nSample Script\n“In the top corner, look for the Bluetooth audio option. If you see it, tap it and confirm that your headphones are selected.”\nIf the Bluetooth indicator is missing: The headphones may not be connected. Disconnect them from the mobile device, reconnect them while remaining in the meeting, then check again.\nSample Script\n“If you don’t see the speaker icon with the Bluetooth indicator, please disconnect the headphones, reconnect them while you stay in the meeting, and then check the audio option again.”\nC. Windows - Use a Zoom-Certified Native Bluetooth Headset\nZoom-certified native Bluetooth headsets can connect directly to a supported Windows PC’s built-in Bluetooth radio without a USB adapter. Supported call controls can include answering or ending calls, adjusting volume, and synchronizing mute status with the Zoom Workplace desktop app.\nmacOS limitation: Native Bluetooth call control is not currently supported in the Zoom Workplace desktop app for macOS. A Zoom-certified headset can still connect by Bluetooth for audio, but a USB adapter is required for full call-control functionality.\nNative Bluetooth Requirements\nZoom-certified native Bluetooth headset.\nZoom Workplace app for Windows version 6.7.5 or higher.\nWindows 11 25H2, Build 26200.8246 or newer.\nSupported Intel processor: Recommended - Intel Core Ultra Series 2 or Series 3; Compatible - Intel Core Ultra Series 1, 2, 3, and 12th-14th Gen Intel Core.\nSample Script\n“For native Bluetooth call controls, we’ll first confirm that the headset is Zoom-certified and that the Windows version, Zoom version, and processor meet the supported requirements.”\nPair the Native Bluetooth Headset to Windows\nBefore pairing, make sure the headset firmware and PC drivers are up to date.\nPut the headset into pairing mode according to the manufacturer’s instructions.\nOpen Windows Settings > Bluetooth & devices.\nIf Swift Pair appears, select the notification to complete pairing. If it does not appear, select Add device > Bluetooth, then choose the headset.\nOpen the Zoom desktop app.\nClick the profile picture > Settings > Audio.\nConfirm that the headset is selected as both the Microphone and Speaker.\nJoin a Zoom test meeting to verify call controls and mute synchronization.\nSample Script\n“Please put the headset in pairing mode, then open Windows Settings and go to Bluetooth & devices. Pair the headset, open Zoom Settings, select Audio, and confirm the headset is selected for both the microphone and speaker. Then we’ll use a Zoom test meeting to verify the controls.”\nD. Check and Update Windows Drivers for Native Bluetooth\nKeeping the supported Intel audio, Bluetooth, and Wi-Fi drivers up to date helps provide the best native Bluetooth call-control experience.\nSample Script\n“If native Bluetooth call controls aren’t working as expected, we’ll check whether the required Bluetooth, Wi-Fi, and audio drivers meet the supported minimum versions.”\nE. Check Individual Intel Driver Versions\nCheck the Intel Bluetooth Driver Version\nPress Windows + X and select Device Manager.\nExpand Bluetooth and locate Intel(R) Wireless Bluetooth.\nRight-click the entry, select Properties, then open the Driver tab to view the version number.\nOptional: To download the latest version, visit the Intel Download Center and navigate to Wireless > Intel Wireless Bluetooth Drivers.\nSample Script\n“Let’s check the Bluetooth driver version. Press Windows + X, open Device Manager, expand Bluetooth, and open the properties for Intel(R) Wireless Bluetooth. Under the Driver tab, we can confirm the installed version.”\nCheck the Intel Wi-Fi Driver Version\nPress Windows + X and select Device Manager.\nExpand Network Adapters and locate Intel(R) Wi-Fi Component.\nRight-click the entry, select Properties, then open the Driver tab to view the version number.\nOptional: To download the latest version, visit the Intel Download Center and navigate to Wireless > Intel Wireless Wi-Fi Drivers.\nSample Script\n“Next, we’ll check the Wi-Fi driver. In Device Manager, expand Network Adapters, locate the Intel Wi-Fi component, open Properties, and check the version under the Driver tab.”\nCheck the Intel iSST Driver Version\nPress Windows + X and select Device Manager.\nExpand Sound, video and game controllers.\nLocate Intel Smart Sound Technology for Bluetooth Audio (Bluetooth Classic).\nIf supported by the headset, also look for Intel Smart Sound Technology for Bluetooth LE Audio.\nRight-click the relevant entry, select Properties, then open the Driver tab to view the version number.\nNote: Intel Smart Sound Technology (iSST) driver updates are provided by the laptop manufacturer (OEM), not directly by Intel. Check the PC manufacturer’s support site for the latest iSST package for the specific model. Depending on company IT policy, elevated privileges may be required. Contact IT if the update cannot be completed.\nSample Script\n“For the iSST driver, expand Sound, video and game controllers and locate the Intel Smart Sound Technology Bluetooth audio entry. We’ll check the Driver tab for the version. If an update is needed, it should come from your computer manufacturer, and your IT team may need to assist.”\nF. When to Use a USB Adapter Instead of Native Bluetooth\nConsider using a USB adapter when native Bluetooth may be less reliable, including:\nA high-density wireless environment where Bluetooth interference is common.\nA PC whose Bluetooth radio is already occupied by multiple paired devices.\nAn older PC whose Bluetooth radio no longer receives driver updates from the manufacturer.\nSample Script\n“A USB adapter may be a better option if there’s heavy Bluetooth interference, several devices are already using the computer’s Bluetooth radio, or the PC no longer receives Bluetooth driver updates.”\nG. Known Limitations of Native Bluetooth Headsets\nMute Synchronization Limitation\nWithout the headset manufacturer’s desktop software, such as Jabra Direct or Poly Lens Desktop on Windows, mute synchronization may not always be consistent.\nWhen mute is triggered from the Zoom app, the headset’s mute state may not update accordingly.\nWhen mute is triggered from the headset, the Zoom app should reflect the mute state correctly.\nFor more consistent mute synchronization, install and run the headset manufacturer’s desktop software alongside Zoom.\nSample Script\n“If the mute status on Zoom and the headset doesn’t always match, the headset’s manufacturer software may be needed for more consistent mute synchronization.”\nConcurrent Call Handling Limitation\nWindows limits concurrent calls to two when Zoom and a third-party application use call control at the same time.\nWhen Zoom and another application share the same headset, Zoom call control may be temporarily suspended in some scenarios to prevent conflicts. Zoom app operations are not affected.\nSample Script\n“If the same headset is being used by Zoom and another calling application, Windows limits concurrent calls and Zoom call controls may temporarily pause to prevent conflicts.”\nH. Troubleshoot Bluetooth Headphones Not Working with Zoom\nTest Audio Settings in Zoom\nSelect the Bluetooth headphones in Zoom audio settings before a meeting, or use a Zoom test meeting to confirm that the headset is connected and working with Zoom.\nStop All Other Music or Audio\nPause or close other apps that are playing audio. Incoming calls, music, or other audio can interrupt Zoom meeting audio.\nDisconnect Bluetooth Headphones from Other Devices\nDisconnect the headphones from other devices so they are connected only to the device being used for the Zoom meeting.\nUnpair and Pair the Headphones Again\nDisconnect or unpair the Bluetooth headphones from the device, then pair and reconnect them again.\nReset AirPods\nIf Apple AirPods continue to have connection problems, reset the AirPods using Apple’s recommended reset process.\nReview Android Nearby Device Permissions\nOn Android, make sure Zoom has Nearby device permissions. If the issue continues, delete app storage, uninstall Zoom, restart the phone, then reinstall the Zoom mobile app.\nConnect to a Different Headset\nIf the issue continues, try another headset. A supported USB device is recommended when available.\nPause or close other music or audio apps that may interrupt Zoom audio.\nDisconnect the Bluetooth headphones from other devices so they are connected only to the device being used for Zoom.\nUnpair and pair the headphones again.\nOn Android, confirm that Zoom has the required Nearby device permissions.\nIf needed, continue with the full Bluetooth headphone troubleshooting process.\nSample Script\n“If the headset still isn’t working, we’ll close other apps using audio, disconnect the headset from any other devices, and pair it again. On Android, we’ll also check Zoom’s Nearby device permission.”\nQuick Guide / Remember the Process\nCONNECT → SELECT → TEST → CHECK DRIVERS → REPAIR → USB / ESCALATE\nCONNECT - pair the Bluetooth headset with the computer or mobile device.\nJOIN AUDIO - join the meeting and connect to computer audio or Wifi/Cellular Data.\nSELECT MIC - choose the headset as the microphone when desktop options are available.\nSELECT SPEAKER - choose the headset as the speaker.\nTEST - confirm audio and, for native Windows headsets, call controls/mute sync.\nTROUBLESHOOT - reconnect, re-pair, check permissions, or verify native Bluetooth requirements and drivers.\nReference\nPrimary Reference: Zoom Support - Using Bluetooth headphones with Zoom\nZoom-certified native Bluetooth headsets\nBluetooth headphone troubleshooting\nZoom test meeting","images":["assets/rendered/using-bluetooth-headphones-with-zoom-on-android-ios-page-1.png","assets/rendered/using-bluetooth-headphones-with-zoom-on-android-ios-page-2.png","assets/rendered/using-bluetooth-headphones-with-zoom-on-android-ios-page-3.png","assets/rendered/using-bluetooth-headphones-with-zoom-on-android-ios-page-4.png","assets/rendered/using-bluetooth-headphones-with-zoom-on-android-ios-page-5.png","assets/rendered/using-bluetooth-headphones-with-zoom-on-android-ios-page-6.png","assets/rendered/using-bluetooth-headphones-with-zoom-on-android-ios-page-7.png","assets/rendered/using-bluetooth-headphones-with-zoom-on-android-ios-page-8.png"],"visualReferences":[]},{"id":"using-non-verbal-feedback-and-meeting-reactions","title":"Using Non-Verbal Feedback and Meeting Reactions","category":"controls","purpose":"To guide agents in helping arbitrators use Zoom reactions and non-verbal feedback to communicate during a meeting without interrupting the speaker. This document also explains how hosts can view, lower raised hands, and clear feedback.","keywords":"Using Non-Verbal Feedback and Meeting Reactions controls","steps":["On Windows and macOS, users can choose a reaction skin tone in Zoom Settings > General > Emoji and reactions skin tone.","In the meeting controls toolbar, click React.","Choose an available reaction or non-verbal feedback option. If permitted, click the ellipsis (...) to open the full emoji set.","To remove persistent feedback, select the same control again or use the corresponding removal option, such as Lower Hand.","Open the Zoom desktop app.","Click your profile picture, then Settings.","Select General.","Under Emoji and reactions skin tone, choose your preferred skin tone."],"referral":"Refer when the issue requires host, account, admin, network, operating-system, or device access beyond basic Zoom guidance.","text":"Process Title: Using Non-Verbal Feedback and Meeting Reactions in Zoom\nPurpose: To guide agents in helping arbitrators use Zoom reactions and non-verbal feedback to communicate during a meeting without interrupting the speaker. This document also explains how hosts can view, lower raised hands, and clear feedback.\nApplies To: This process applies to Zoom participants and hosts using the Zoom desktop app on Windows, macOS, or Linux, the Zoom Web App, or the Zoom mobile app on Android or iOS to use or manage meeting reactions and non-verbal feedback. \nWhen To Use: This process should be used when an arbitrator needs to use meeting reactions or non-verbal feedback, such as Raise Hand, Yes/No, Slow Down, or Speed Up; when they need assistance locating these controls; when a host needs to review or clear participant feedback; or when preparing arbitrators to use non-verbal participation features before a Zoom proceeding. \nRequirements and Important Behavior\nMeeting reactions must be enabled for the meeting/account.\nUse a supported current Zoom desktop/mobile version or the Zoom Web App.\nAnimated reactions require a device that meets Zoom's virtual-background system requirements.\nEmoji reactions disappear after about 10 seconds.\nRaise Hand and persistent non-verbal feedback such as Yes, No, Slow Down, and Speed Up remain until manually removed by the participant or host.\nAvailable reactions can depend on host/account settings. The full emoji set may not be available.\nOn Windows and macOS, users can choose a reaction skin tone in Zoom Settings > General > Emoji and reactions skin tone.\nOn supported Apple devices, macOS/iOS gesture-triggered video effects are controlled by Apple settings, not Zoom.\nIntroduction\nZoom’s non-verbal feedback and meeting reactions allow participants to communicate during a meeting without interrupting the person who is speaking. These features are especially useful during arbitration proceedings, where participants may need to signal that they have a question, provide a simple response, or request a change in pace while maintaining an orderly meeting.\nParticipants can use options such as Raise Hand, Yes, No, Slow Down, Speed Up, and other available reactions. Selected feedback appears with the participant’s name and, depending on the platform, may also appear in their video panel, allowing the host and other participants to recognize the response.\nSome reactions are temporary, while others remain visible until they are manually removed. Standard emoji reactions typically disappear after approximately 10 seconds, while Raise Hand and non-verbal feedback such as Yes, No, Slow Down, and Speed Up remain active until cleared by the participant or host.\nThe location and availability of these controls may differ depending on whether the participant is using the Zoom desktop app, Zoom Web App, or Zoom mobile app, as well as the meeting settings configured by the host. Agents should therefore first identify the arbitrator’s device and platform before providing instructions.\nProcess / Step-by-Step Guide\nThe following steps explain how participants can provide non-verbal feedback and reactions and how hosts can view and manage them across the different Zoom platforms. \nA. Windows | macOS | Linux\nParticipant: Provide Non-Verbal Feedback or a Reaction\nJoin the Zoom meeting as a participant.\nIn the meeting controls toolbar, click React.\nChoose an available reaction or non-verbal feedback option. If permitted, click the ellipsis (...) to open the full emoji set.\nAvailable options can include Clapping Hands, Thumbs Up, Heart, Tears of Joy, Open Mouth, Party Popper, Yes, No, Slow Down, Speed Up, I'm Away, Raise Hand/Lower Hand, and Be Right Back.\nThe selected icon appears in the participant's video panel and beside their name in the Participants list.\nTo remove persistent feedback, select the same control again or use the corresponding removal option, such as Lower Hand.\nSample Script\n\t“At the bottom of your Zoom window, click React. Choose the reaction or feedback you need. If you need to signal that you would like to speak, select Raise Hand. Persistent feedback stays visible until it is removed.”\nWindows and macOS Only: Set Reaction Skin Tone\nOpen the Zoom desktop app.\nClick your profile picture, then Settings.\nSelect General.\nUnder Emoji and reactions skin tone, choose your preferred skin tone.\nNote: This skin-tone setting is documented for Windows and macOS, not Linux.\nHost: Manage Feedback and Reactions\nClick Participants to open the participant list.\nReview icons displayed beside participant names. Counts above feedback icons show how many participants selected each option.\nTo lower a participant's raised hand, hover over the participant's name and click Lower Hand.\nTo remove all persistent feedback, open Participants, select the More (...) control, then choose Clear All Feedback.\nRemember that ordinary emoji reactions disappear automatically after about 10 seconds; persistent feedback and Raise Hand require manual removal.\nSample Script\n\t“Open Participants to view the feedback beside each participant's name. You can lower an individual raised hand or use Clear All Feedback when you need to remove persistent feedback from the meeting.”\nB. Zoom Web App\nParticipant: Provide Non-Verbal Feedback\nJoin the Zoom meeting as a participant in the Zoom Web App.\nIn the meeting controls toolbar, click React.\nSelect an available reaction or feedback icon. If the host allows it, use the ellipsis (...) to access additional emojis.\nOnly one feedback icon can be active at a time.\nThe selected feedback appears beside the participant's name in the Participants list.\nSelect the icon again, or use the applicable removal option, to remove persistent feedback.\nSample Script\n\t\t“In the meeting toolbar, click React and select the feedback you want to send. Only one feedback icon can be active at a time in the Web App.”\nHost: Manage Feedback\nClick Participants to open the participant list.\nReview the feedback icon beside each participant's name and the count for each feedback type.\nTo lower a raised hand, hover over the participant and select Lower Hand.\nTo remove persistent feedback for everyone, use the clear-all feedback option in the Participants panel.\nEmoji reactions disappear automatically after about 10 seconds; Raise Hand and persistent feedback must be manually removed.\nSample Script\n\t\t“Open Participants to review participant feedback. If needed, lower an individual hand or use the clear-all option to remove persistent feedback.”\nC. Android | iOS\nParticipant: Provide Non-Verbal Feedback or a Reaction\nJoin the Zoom meeting using the Zoom mobile app.\nTap the meeting screen if necessary to display the meeting controls.\nTap More.\nAt the top of the menu, choose an available reaction or feedback icon. Tap the ellipsis (...) to view the full set of reactions when available.\nOnly one feedback icon can be active at a time.\nThe selected icon appears in the participant's video panel and beside their name in the Participants list.\nSample Script\n\t“Tap the Zoom meeting screen to show the controls, then tap More. Choose the reaction or feedback you need. If you need to speak, select Raise Hand when it is available.”\nHost: Manage Feedback\nTap More in the meeting controls.\nTap Participants.\nTo clear all feedback, tap the ellipsis (...) in the upper-right corner of the Participants panel.\nTap Clear all feedback.\nTo lower one participant's raised hand, tap the participant's name, then tap Lower Hand.\nSample Script\n\t“Tap More, then Participants. From there, you can lower an individual raised hand or open the additional options and select Clear all feedback.”\nQuick Guide / Remember the Process\nKey Agent Reminders\nAsk which platform the arbitrator is using before giving directions.\nDo not assume every reaction is available; meeting settings can limit reactions.\nRaise Hand and persistent non-verbal feedback do not disappear automatically.\nDo not confuse Apple's gesture-based video effects with Zoom reactions on supported macOS/iOS devices.\nGive one instruction at a time and confirm the arbitrator can see the expected icon.\nFor arbitration support, remain neutral and procedural; reactions should be used according to the proceeding's instructions.\nReference\nZoom Support: Using non-verbal feedback and meeting reactions\nSupporting Zoom references cited in the source article include Meeting Reactions settings, Participant Controls, Host Controls, Global Minimum Version, and Virtual Background system requirements.","images":["assets/rendered/using-non-verbal-feedback-and-meeting-reactions-page-1.png","assets/rendered/using-non-verbal-feedback-and-meeting-reactions-page-2.png","assets/rendered/using-non-verbal-feedback-and-meeting-reactions-page-3.png","assets/rendered/using-non-verbal-feedback-and-meeting-reactions-page-4.png","assets/rendered/using-non-verbal-feedback-and-meeting-reactions-page-5.png","assets/rendered/using-non-verbal-feedback-and-meeting-reactions-page-6.png"],"visualReferences":[{"title":"Locate React and Raise Hand","image":"assets/visual-references/workplace-controls.png","documentStep":"Reactions and Non-verbal Feedback — Select React from the meeting toolbar","instruction":"Click React to choose an available reaction or Raise Hand. If the option is absent, check More and remember that host settings can limit availability.","expectedResult":"The selected reaction appears in the meeting or the hand status changes.","platform":"Desktop — Zoom Workplace 6.x meeting toolbar","sourceName":"Stanford University IT","sourceUrl":"https://uit.stanford.edu/service/zoom/release","status":"Current Zoom Workplace interface matched to process document"}]},{"id":"using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting","title":"Using Participant Controls in a Zoom Meeting","category":"controls","purpose":"Provide a clear guide for helping participants locate and use common in-meeting controls, Zoom AI features, and personal meeting settings on Android/iOS and the Zoom Web App.","keywords":"Using Participant Controls in a Zoom Meeting controls","steps":["Quick Guide / Remember the Process","If the controls are hidden, tap anywhere on the meeting screen to display them.","To keep the controls visible: tap More > Settings > Meeting settings > enable Always show meeting controls.","If Zoom AI is already enabled, select the available AI feature and follow the prompts.","If the host has not enabled Zoom AI, tap the AI icon and select Ask host to start Zoom AI.","“On the Zoom Web App, your meeting controls are at the bottom of the meeting window. The order can vary, so if you don’t see a control immediately, check the More menu.”","“If the control you need isn’t on the main toolbar, click More. You may find recording requests, captions, Whiteboards, incoming-video controls, Settings, or Reset there.”","Tap or click More and check whether the control is located there."],"referral":"Refer when the issue requires host, account, admin, network, operating-system, or device access beyond basic Zoom guidance.","text":"Process Title: Using Participant Controls in a Zoom Meeting\nPurpose: Provide a clear guide for helping participants locate and use common in-meeting controls, Zoom AI features, and personal meeting settings on Android/iOS and the Zoom Web App.\nApplies To: Meeting participants using the Zoom mobile app for Android/iOS or the Zoom Web App. Available controls can vary by host/admin settings, Zoom plan, device support, customization, and usage.\nWhen To Use: When a customer needs help finding or using audio, video, chat, participants, sharing, AI, captions, settings, or other participant controls during a Zoom meeting.\nRequirements\nParticipant in a Zoom meeting.\nZoom mobile app for Android or iOS using the supported minimum version, or the Zoom Web App.\nSome controls require the host or account administrator to enable the feature and may depend on the customer's Zoom plan or device.\nIntroduction\nZoom participant controls allow customers to manage common meeting functions such as audio, video, chat, screen sharing, reactions, captions, and other available features during a meeting. Depending on the device, account, and meeting settings, customers may also have access to Zoom AI features and additional controls. This process provides step-by-step guidance for locating and using participant controls on Android, iOS, and the Zoom Web App.\nProcess / Step-by-Step Guide\nA. Android / iOS - Access the Meeting Controls\nJoin the Zoom meeting using the mobile app.\nLook for the meeting controls toolbar at the bottom of the meeting window.\nIf the controls are hidden, tap anywhere on the meeting screen to display them.\nTo keep the controls visible: tap More > Settings > Meeting settings > enable Always show meeting controls.\nSample Script\n“Please tap the meeting screen once to display the controls at the bottom. If you want them to stay visible, tap More, then Settings, Meeting settings, and turn on ‘Always show meeting controls.’”\nB. Android / iOS - Main Controls to Look For\nSample Script\n“The main controls are along the bottom of the meeting. Look for Join Audio or the microphone, Video, Chat, and More. If you don’t see the option you need, tap More for additional controls.”\nC. Android / iOS - Controls Under More\nSample Script\n“Tap More to see additional participant options. Depending on the meeting, you may find reactions, Participants, sharing, recording requests, captions, Transcript, Apps, Meeting info, Settings, and Leave.”\nD. Android / iOS - Use Zoom AI Features\nIf Zoom AI is already enabled, select the available AI feature and follow the prompts.\nIf the host has not enabled Zoom AI, tap the AI icon and select Ask host to start Zoom AI.\nAvailability depends on the meeting, account, plan, and host/admin settings.\nSample Script\n“To use Zoom AI, tap the AI icon at the top of the meeting. If the feature hasn’t been started yet, select ‘Ask host to start Zoom AI.’ If it’s available, you can use options such as meeting questions or meeting summary.”\nE. Zoom Web App - Access the Meeting Controls\nJoin the meeting using the Zoom Web App.\nUse the meeting controls toolbar at the bottom of the meeting window.\nControls may appear in a different order depending on customization and usage.\nSample Script\n“On the Zoom Web App, your meeting controls are at the bottom of the meeting window. The order can vary, so if you don’t see a control immediately, check the More menu.”\nF. Zoom Web App - Main Participant Controls\nScript for Agents to Tell the Customers\n“On the Web App, you can use Audio, Video, Participants, Chat, React, Share, Zoom AI, More, and Leave from the meeting toolbar. The small arrows next to Audio or Video open additional device and settings options.”\nG. Zoom Web App - Additional Controls Under More\nStart/Stop Incoming Video can disable incoming participant videos to help preserve bandwidth.\nReset restores toolbar options to their default placement.\nSome options may be unavailable because of host/admin settings, plan limitations, or device/browser support.\nScript for Agents to Tell the Customers\n“If the control you need isn’t on the main toolbar, click More. You may find recording requests, captions, Whiteboards, incoming-video controls, Settings, or Reset there.”\nIf a Control Is Missing\nTap or click More and check whether the control is located there.\nConfirm that the meeting host has enabled the feature.\nConfirm that the account administrator has not disabled the feature.\nCheck whether the customer's Zoom plan and device or browser support the feature.\nRemember that toolbar placement may vary because controls can be customized or reordered.\nScript for Agents to Tell the Customers\n“If you don’t see the control, please check More first. If it still isn’t available, the host or account administrator may have disabled it, or the feature may not be supported by your plan or device.”\n6. Quick Guide / Remember the Process\nTAP / LOOK → FIND CONTROL → MORE → USE FEATURE → CHECK SETTINGS\nTAP / LOOK - tap the mobile screen or look at the bottom toolbar in the Web App.\nFIND CONTROL - locate Audio, Video, Chat, Participants, Share, AI, or another needed option.\nMORE - open More when the control is not on the main toolbar.\nUSE FEATURE - select the needed meeting function.\nCHECK SETTINGS - if the option is missing or unavailable.\n7. Reference\nPrimary Reference: Zoom Support - Participant controls in a meeting\nSupporting Zoom resources referenced in the source include Meeting Summary, Meeting Questions, My Notes, Joining a Meeting, Chat, Screen Sharing, Cloud Recording, Captions, Zoom Apps, Virtual Backgrounds, Filters, Avatars, Zoom Rooms, Whiteboards, and personal in-meeting Settings.","images":["assets/rendered/using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-1.png","assets/rendered/using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-10.png","assets/rendered/using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-11.png","assets/rendered/using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-12.png","assets/rendered/using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-13.png","assets/rendered/using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-14.png","assets/rendered/using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-15.png","assets/rendered/using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-16.png","assets/rendered/using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-17.png","assets/rendered/using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-18.png","assets/rendered/using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-19.png","assets/rendered/using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-2.png","assets/rendered/using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-20.png","assets/rendered/using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-21.png","assets/rendered/using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-22.png","assets/rendered/using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-23.png","assets/rendered/using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-24.png","assets/rendered/using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-25.png","assets/rendered/using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-26.png","assets/rendered/using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-27.png","assets/rendered/using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-28.png","assets/rendered/using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-29.png","assets/rendered/using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-3.png","assets/rendered/using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-30.png","assets/rendered/using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-31.png","assets/rendered/using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-32.png","assets/rendered/using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-33.png","assets/rendered/using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-4.png","assets/rendered/using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-5.png","assets/rendered/using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-6.png","assets/rendered/using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-7.png","assets/rendered/using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-8.png","assets/rendered/using-participant-controls-in-a-zoom-meeting-page-9.png"],"visualReferences":[{"title":"Open participant controls","image":"assets/visual-references/workplace-controls.png","documentStep":"Participant Controls — Select Participants from the meeting toolbar","instruction":"Click Participants on the bottom toolbar to open the participant list and available participant actions.","expectedResult":"The participant panel opens and the customer can see who is connected.","platform":"Desktop — Zoom Workplace 6.x meeting toolbar","sourceName":"Stanford University IT","sourceUrl":"https://uit.stanford.edu/service/zoom/release","status":"Current Zoom Workplace interface matched to process document"}]},{"id":"viewing-participants-already-in-a-meeting-before-joining","title":"Viewing Participants Already in a Meeting Before Joining","category":"controls","purpose":"Provide a standardized process for helping customers check which participants have already joined a Zoom meeting before they enter the session.","keywords":"Viewing Participants Already in a Meeting Before Joining controls","steps":["Purpose: Provide a standardized process for helping customers check which participants have already joined a Zoom meeting before they enter the session.","Click the Home tab.","Open or view the meeting details.","Click the Calendar tab.","Select the meeting you want to review.","Click the forward arrow next to Invitees to view the participants who have already joined.","In the upper-right corner, click the Calendar side panel button.","In the upper-right corner, open the calendar view drop-down menu and select Day."],"referral":"Refer when the issue requires host, account, admin, network, operating-system, or device access beyond basic Zoom guidance.","text":"Process Title: Viewing Participants Already in a Meeting Before Joining\nPurpose: Provide a standardized process for helping customers check which participants have already joined a Zoom meeting before they enter the session.\nApplies To: Eligible Pro, Business, Enterprise, and Education accounts using the Zoom Workplace desktop app with Zoom Calendar enabled and a calendar service integrated with Zoom.\nWhen To Use: When a customer wants to see who is already in a scheduled Zoom meeting before joining, so they can confirm attendance or prepare before entering.\nRequirements\nEligible Zoom account: Pro, Business, Enterprise, or Education.\nZoom Workplace desktop app.\nZoom Calendar enabled.\nA supported calendar service integrated with Zoom.\nEnabling and using Zoom Calendar\nIntegrating a calendar service with Zoom\nIntroduction\nZoom allows eligible customers to see which participants have already joined a scheduled meeting before entering the session. This can help customers confirm attendance and prepare before joining. This process provides step-by-step guidance for viewing participants before joining a meeting using the Zoom Workplace desktop app with Zoom Calendar enabled and an integrated calendar service.\nProcess / Step-by-Step Guide\nA. View Participants from the Home Tab\nSign in to the Zoom Workplace desktop app.\nClick the Home tab.\nLocate or search for the meeting you want to review.\nOpen or view the meeting details.\nUnder Host, look for the initials or profile pictures of participants who have already joined the meeting.\nSample Script\n“Please sign in to the Zoom Workplace app and open the Home tab. Find the meeting you’re about to join. In the meeting details, look under Host for the initials or profile pictures of participants who are already in the meeting.”\nB. View Participants from the Calendar Tab\nSign in to the Zoom Workplace desktop app.\nClick the Calendar tab.\nSelect the meeting you want to review.\nIn the calendar event card, locate the Invitees section.\nClick the forward arrow next to Invitees to view the participants who have already joined.\nSample Script\n“You can also check from the Calendar tab. Please select the meeting, then look for the Invitees section in the event card. Click the forward arrow to view the participants who have already joined.”\nC. View Participants Using Agenda / Calendar View - Option 1\nSign in to the Zoom Workplace desktop app.\nClick the Calendar tab.\nIn the upper-right corner, click the Calendar side panel button.\nSelect the meeting you want to review.\nIn the calendar event card, look for the initials or profile pictures of participants who have already joined.\nSample Script\n“From the Calendar tab, please open the Calendar side panel in the upper-right corner and select the meeting. The event card should show the initials or profile pictures of participants who are already in the meeting.”\nD. View Participants Using Agenda / Calendar View - Option 2\nSign in to the Zoom Workplace desktop app.\nClick the Calendar tab.\nIn the upper-right corner, open the calendar view drop-down menu and select Day.\nThe calendar changes to Day view or a time-grid view.\nSelect the meeting you want to review.\nIn the calendar event card, look under Host for the initials or profile pictures of participants who have already joined.\nSample Script\n“Another option is to open the Calendar tab and switch the calendar view to Day. Select the meeting, then look under Host in the event card for the participants who have already joined.”\nIf Participant Information Is Not Displayed\nConfirm that the customer is using an eligible Pro, Business, Enterprise, or Education account.\nConfirm that Zoom Calendar is enabled.\nConfirm that a calendar service is integrated with Zoom.\nMake sure the correct scheduled meeting is selected.\nIf the requirements are met but participant information is still unavailable, follow the organization's escalation process or review the Zoom Calendar configuration.\nSample Script\n“If you don’t see the participant information, let’s confirm that Zoom Calendar is enabled and that your calendar service is connected to Zoom. We’ll also make sure you’re viewing the correct scheduled meeting.”\nImportant Reminder\nThis feature allows the customer to check participants who have already joined before entering the meeting. The available display may show participant initials, profile pictures, or a participant list depending on the view being used.\nSample Script\n“The participant display can look slightly different depending on which Zoom Calendar view you’re using. You may see initials, profile pictures, or a list of participants who are already in the meeting.”\nQuick Guide / Remember the Process\nSIGN IN → FIND MEETING → OPEN DETAILS → VIEW PARTICIPANTS → JOIN\nSIGN IN - to the Zoom Workplace desktop app.\nFIND MEETING - from Home or Calendar.\nOPEN DETAILS - for the scheduled meeting.\nVIEW PARTICIPANTS - under Host, Invitees, or the calendar event card.\nJOIN - the meeting when ready.\nReference\nPrimary Reference: Zoom Support - Viewing participants already in a meeting before joining \nSupporting Zoom resources:\nZoom Calendar\nCalendar service integration with Zoom","images":["assets/rendered/viewing-participants-already-in-a-meeting-before-joining-page-1.png","assets/rendered/viewing-participants-already-in-a-meeting-before-joining-page-2.png","assets/rendered/viewing-participants-already-in-a-meeting-before-joining-page-3.png","assets/rendered/viewing-participants-already-in-a-meeting-before-joining-page-4.png","assets/rendered/viewing-participants-already-in-a-meeting-before-joining-page-5.png"],"visualReferences":[]},{"id":"waiting-for-the-host-to-start-a-meeting-or-webinar","title":"Waiting for the Host to Start a Meeting or Webinar","category":"join","purpose":"To guide agents in assisting an arbitrator who has successfully connected to Zoom but receives a message indicating that the meeting or webinar has not yet been started by the host, or that the session is scheduled for a different date or time.","keywords":"Waiting for the Host to Start a Meeting or Webinar join","steps":["- Identify the Message on the Screen","- If the Message Says “Please Wait for the Host to Start”","- If the Message Shows a Scheduled Date and Time","- Confirm Whether the Information Appears Correct","- Refer the Arbitrator When Host Action Is Required","- Document the Interaction"],"referral":"Refer when the issue requires host, account, admin, network, operating-system, or device access beyond basic Zoom guidance.","text":"Process Title: Waiting for the Host to Start a Zoom Meeting or Webinar\nPurpose: To guide agents in assisting an arbitrator who has successfully connected to Zoom but receives a message indicating that the meeting or webinar has not yet been started by the host, or that the session is scheduled for a different date or time.\nApplies To: Agents providing basic Zoom joining and meeting-readiness assistance to arbitrators using the Zoom desktop app, Zoom Web App, or Zoom mobile app.\nWhen To Use: Use this process when an arbitrator attempts to join a Zoom meeting or webinar and sees a message such as “Please wait for the host to start this meeting/webinar” or a notice showing that the meeting or webinar is scheduled for a particular date and time.\nIntroduction\nAn arbitrator may sometimes open the correct Zoom meeting link and successfully connect to Zoom but still be unable to enter the session. This does not automatically mean there is a technical problem. If the host has not started the meeting and participants are not permitted to join before the host, Zoom will display a message asking the participant to wait. For webinars, the host may not have started the webinar yet, or the webinar may still be in practice mode and not broadcasting to attendees.\nZoom may also display the scheduled date and time when the participant is attempting to join a session that is scheduled for another day or time. Agents should help the arbitrator understand the message, verify the meeting information they received, and avoid unnecessary troubleshooting when Zoom has already confirmed a successful connection.\nKey point: A “waiting for host” message generally means the participant has connected to Zoom successfully; the session itself has not yet been made available by the host.\nProcess / Step-by-Step Guide\nStep 1 - Identify the Message on the Screen\nAsk the arbitrator to read or describe the message displayed in Zoom.\nDetermine whether the message says to wait for the host or displays a scheduled meeting/webinar date and time.\nDo not begin audio, video, network, or application troubleshooting unless another technical symptom is present.\nSample Script\n“Please read the message you see on the Zoom screen. Does it say that you are waiting for the host to start the meeting, or does it show a scheduled date and time?”\nStep 2 - If the Message Says “Please Wait for the Host to Start”\nExplain that the arbitrator has successfully connected to Zoom.\nExplain that the host has not yet started the meeting or webinar.\nFor a webinar, the host may not have started the webinar or may still be in practice mode and not yet broadcasting.\nAdvise the arbitrator to remain on the waiting screen until the host starts the session.\nDo not instruct the arbitrator to repeatedly leave and rejoin unless there is another identified issue.\nSample Script\n“You have successfully connected to Zoom. This message means the host has not started the session yet. Please remain on this screen, and the meeting or webinar should become available when the host starts it.”\nStep 3 - If the Message Shows a Scheduled Date and Time\nAsk the arbitrator to review the displayed date and start time.\nRemind the arbitrator to check the timezone shown or provided in the meeting information.\nCompare the displayed information with the invitation the arbitrator received, without changing or independently interpreting the proceeding schedule.\nIf the session is scheduled for another day or time, explain that Zoom is showing the scheduled session information.\nSample Script\n“Zoom is showing the date and time currently scheduled for this session. Please compare that information with your meeting invitation, including the timezone.”\nStep 4 - Confirm Whether the Information Appears Correct\nIf the date/time and waiting message are expected, advise the arbitrator to wait for the host.\nIf the arbitrator believes the host should already have started the session, or believes the date/time is incorrect, do not attempt to change the meeting information.\nProceed to the referral step.\nSample Script\n“If this matches your invitation and the meeting has not started yet, please remain connected and wait for the host. If you believe the meeting should already be in progress, I can explain who you should contact next.”\nStep 5 - Refer the Arbitrator When Host Action Is Required\nThe support agent cannot start the meeting, admit the participant, change the scheduled time, or contact the host on the arbitrator’s behalf.\nIf the arbitrator believes the message is incorrect, the host is late, or the meeting information does not match the invitation, direct the arbitrator to the meeting host, organizer, or designated contact listed in the invitation.\nDo not promise when the host will start the session.\nSample Script\n“Since the meeting has not been started by the host, we are unable to open or start the session from our side. If you believe the meeting should already be in progress, please contact the meeting host, organizer, or the designated contact listed in your invitation.”\nStep 6 - Document the Interaction\nDocument the message reported by the arbitrator.\nNote whether the date/time was reviewed.\nNote whether the arbitrator was advised to wait or to contact the meeting host/organizer.\nRecord the appropriate call outcome.\nSample Script\n“I’ll document that we confirmed your connection to Zoom and reviewed the message displayed. Please follow the meeting information provided by the organizer, and contact the designated meeting contact if you need clarification about the session start time.”\nDecision Guide\nImportant Agent Reminders\nA waiting-for-host message is not, by itself, evidence of a Zoom connection failure.\nDo not tell the arbitrator to reinstall Zoom, restart the device, or troubleshoot audio/video solely because this message appears.\nDo not tell the arbitrator that the host is late or make assumptions about why the session has not started.\nDo not change or interpret the official arbitration schedule.\nDo not contact the host, organizer, administrator, or IT team on the arbitrator’s behalf unless a separate client-approved process explicitly allows it.\nDo not tell the arbitrator to use a different meeting link unless the organizer has provided one.\nIf the arbitrator is unsure about the schedule, direct them to the official meeting invitation and the designated organizer/contact.\nQuick Guide / Remember the Process\nREAD MESSAGE  →  EXPLAIN  →  CHECK DATE/TIME  →  WAIT OR REFER  →  DOCUMENT\nReference\nZoom Support: Waiting for the host to start meeting/webinar. Related setting: Allow participants to join anytime. Source content provided for this process document. Agents should use current official Zoom Support guidance and the client-approved support scope when assisting arbitrators.","images":["assets/rendered/waiting-for-the-host-to-start-a-meeting-or-webinar-page-1.png","assets/rendered/waiting-for-the-host-to-start-a-meeting-or-webinar-page-2.png","assets/rendered/waiting-for-the-host-to-start-a-meeting-or-webinar-page-3.png","assets/rendered/waiting-for-the-host-to-start-a-meeting-or-webinar-page-4.png","assets/rendered/waiting-for-the-host-to-start-a-meeting-or-webinar-page-5.png"],"visualReferences":[{"title":"Recognize the waiting-for-host screen","image":"assets/visual-references/waiting-for-host.png","documentStep":"Waiting for the Host — Identify the message and test audio while waiting","instruction":"Confirm that the screen says “Waiting for the host to start the meeting.” The customer may use Test speaker and mic, but only the host can start the session.","expectedResult":"The customer understands that their Zoom app is connected and they must wait for the host or contact the host if the meeting should already be open.","platform":"Desktop — Zoom Workplace","sourceName":"UTokyo utelecon","sourceUrl":"https://utelecon.adm.u-tokyo.ac.jp/en/zoom/join/","status":"Real Zoom Workplace screen matched to process document"}]},{"id":"zoom-audio-troubleshooting","title":"Zoom Audio Troubleshooting","category":"audio","purpose":"Provide a standard process for resolving common Zoom audio, microphone, speaker, and headset issues.","keywords":"Zoom Audio Troubleshooting audio","steps":["Check Your Audio Device","Check Your Headphones","Test Your Audio in Zoom","Check Zoom's Audio Settings","Check Microphone Permissions","Check Security Software","Check Audio Drivers","Perform General Troubleshooting"],"referral":"Refer when the issue requires host, account, admin, network, operating-system, or device access beyond basic Zoom guidance.","text":"Process Title: Zoom Audio Troubleshooting\nPurpose: Provide a standard process for resolving common Zoom audio, microphone, speaker, and headset issues.\nApplies To: Zoom Desktop App and Zoom Mobile App\nWhen to Use: When a user cannot hear others, cannot be heard, or experiences audio problems before or during a Zoom meeting.\nIntroduction\nAudio issues can affect communication before or during a Zoom meeting, making it difficult for users to hear others or be heard clearly. These problems may involve the microphone, speakers, headset, audio settings, or device connection. This process provides step-by-step guidance for identifying and resolving common Zoom audio issues so users can communicate effectively during their meeting. \nProcess / Step-by-Step Guide:\n1. Check Your Audio Device\nStart with the physical device.\nMake sure your microphone, speakers, headset, or headphones are properly connected.\nCheck that the device is not muted and the volume is turned up.\nUnplug the device and plug it back in.\nFor Bluetooth devices, confirm they are turned on and connected.\nIf using a USB audio device, confirm that it is a Zoom-supported USB device. \nClose other applications that may be using your microphone.\nNote: USB devices that are not on Zoom's supported-device list may still work. However, Zoom recommends supported USB devices for the best experience because they are less likely to encounter audio issues.\nView Zoom's supported USB devices\nTip: Always check the device first before changing Zoom settings.\nSample Script:\n“Let’s first check your audio device. Please make sure your microphone, speakers, or headset are connected, turned on, and not muted. If possible, unplug the device and reconnect it. If you’re using Bluetooth, please confirm that it is connected to the correct device.”\n2. Check Your Headphones\nIf you are using a headset or headphones:\nBluetooth: Make sure the headset is connected to the correct computer or mobile device.\nWired: Make sure it is fully plugged into the correct port.\nOn Windows, check the Volume Mixer and make sure Zoom isn't muted. Adjusting the volume of a Zoom meeting\nSample Script:\n“Next, let’s check your headset connection. If it’s Bluetooth, please confirm that it’s connected to the correct device. If it’s wired, make sure it’s fully plugged in. If you’re on Windows, we’ll also check that Zoom isn’t muted in the Volume Mixer.”\n3. Test Your Audio in Zoom\nNext, determine whether Zoom can detect your equipment.\nOpen Zoom → Settings → Audio.\nTest your microphone by speaking and checking whether Zoom detects your voice.\nRecord or test your microphone and listen to the playback.\nTest your speaker/headphones and confirm that you can hear the test sound.\nTesting your audio settings for Zoom meetings\nTip: Testing helps identify whether the problem is with the microphone, speaker, or another setting.\n\tSample Script:\n“Now let’s test your audio in Zoom. Please open Settings, then Audio. We’ll test your speaker first and then your microphone to confirm whether Zoom can detect and play audio correctly.”\n4. Check Zoom's Audio Settings\nIf the equipment works but Zoom audio still doesn't:\nMake sure the correct microphone is selected. Check audio settings\nMake sure the correct speaker/headphones are selected.\nCheck the microphone input level.\nCheck and adjust your device's volume. Audio Volume \nFor example, if your laptop microphone and USB headset are both available, make sure Zoom is using the device you actually want to use.\n\tSample Script:\n“Your device is being detected, so let’s check which audio devices Zoom is using. Please confirm that the correct microphone and speaker or headset are selected, and make sure the volume and microphone levels are set appropriately.”\n5. Check Microphone Permissions\nSometimes the device works, but the operating system is blocking Zoom from accessing it.\nGo to your device's Privacy/Permission Settings (Check Privacy Settings) and confirm that:\nMicrophone Access = ON\nZoom Access = Allowed\nSample Script:\n“Next, let’s check your device permissions. Please open your Privacy or Permission Settings and make sure microphone access is turned on and Zoom is allowed to use the microphone.”\n6. Check Security Software\nIf Zoom still cannot access your microphone, check your antivirus or security software.\nSome security programs can block applications from accessing microphones. Make sure Zoom has permission to use the microphone.\nSample Script:\n“If Zoom still can’t access the microphone, let’s check whether your antivirus or security software is blocking it. Please make sure Zoom has permission to use your microphone.”\n7. Check Audio Drivers\nIf the problem continues, especially on a computer:\nCheck for audio driver updates.\nUpdate the driver if one is available.\nIf necessary, uninstall and reinstall the audio driver.\nNote: This is a more advanced troubleshooting step and may require IT support depending on company policy.\nSample Script:\n“The next step is to check your audio drivers. We’ll verify whether an update is available. If the driver needs to be reinstalled or you don’t have permission to make the change, we may need to involve your IT support team.”\n8. Perform General Troubleshooting\nIf none of the previous steps resolve the issue:\nUpdate Zoom to the latest version. Updating Zoom to the latest version\nRestart Zoom.\nRestart the computer or mobile device.\nCheck for operating system updates.\nIf using Zoom through a browser, clear the browser's cache and cookies and restart it. Clearing Zoom cache and cookies\nAs a final step, uninstall and reinstall Zoom.\nEasy Troubleshooting Flow for Training\nSample Script:\n“Since the audio issue is still happening, let’s complete some general troubleshooting. We’ll make sure Zoom and your operating system are updated, restart Zoom and your device, and if needed, reinstall Zoom.”\nREMEMBER THE PROCESS AS :\nCHECK → TEST → SELECT → ALLOW → UPDATE → RESTART\nCHECK — the physical audio device, headset connection, mute status, and volume.\nTEST — the microphone and speaker in Zoom.\nSELECT — the correct microphone and speaker/headset in Zoom's Audio Settings.\nALLOW — Zoom to access the microphone through device privacy and security settings.\nUPDATE — Zoom, the operating system, and audio drivers when needed.\nRESTART — Zoom or the device; reinstall Zoom if the issue remains unresolved.\nReference\nZoom Support — Troubleshooting audio issues","images":["assets/rendered/zoom-audio-troubleshooting-page-1.png","assets/rendered/zoom-audio-troubleshooting-page-2.png","assets/rendered/zoom-audio-troubleshooting-page-3.png","assets/rendered/zoom-audio-troubleshooting-page-4.png"],"visualReferences":[{"title":"Check the selected speaker and microphone","image":"assets/visual-references/audio-settings.png","documentStep":"Audio Troubleshooting — Open Audio Settings and test each device","instruction":"Confirm the correct Speaker and Microphone selections, run the built-in tests, and watch the input level while the customer speaks.","expectedResult":"The speaker test is audible and the microphone input meter responds.","platform":"Desktop — Zoom audio settings","sourceName":"The Royal Conservatory of Music","sourceUrl":"https://www.rcmusic.com/learning/examinations/remote-exams/help-with-remote-practical-exams/remote-examination-zoom-guide","status":"Real Zoom settings screen matched to process document"}]},{"id":"zoom-basic-support-boundaries-decision-path-referral-process","title":"Zoom Basic Support Boundaries, Decision Path & Referral Process","category":"support","purpose":"To provide agents with a consistent process for handling Zoom-related questions from arbitrators, determining whether an issue is within the company’s basic support scope, completing appropriate troubleshooting, and directing the arbitrator to the correct external contact when additional access or authority is required.","keywords":"Zoom Basic Support Boundaries, Decision Path & Referral Process support","steps":["— Identify the Exact Issue","— Determine Whether the Issue Is Within Basic Support Scope","— Perform Approved Basic Troubleshooting","— Test and Confirm the Result","— Recognize the Roadblock and Stop at the Support Boundary","— Identify Who Owns the Next Step","— Document and Close"],"referral":"Refer when the issue requires host, account, admin, network, operating-system, or device access beyond basic Zoom guidance.","text":"Process Title: Zoom Basic Support Boundaries, Decision Path, and Referral for Additional Assistance\nPurpose: To provide agents with a consistent process for handling Zoom-related questions from arbitrators, determining whether an issue is within the company’s basic support scope, completing appropriate troubleshooting, and directing the arbitrator to the correct external contact when additional access or authority is required.\nApplies To: Agents providing basic Zoom setup, navigation, readiness, and troubleshooting assistance to arbitrators using the Zoom desktop app, Zoom Web App, or Zoom mobile app.\nWhen To Use: Use this process whenever an arbitrator contacts the support team for Zoom assistance and the agent must determine whether to continue basic troubleshooting, close the interaction as resolved, or direct the arbitrator to another appropriate support contact.\nIntroduction\nOur role is to provide arbitrators with basic Zoom assistance, including setup, navigation, pre-meeting readiness, and approved troubleshooting. Our agents do not administer the arbitrator’s Zoom account, control the meeting, change organization-level settings, contact the arbitrator’s administrator or IT team on their behalf, or make decisions about an arbitration proceeding.\nWhen an issue reaches a point that requires host action, administrative access, organization-managed device or network support, or a proceeding-related decision, the agent should stop troubleshooting, explain the support boundary clearly, and guide the arbitrator on who they should contact next.\nKey principle: We do not need to fix every issue. We need to resolve what is within our approved scope and recognize when the issue has moved outside that scope.\nScope of Support\nWhat Agents CAN Assist With\nLocating and explaining basic Zoom meeting controls.\nJoining a meeting using an invitation link, Meeting ID, and passcode when available.\nExplaining the Waiting Room and basic joining experience.\nTesting and selecting the correct microphone, speaker, and camera in Zoom.\nChecking Mute/Unmute and Start/Stop Video.\nBasic audio and video troubleshooting using approved steps.\nLocating Participants, Chat, meeting views, reactions/non-verbal feedback, and screen sharing controls.\nBasic guidance for Windows, macOS, Linux, Zoom Web App, Android, and iOS when covered by the support process.\nHelping the arbitrator identify what they see on screen and confirming whether a troubleshooting step worked.\nWhat Agents MUST NOT Do\nContact the arbitrator’s Zoom administrator, IT department, meeting host, or other organization on the arbitrator’s behalf unless a separate client-approved process explicitly authorizes it.\nChange organization-level, account-level, or administrator-controlled Zoom settings.\nAttempt to bypass host restrictions or meeting permissions.\nMake changes to managed devices, firewalls, VPNs, security software, or organization-controlled permissions outside approved basic troubleshooting.\nMake decisions about whether an arbitration proceeding should continue, pause, be postponed, or be rescheduled.\nInterpret legal, confidentiality, recording, or proceeding rules.\nPromise that another party or Zoom Support will resolve the issue within a specific timeframe.\nProcess / Step-by-Step Guide\nStep 1 — Identify the Exact Issue\nAsk the arbitrator to describe what they are trying to do and what is happening instead.\nClarify the specific issue or error the arbitrator is experiencing before providing troubleshooting instructions.\nWhen relevant, identify the device/platform: Windows, macOS, Linux, Web App, Android, or iOS.\nAsk for the exact wording of any error message when available.\nSample Script\n“I’ll help you check the basic Zoom setup. First, can you tell me what you’re trying to do and what happens when you try?”\nStep 2 — Determine Whether the Issue Is Within Basic Support Scope\nDecide whether the concern involves a basic Zoom control, setup step, or approved troubleshooting procedure.\nIf it is within scope, continue to Step 3.\nIf it clearly requires host action, administrative access, organization-managed IT support, or a proceeding decision, do not perform unnecessary troubleshooting. Move to Step 5.\nSample Script\n“Based on what you’re seeing, I’ll first check the basic Zoom settings and controls that we can assist you with.”\nStep 3 — Perform Approved Basic Troubleshooting\nUse the relevant approved process document for the issue.\nProvide one instruction at a time.\nUse the communication method: Locate → Describe → Guide → Confirm.\nDo not assume that all Zoom interfaces look the same.\nDo not change settings outside the approved support scope.\nSample Script\n“I’ll guide you one step at a time. Please let me know when you see the option I describe before we continue.”\nStep 4 — Test and Confirm the Result\nAfter each meaningful troubleshooting action, verify whether the issue is resolved.\nDo not assume that changing a setting fixed the problem.\nIf resolved, document the resolution and close the interaction.\nIf not resolved and additional approved steps remain, continue troubleshooting.\nIf all approved steps have been completed, move to Step 5.\nSample Script\n“Let’s check whether that resolved the issue. Can you confirm if you’re now able to use the feature as expected?”\nStep 5 — Recognize the Roadblock and Stop at the Support Boundary\nStop troubleshooting when the remaining action requires access, authority, permissions, hardware/network support, or a proceeding decision outside the agent’s scope.\nExplain what has already been checked.\nAvoid saying that the agent will escalate the issue if the company does not actually contact the next support party.\nUse language such as “additional assistance” or “please contact” instead.\nSample Script\n“We’ve completed the basic troubleshooting steps available to us, and the issue is still occurring. The next step requires assistance outside the level of access we provide.”\nStep 6 — Identify Who Owns the Next Step\nUse the referral matrix in this document to determine the appropriate contact.\nTell the arbitrator why that contact is appropriate.\nDo not guess a person’s name, phone number, email address, or support channel.\nIf the arbitrator does not know who the contact is, direct them to the meeting invitation, organization help desk information, or other official information they already have\nSample Script\n“The next step will need to be handled by the appropriate support contact. I’ll explain who you should reach out to based on the type of issue we identified.”\nStep 7 — Document and Close\nDocument the reason for contact, troubleshooting completed, current status, recommended next contact, and call outcome.\nDo not document assumptions as facts.\nIf the issue is unresolved, clearly note that the arbitrator was advised to contact the appropriate party for additional assistance.\nSample Script\n“I’ll document the troubleshooting we completed today and the next step we discussed. Is there anything else within the basic Zoom setup or controls that I can clarify before we end the call?”\nReferral / Roadblock Matrix\nImmediate Stop / Refer Triggers\nAgents should stop basic troubleshooting and refer the arbitrator when any of the following is identified:\nThe remaining step requires host action or meeting-owner permission.\nThe remaining step requires Zoom administrator, account owner, license, or organization-level access.\nThe device, operating system, network, VPN, firewall, or security controls require support beyond the approved basic process.\nThe microphone, speaker, or camera is not recognized by the device itself after basic connection checks.\nAll approved troubleshooting steps have been completed and the issue remains unresolved.\nThe arbitrator asks the agent to make a decision about the arbitration proceeding.\nThe question involves authorization to record, share confidential information, use AI features, or another proceeding/privacy rule.\nContinuing troubleshooting would require the agent to guess, bypass controls, or perform an unauthorized change.\nWhat NOT to Say\nQuick Guide / Remember the Process\nIDENTIFY  →  CHECK SCOPE  →  TROUBLESHOOT  →  TEST  →  RESOLVE OR REFER  →  DOCUMENT\nCall Documentation\nUse the following fields when documenting the interaction:\nArbitrator Name: ______________________________________________\nReason for Contact: ___________________________________________\nTroubleshooting Completed: ____________________________________\nResolution / Next Steps: ______________________________________\nRecommended Contact (if referred): _____________________________\nCall Outcome:\n☐ Resolved\n☐ Partially Resolved\n☐ Escalated / Referred for Additional Assistance\n☐ Follow-Up Required\n☐ No Issue Found / General Assistance\nSample Closing Scripts\nChecking if Arbitrator needs further assistance\n“Your Zoom issue has been resolved. Before we end the call, is there anything else regarding the basic Zoom setup or meeting controls that you would like me to clarify?”\nRefer to Organization’s IT\n“We’ve completed the basic troubleshooting steps available to us, but the issue is still occurring. Please contact your organization’s IT or technical support team for additional assistance.”\nRefer to the Meeting Host or Organizer\n“Your Zoom setup appears to be working, but the next step is controlled by the meeting host or organizer. Please contact the host or the contact listed in your meeting invitation for further assistance.”\nDecision about arbitration proceeding\n“We can assist with the technical Zoom steps, but we’re unable to make decisions regarding the arbitration proceeding. Please contact the designated arbitration contact or meeting organizer for guidance.”\nReference\nUse the client-approved Zoom process documents and current official Zoom Support documentation for the specific basic setup or troubleshooting procedure being performed. Follow the client’s defined support scope and referral contacts when available. Agents should not invent or assume administrative contacts, permissions, or proceeding rules.","images":["assets/rendered/zoom-basic-support-boundaries-decision-path-referral-process-page-1.png","assets/rendered/zoom-basic-support-boundaries-decision-path-referral-process-page-2.png","assets/rendered/zoom-basic-support-boundaries-decision-path-referral-process-page-3.png","assets/rendered/zoom-basic-support-boundaries-decision-path-referral-process-page-4.png","assets/rendered/zoom-basic-support-boundaries-decision-path-referral-process-page-5.png","assets/rendered/zoom-basic-support-boundaries-decision-path-referral-process-page-6.png","assets/rendered/zoom-basic-support-boundaries-decision-path-referral-process-page-7.png","assets/rendered/zoom-basic-support-boundaries-decision-path-referral-process-page-8.png","assets/rendered/zoom-basic-support-boundaries-decision-path-referral-process-page-9.png"],"visualReferences":[]},{"id":"zoom-camera-troubleshooting-during-a-meeting","title":"Zoom Camera Troubleshooting During a Meeting","category":"video","purpose":"Provide a standard process for resolving common camera issues when a customer's video is not showing or the correct camera is not being used during a Zoom meeting.","keywords":"Zoom Camera Troubleshooting During a Meeting video","steps":["Select a Different Camera","Ensure That Zoom Can Access Your Camera","Uninstall and Reinstall Zoom"],"referral":"Refer when the issue requires host, account, admin, network, operating-system, or device access beyond basic Zoom guidance.","text":"Process Title:\tZoom Camera Troubleshooting During a Meeting\nPurpose: \tProvide a standard process for resolving common camera issues when a customer's video is not showing or the correct camera is not being used during a Zoom meeting.\nApplies To: \tZoom Workplace Desktop App and Zoom Workplace Mobile App; camera access and device/security settings may vary by operating system.\nWhen To Use: \tWhen a customer's camera or video is not showing during a Zoom meeting, Zoom cannot access the camera, or the wrong camera is selected.\nIntroduction\nCamera issues during a Zoom meeting can prevent participants from being seen clearly or using their video as expected. These issues may be caused by an incorrect camera selection, device permissions, or problems with the Zoom application. This process will guide you through the basic troubleshooting steps to identify and resolve common camera issues during a meeting. \nProcess / Step-by-Step Guide\nBefore Troubleshooting\nConfirm that the issue is related to the camera/video not displaying during the meeting. If the customer is experiencing video-related crashes, use Zoom's video-crash troubleshooting process instead. The customer may also test the camera before joining a meeting.\nTesting your video before a meeting\nTroubleshooting video crashes\nSample Script\n“I’ll help you check why your camera isn’t displaying in Zoom. We’ll start by checking which camera Zoom is using, then confirm that Zoom has permission to access it. If the issue continues, we’ll reinstall Zoom as a final step.”\n1. Select a Different Camera\nWhile in the meeting, click the arrow next to Start / Stop Video.\nSelect Video Settings.\nUnder Camera, open the drop-down menu.\nIf more than one camera is available, select another camera and check whether the video appears.\nAlternatively, click the arrow next to Start / Stop Video and choose a camera under Select a Camera.\nNote: Your device may have more than one camera. Selecting another available camera can resolve the issue if Zoom is using the wrong camera.\nSample Script\n“Let’s first make sure Zoom is using the correct camera. Please click the arrow next to Start or Stop Video, then open Video Settings. Under Camera, select another available camera and check whether your video appears.”\n2. Ensure That Zoom Can Access Your Camera\nCheck the device's camera privacy or permission settings.\nMake sure camera access is enabled for the device.\nConfirm that Zoom is allowed to access the camera.\nCheck security software settings if applicable and make sure they are not preventing Zoom from accessing the camera.\nNote: A device privacy setting or security application may block Zoom from accessing the camera even when the camera itself is working.\nSample Script\n“If changing the camera didn’t resolve the issue, let’s check your camera permissions. Please make sure camera access is turned on for your device and that Zoom is allowed to use the camera. We’ll also check whether any security software is blocking access.”\n3. Uninstall and Reinstall Zoom\nCompletely uninstall Zoom from the device.\nGo to the official Zoom Download Center.\nDownload and install the latest version of the Zoom Workplace app.\nOpen Zoom after installation.\nTest the camera again and check whether the issue continues.\nNote: Use this step after the previous camera-selection and access checks have not resolved the issue.\nUninstall Zoom\nZoom Download Center\nSample Script\n“Since the camera issue is still happening, the next step is to reinstall Zoom. Please uninstall Zoom completely, download the latest version from the official Zoom Download Center, reinstall it, and then test your camera again.”\nQuick Guide / Remember the Process\nSELECT → ALLOW → REINSTALL → TEST\nSELECT — the correct camera in Zoom.\nALLOW — Zoom to access the camera through device privacy and security settings.\nREINSTALL — the latest version of Zoom if the issue remains unresolved.\nTEST — the camera again to confirm that the video is working.\nReference\nPrimary Reference: Zoom Support — Troubleshooting camera issues during a meeting\nSupporting Zoom resources:\nTroubleshooting video crashes\nTesting your video\nUninstalling Zoom\nZoom Download Center","images":["assets/rendered/zoom-camera-troubleshooting-during-a-meeting-page-1.png","assets/rendered/zoom-camera-troubleshooting-during-a-meeting-page-2.png","assets/rendered/zoom-camera-troubleshooting-during-a-meeting-page-3.png"],"visualReferences":[{"title":"Confirm the selected camera","image":"assets/visual-references/video-settings.png","documentStep":"Camera Troubleshooting — Open Video settings and select the correct camera","instruction":"Check that the intended camera is selected and that a preview appears. Then return to the meeting and use Start Video.","expectedResult":"The preview displays the camera feed and the customer can start video in the meeting.","platform":"Desktop — Zoom video settings","sourceName":"Prodigy EMS","sourceUrl":"https://marketing.prodigyems.com/blog-posts/how-to-maximize-your-zoom-recordings","status":"Real Zoom settings screen matched to process document"}]},{"id":"zoom-error-unable-to-establish-secure-connection-to-zoom","title":"Zoom Error  “Unable to Establish Secure Connection to Zoom”","category":"join","purpose":"To guide agents through the approved basic troubleshooting steps when an arbitrator using a Mac repeatedly receives the Zoom error message “Unable to establish secure connection to Zoom,” and to help agents recognize when the issue has reached the company’s support boundary.","keywords":"Zoom Error  “Unable to Establish Secure Connection to Zoom” join","steps":["— Confirm the Error and Platform","— Update Zoom if the App Can Be Opened","— Completely Uninstall Zoom","— Reinstall Zoom","— Check for Calendar and Contact Integration","— Combined Troubleshooting Method","— Check macOS Updates and Restart","— Confirm the Result"],"referral":"Refer when the issue requires host, account, admin, network, operating-system, or device access beyond basic Zoom guidance.","text":"Process Title: Troubleshooting Zoom Error Message “Unable to Establish Secure Connection to Zoom”\nPurpose: To guide agents through the approved basic troubleshooting steps when an arbitrator using a Mac repeatedly receives the Zoom error message “Unable to establish secure connection to Zoom,” and to help agents recognize when the issue has reached the company’s support boundary.\nApplies To: Agents providing basic Zoom assistance to arbitrators who encounter this error while using the Zoom desktop app on macOS. The source material identifies this as a known error affecting some macOS users.\nWhen To Use: Use this process when an arbitrator reports that the Zoom desktop app repeatedly displays the message “Unable to establish secure connection to Zoom,” particularly on a Mac, and basic assistance is needed to restore the Zoom connection.\nIntroduction\nThe “Unable to establish secure connection to Zoom” message means the Zoom desktop app is unable to complete the secure connection it needs to operate normally. Zoom identifies this as a known error affecting some macOS users. The recommended troubleshooting focuses on removing cached application components through a complete uninstall, reinstalling or updating Zoom, checking macOS updates, and—when applicable—addressing an existing Calendar and Contact Integration.\nBecause your support team provides basic Zoom assistance only, agents should begin with steps that can be completed safely by the arbitrator. Actions involving an organization-managed calendar/contact integration, administrator-controlled settings, or restrictions that the arbitrator is not authorized to change should be referred to the arbitrator’s own IT or Zoom administrator rather than changed by the agent.\nImportant: Before uninstalling Zoom or removing an integration, explain what the step will do and confirm that the arbitrator is comfortable and authorized to proceed.\nProcess / Step-by-Step Guide\nStep 1 — Confirm the Error and Platform\nAsk the arbitrator to confirm the exact error message.\nConfirm that the arbitrator is using macOS and the Zoom desktop app.\nIf the message is different, use the process for that specific error instead of assuming the same resolution.\nIf the device is organization-managed and the arbitrator cannot make application or system changes, move to the referral guidance.\nSample Script\n“To make sure we use the correct troubleshooting steps, can you confirm that the message says “Unable to establish secure connection to Zoom” and that you are using the Zoom desktop app on a Mac?”\nStep 2 — Update Zoom if the App Can Be Opened\nIf Zoom can still be opened and an update can be completed, check for and install the latest available Zoom version before moving to more disruptive steps.\nOpen the Zoom desktop app.\nCheck for an available Zoom update.\nInstall the latest version if available.\nRestart Zoom and check whether the error still appears.\nIf the error remains, continue to Step 3.\nSample Script\n“Let’s first make sure Zoom is up to date. If an update is available, please install it, reopen Zoom, and let me know whether the same secure connection message appears.”\nStep 3 — Completely Uninstall Zoom\nA complete uninstall removes the Zoom application and its app cache/components before reinstalling a clean copy.\nOpen the Zoom desktop app.\nAt the top of the Mac screen, select zoom.us.\nSelect Uninstall Zoom.\nSelect OK to confirm uninstalling the Zoom desktop application and its components.\nRestart the Mac.\nSample Script\n“The next Zoom-recommended step is to completely uninstall the Zoom desktop app, restart your Mac, and then install a fresh copy. This removes the existing Zoom app components and cache. Are you comfortable proceeding with that step?”\nStep 4 — Reinstall Zoom\nAfter the Mac restarts, go to the official Zoom Download Center.\nDownload the current Zoom Workplace desktop app for macOS.\nInstall Zoom.\nOpen the app and check whether the secure connection error returns.\nSample Script\n“Now that the Mac has restarted, please reinstall Zoom from the official Zoom Download Center. Once it is installed, open Zoom and let me know whether the same error appears.”\nStep 5 — Check for Calendar and Contact Integration\nZoom also recommends deleting and reconfiguring an existing Calendar and Contact Integration as a troubleshooting method. This step may involve the arbitrator’s organization or company-standard configuration.\nAsk whether the arbitrator uses a Calendar and Contact Integration with Zoom.\nDo not delete or reconfigure an organization-managed integration unless the arbitrator is authorized to do so and the applicable company procedure is known.\nIf the integration is organization-managed or the arbitrator is unsure, stop and advise them to contact their IT team or Zoom administrator.\nIf the arbitrator is authorized to manage the integration, the Zoom steps are: sign in to the Zoom web portal → Profile → Others → Calendar and Contact Integration → locate the existing integration → Delete → confirm Delete → reconfigure the integration according to the organization’s standard.\nSample Script\n“Zoom also lists the Calendar and Contact Integration as a possible cause. If this integration is managed by your organization, please do not remove it. You’ll need to contact your IT team or Zoom administrator so they can review or reconfigure it according to your company’s standard.”\nStep 6 — Combined Troubleshooting Method\nIf the error continues and the arbitrator is authorized to manage the applicable settings, Zoom recommends combining the uninstall and integration steps.\nUninstall the Zoom app.\nDelete the existing Calendar and Contact Integration setting, if applicable and authorized.\nRestart the Mac.\nReinstall Zoom from the official Zoom Download Center.\nReconfigure the Calendar and Contact Integration according to the arbitrator’s company standard.\nOpen Zoom and test again.\nSample Script\n“If you are authorized to manage the calendar and contact integration, Zoom recommends combining the clean uninstall with reconfiguring that integration, followed by a restart and reinstall. If the integration is company-managed, please contact your IT or Zoom administrator instead.”\nStep 7 — Check macOS Updates and Restart\nIf the issue continues, check for available macOS system updates. Install any approved update, restart the Mac, and test Zoom again.\nSample Script\n“The next basic check is to make sure your Mac is up to date. Please check for any available macOS system updates that you are authorized to install, restart the Mac, and then test Zoom again.”\nStep 8 — Confirm the Result\nOpen Zoom after the troubleshooting step.\nConfirm whether the error still appears.\nIf Zoom opens and connects normally, the troubleshooting is complete.\nIf the error continues after the approved steps, stop troubleshooting and follow the referral guidance below.\nSample Script\n“Please open Zoom again and confirm whether the “Unable to establish secure connection to Zoom” message still appears.”\nWhen to Stop and Refer for Additional Assistance\nBecause the support team provides basic Zoom assistance and cannot contact administrators on the arbitrator’s behalf, agents should stop troubleshooting when the next action requires access or authority outside the approved support scope.\nThe Mac or Zoom installation is managed by the arbitrator’s organization and the arbitrator cannot uninstall, reinstall, or update applications.\nCalendar and Contact Integration is company-managed or the arbitrator is not authorized to delete/reconfigure it.\nThe arbitrator needs administrator credentials or permissions that they do not have.\nmacOS updates or security settings are controlled by the organization.\nThe error continues after the approved basic troubleshooting steps have been completed.\nThe issue appears to involve the organization’s network, security controls, account configuration, or another system outside basic Zoom support.\nWho the Arbitrator Should Contact\nSample Script\n“We’ve completed the basic troubleshooting steps available to us, but the secure connection error is still occurring. The next step requires additional system or account-level assistance. Please contact your organization’s IT team or Zoom administrator for further support.”\nQuick Guide / Remember the Process\nAgent Reminders\nUse one instruction at a time and confirm the result before moving forward.\nDo not remove an organization-managed integration without authorization.\nDo not ask the arbitrator to bypass administrator, security, or device-management restrictions.\nDo not promise that reinstalling Zoom will resolve the error.\nIf the error persists after approved troubleshooting, explain the support boundary and direct the arbitrator to their own IT or Zoom administrator.\nDo not contact the arbitrator’s administrator or IT team on their behalf unless a separate client-approved process explicitly authorizes it.\nReference\nPrimary source: Zoom Support — Zoom error message “Unable to establish secure connection to Zoom.” Related Zoom Support resources referenced by the source include uninstalling/reinstalling Zoom, Calendar and Contact Integration, updating Zoom, checking the installed Zoom version, and Zoom release notes.","images":["assets/rendered/zoom-error-unable-to-establish-secure-connection-to-zoom-page-1.png","assets/rendered/zoom-error-unable-to-establish-secure-connection-to-zoom-page-2.png","assets/rendered/zoom-error-unable-to-establish-secure-connection-to-zoom-page-3.png","assets/rendered/zoom-error-unable-to-establish-secure-connection-to-zoom-page-4.png","assets/rendered/zoom-error-unable-to-establish-secure-connection-to-zoom-page-5.png","assets/rendered/zoom-error-unable-to-establish-secure-connection-to-zoom-page-6.png"],"visualReferences":[{"title":"Confirm the exact secure-connection error","image":"assets/visual-references/secure-connection-error.png","documentStep":"Step 1 — Confirm the Error and Platform","instruction":"Match the message “Unable to establish secure connection to Zoom.” Do not assume a different connection error uses the same troubleshooting path.","expectedResult":"The agent confirms the exact error before updating, uninstalling, reinstalling, or referring according to the process document.","platform":"macOS — Zoom secure connection error example","sourceName":"Zoom Community","sourceUrl":"https://community.zoom.com/meetings-2/error-unable-to-establish-secure-connection-to-zoom-error-code-205digicert-26586","status":"Real Zoom error screen matched to process document"}]},{"id":"zoom-meeting-controls-icons","title":"Zoom Meeting Controls & Icons","category":"controls","purpose":"To provide agents with a consistent, simple process for explaining the main Zoom meeting controls to an arbitrator. The goal is to help the arbitrator locate and use essential controls without overwhelming them or changing unrelated meeting settings.","keywords":"Zoom Meeting Controls & Icons controls","steps":["“Click Participants to open the list of people in the meeting. This lets you see who is currently connected.”","“Click Chat if you need to open the meeting messages. Before sending anything, please confirm who the message will be sent to.”","“Click React if you need to use a reaction or raise your hand. If the option you need is not displayed, we can also check More.”","“The red Leave button exits you from the meeting. Please click it only when you are ready to leave the session or when instructed to do so.”"],"referral":"Refer when the issue requires host, account, admin, network, operating-system, or device access beyond basic Zoom guidance.","text":"Process Title: Helping an Arbitrator Understand Zoom Meeting Controls and Icons\nPurpose: To provide agents with a consistent, simple process for explaining the main Zoom meeting controls to an arbitrator. The goal is to help the arbitrator locate and use essential controls without overwhelming them or changing unrelated meeting settings.\nApplies To: Agents assisting arbitrators who are participating in a Zoom meeting using the Zoom Workplace meeting interface. Control placement and availability may vary depending on device, app version, meeting role, host permissions, and account settings.\nWhen To Use: Use this process when an arbitrator is unfamiliar with the Zoom interface, cannot locate a meeting control, needs help using a control during a proceeding, or asks what an icon means.\nIntroduction\nThe Zoom Meeting Controls & Icons allow participants to manage how they communicate and interact during a Zoom meeting. Understanding these controls is especially important for arbitrators so they can confidently navigate the meeting without unnecessary interruptions or delays.\nCommon controls include Mute/Unmute, Start/Stop Video, Participants, Chat, Share Screen, Reactions, More, and Leave. Depending on the device, Zoom version, meeting settings, and participant permissions, the location or availability of some controls may vary.\nAgents should help arbitrators understand where each control is located, what it does, and how to use it appropriately. When providing assistance, agents should follow the Locate → Describe → Guide → Confirm approach and provide one instruction at a time to ensure the arbitrator can successfully complete the intended action.\nProcess / Step-by-Step Guide\nOrient the Arbitrator to the Meeting Controls Toolbar\nExplain that the main meeting controls are located along the bottom of the Zoom meeting window. If the toolbar is hidden, ask the arbitrator to move the mouse over the meeting window. On mobile devices, tapping the screen generally reveals the meeting controls.\nSample Script\n\t“At the bottom of your Zoom meeting window, you should see the meeting controls. If you don’t see them, move your mouse over the Zoom window. We’ll go through the controls one at a time.”\nMute / Unmute\nThe microphone icon controls whether the arbitrator’s microphone is on or muted. The small arrow beside the microphone opens additional audio options, including available microphone/speaker selections and audio settings.\nSample Script\n\t“Look at the bottom-left of the Zoom window for the microphone icon. Click Mute if you need to turn your microphone off, or Unmute when you need to speak. If we need to change your microphone or audio device, click the small arrow beside it.”\nStop Video / Start Video\nThe camera icon turns the arbitrator’s video on or off. The small arrow beside it provides additional camera/video options when available.\nSample Script\n\t“Next to the microphone, you should see a camera icon. Click it to start or stop your video. If we need to select a different camera or check video settings, click the small arrow beside the camera.”\nParticipants\nParticipants opens the participant list so the arbitrator can see who is currently in the meeting and access participant-related options that are available to their role.\nSample Script\n\t“Click Participants to open the list of people in the meeting. This lets you see who is currently connected.”\nChat\nChat opens in-meeting messaging when chat is enabled by the meeting settings. Available recipients and chat functions may be restricted by the host or organization.\nSample Script\n\t“Click Chat if you need to open the meeting messages. Before sending anything, please confirm who the message will be sent to.”\nShare\nShare allows permitted participants to present a screen, application, document, whiteboard, or other supported content. Before sharing, the arbitrator should verify that only the intended information will be visible.\nSample Script\n\t“Click Share if you have been asked and are permitted to present something. Select only the screen or window you intend to show, and check that no confidential information is visible before you begin sharing.”\nZoom AI\nThe Zoom AI control provides access to AI Companion features that may include meeting questions or summaries. Availability depends on the organization’s Zoom plan, administrator settings, host settings, and meeting configuration.\nSample Script\n\t“If you see Zoom AI, it means AI features may be available for this meeting. Please use only the features approved for the proceeding and your organization.”\nReact\nReact provides meeting reactions and may provide access to Raise Hand or other feedback controls, depending on the interface and meeting configuration.\nSample Script\n\t“Click React if you need to use a reaction or raise your hand. If the option you need is not displayed, we can also check More.”\nMore\nMore (three dots) opens additional meeting controls. Based on the attached guide, examples can include Raise Hand/Reactions, Participants, Record, Show Captions, Transcript, Apps, Transfer to Room, Meeting Info, and Settings. The exact list varies by role, platform, permissions, and enabled features.\nSample Script\n\t“Click More, shown as three dots, to see additional meeting options. Tell me what you are trying to do, and I’ll help you choose the correct option without changing anything else.”\nLeave\nLeave exits the arbitrator from the meeting. A participant should use Leave only when they intend to exit. Host users may have additional end-meeting options.\nSample Script\n\t“The red Leave button exits you from the meeting. Please click it only when you are ready to leave the session or when instructed to do so.”\nConfirm Understanding\nBefore ending assistance, ask the arbitrator to identify the controls they are most likely to need: Mute/Unmute, Start/Stop Video, Participants, Chat, Share, React/Raise Hand, More, and Leave.\nSample Script\n\t\t“Before we finish, please confirm that you can locate your microphone, camera, Participants, Chat, Share, reactions or Raise Hand, More, and Leave controls.”\nQuick Guide / Remember the Process\nAgent Guidance Standard\nUse Locate -> Describe -> Guide -> Confirm.\nGive one instruction at a time.\nUse plain language and describe both the icon and where it appears.\nConfirm the arbitrator’s device/platform before giving device-specific directions.\nDo not assume every control shown in the reference image will appear for every arbitrator.\nDo not advise an arbitrator to record, use AI, share confidential content, or change meeting settings unless permitted by the organization/proceeding.\nRemain neutral and procedural; Zoom support should not become legal or case-related advice.","images":["assets/rendered/zoom-meeting-controls-icons-page-1.png","assets/rendered/zoom-meeting-controls-icons-page-2.png","assets/rendered/zoom-meeting-controls-icons-page-3.png","assets/rendered/zoom-meeting-controls-icons-page-4.png","assets/rendered/zoom-meeting-controls-icons-page-5.png"],"visualReferences":[{"title":"Locate the main in-meeting controls","image":"assets/visual-references/meeting-controls.png","documentStep":"Zoom Meeting Controls and Icons — Main participant controls","instruction":"Use the bottom meeting toolbar to locate Audio, Video, Participants, Chat, Reactions, Share, Apps, More, and Leave or End.","expectedResult":"The customer can identify the requested control before receiving the next instruction.","platform":"Desktop — current Zoom Workplace meeting interface shown","sourceName":"Zoom Education","sourceUrl":"https://zoom.us/fr/pricing/education","status":"Official Zoom visual matched to process document"}]}];
